{"name":"Pattanam Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"North Paravur","state":"Kerala","about":"Pattanam is an important archaeological site on Kerala’s Malabar Coast, offering evidence of early historic settlement, material culture and maritime connections across the Indian Ocean.","full":"Pattanam is an important archaeological site on Kerala’s Malabar Coast, offering evidence of early historic settlement, material culture and maritime connections across the Indian Ocean. Excavations have contributed to research on trade, craft production and coastal communities. Its possible identification with the ancient port of Muziris remains a subject of scholarly discussion.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-001","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-001","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/muziris/historical-sites/pattanam","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":10.15638,"lng":76.20886,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kottappuram Fort (also styled Kottapuram Fort)","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Located near Kodungallur, Kottappuram Fort reflects Kerala’s early modern coastal history and the strategic importance of maritime trade routes.","full":"Located near Kodungallur, Kottappuram Fort reflects Kerala’s early modern coastal history and the strategic importance of maritime trade routes. Its surviving heritage provides a starting point for exploring European colonial expansion, coastal defence and changing regional power relations. The fort’s historical context should be distinguished from debates about the ancient port of Muziris.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-002","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-002","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/muziris/historical-sites/kottappuram-fort","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pallippuram Fort (Ayakotta / Alikotta)","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kochi","state":"Kerala","about":"Pallippuram Fort stands as a reminder of the military and maritime history of Kerala’s coast.","full":"Pallippuram Fort stands as a reminder of the military and maritime history of Kerala’s coast. Its fortifications and construction materials offer opportunities to examine early modern defensive architecture, colonial encounters and the strategic importance of Vypin Island. Its history also illustrates how coastal structures were shaped by changing political control and regional trade.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-003","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-003","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/muziris/historical-sites/pallipuram-fort","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.98241,"lng":76.24101,"approx":false},
{"name":"Cheraman Parambu","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Cheraman Parambu is an archaeological site associated with the historical landscape of the Chera rulers and the Kodungallur region.","full":"Cheraman Parambu is an archaeological site associated with the historical landscape of the Chera rulers and the Kodungallur region. Reported archaeological materials, including pottery, metal objects and structural remains, offer avenues for investigating settlement, craft and political history. Interpretations identifying the site as a royal or administrative centre require careful archaeological contextualisation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-004","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-004","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/muziris/historical-sites/cheraman-parambu","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Jewish Cemetery near Kottayil Kovilakam (Muziris Heritage Project; also called Harmony Park)","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"This historic cemetery at Chendamangalam preserves material evidence of Kerala’s Jewish communities through funerary heritage and Hebrew-inscribed tombstones.","full":"This historic cemetery at Chendamangalam preserves material evidence of Kerala’s Jewish communities through funerary heritage and Hebrew-inscribed tombstones. Its setting within a landscape of different religious institutions highlights the region’s diverse cultural history. The inscriptions and cemetery boundaries offer valuable subjects for research into community memory, epigraphy and Kerala’s plural heritage.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-005","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-005","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/muziris/historical-sites/harmony-park","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chendamangalam Synagogue (Chennamangalam Synagogue; Kerala Jews Lifestyle Museum)","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Chendamangalam Synagogue reflects the history of Jewish life in Kerala and the architectural exchanges that shaped its built heritage.","full":"The Chendamangalam Synagogue reflects the history of Jewish life in Kerala and the architectural exchanges that shaped its built heritage. Its interior features, carved woodwork and arrangement of worship spaces provide opportunities to study religious practice, craftsmanship and cultural interaction. Its chronology should be presented with reference to reliable historical and architectural evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-006","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-006","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/muziris/museums/kerala-jews-lifestyle-museum","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Paliam Kovilakam (Paliam Palace / Dutch Palace)","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Paliam Kovilakam is a historic residence associated with the Paliath Achans, prominent political figures in the Cochin kingdom.","full":"Paliam Kovilakam is a historic residence associated with the Paliath Achans, prominent political figures in the Cochin kingdom. Its architecture and surviving documentary heritage offer insights into governance, diplomacy, domestic design and Kerala’s interactions with European powers. The building’s Kerala and Dutch influences provide a lens for studying architectural adaptation and historical exchange.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-007","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-007","src":"https://chendamangalampanchayat.lsgkerala.gov.in/en/gallery/paliam-palace","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Paliam Nalukettu Museum","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Paliam Nalukettu introduces visitors to traditional Kerala domestic architecture through a residential complex organised around a central open courtyard, or nadumuttam.","full":"Paliam Nalukettu introduces visitors to traditional Kerala domestic architecture through a residential complex organised around a central open courtyard, or nadumuttam. Its spatial arrangement provides a starting point for exploring household organisation, domestic practices and the social history of the Paliam family. The complex also illustrates the relationship between architecture and everyday life.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-008","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-008","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/muziris/museums/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thrikkur Rock-Cut Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Thrikkur Rock-Cut Temple offers an opportunity to explore Kerala’s rock-cut architectural heritage, sculptural practices and sacred spaces.","full":"The Thrikkur Rock-Cut Temple offers an opportunity to explore Kerala’s rock-cut architectural heritage, sculptural practices and sacred spaces. Carving a place of worship into natural rock demonstrates the relationship between landscape, material and religious architecture. Claims about the monument’s precise date and earlier religious associations should be distinguished from interpretations that remain under investigation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-009","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-009","src":"https://www.archaeology.kerala.gov.in/monuments/thrikkur-rock-cut-temple/74","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ariyannoor Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Ariyannoor Temple is a heritage site in Thrissur district known for its temple architecture and carved wooden entrance gable.","full":"Ariyannoor Temple is a heritage site in Thrissur district known for its temple architecture and carved wooden entrance gable. Its architectural details provide a starting point for examining regional craftsmanship, sacred design and continuing religious practice. The temple should be documented separately from the nearby Ariyannur Umbrella Stones, a distinct megalithic monument.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-010","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-010","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/ariyannur-thrissur/78","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Menhirs at Kuttoor","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Kuttoor","state":"Kerala","about":"The menhirs at Kuttoor are standing-stone monuments associated with Kerala’s megalithic archaeological landscape.","full":"The menhirs at Kuttoor are standing-stone monuments associated with Kerala’s megalithic archaeological landscape. They offer an opportunity to investigate prehistoric and early historic stone-setting practices, monument construction and the ways communities shaped their surroundings. Their exact chronology and original purpose require site-specific archaeological evidence rather than assumptions based on comparable monuments.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-011","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-011","src":"https://www.archaeology.kerala.gov.in/monuments/thrikkur-rock-cut-temple/74","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":9.35879,"lng":76.5881,"approx":true},
{"name":"Menhir at Ramavarmapuram","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Ramavarmapuram Menhir is a prominent granite monument in Thrissur’s archaeological landscape, also known locally by names including Padakkallu or Pulachikkallu.","full":"The Ramavarmapuram Menhir is a prominent granite monument in Thrissur’s archaeological landscape, also known locally by names including Padakkallu or Pulachikkallu. Its scale and surviving form make it relevant to the study of megalithic monuments and commemorative landscapes. Its original function and precise date should be established through reliable archaeological documentation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-012","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-012","src":"https://www.archaeology.kerala.gov.in/monuments/thrikkur-rock-cut-temple/74","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Hill Palace Archaeological Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Hill Palace Archaeological Museum in Thrippunithura brings together heritage associated with Kerala’s archaeology, royal history and material culture.","full":"Hill Palace Archaeological Museum in Thrippunithura brings together heritage associated with Kerala’s archaeology, royal history and material culture. Its reported collections include sculptures, coins, inscriptions, manuscripts and objects connected with traditional life. The museum provides a way to explore how material evidence helps researchers reconstruct historical institutions, artistic practices and regional knowledge traditions.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-013","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-013","src":"https://www.archaeology.kerala.gov.in/pages/hill-palace-archaeological-museum-thrippunithura/49","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Napier Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Napier Museum in Thiruvananthapuram is known for collections representing South Indian art, craft and material culture.","full":"Napier Museum in Thiruvananthapuram is known for collections representing South Indian art, craft and material culture. Sculptures, traditional musical instruments, textiles, lamps and carved objects offer opportunities to explore artistic techniques, cultural exchange and historical craftsmanship. The museum building itself is notable for its architectural character and its adaptation to the local climate.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-014","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-014","src":"https://museumandzoo.kerala.gov.in/eng/index.php/institution/thiruvananthapuram/napier-museum","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":8.50893,"lng":76.95514,"approx":false},
{"name":"State Museum and Zoo, Thrissur","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The historic museum campus at Chembukkavu, Thrissur, provides a starting point for exploring archaeological collections, regional art and material culture.","full":"The historic museum campus at Chembukkavu, Thrissur, provides a starting point for exploring archaeological collections, regional art and material culture. Its museum and art-gallery collections may illuminate local craftsmanship, historical objects and artistic traditions. This record concerns the museum campus, not the relocated zoological park; current museum access and collection details require confirmation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-015","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-015","src":"https://www.museumandzoo.kerala.gov.in/eng/index.php/thrissur","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kerala State Archives Department","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Kerala State Archives preserves documentary evidence of the region’s political, administrative, social and cultural history.","full":"The Kerala State Archives preserves documentary evidence of the region’s political, administrative, social and cultural history. Its holdings include historical records and palm-leaf manuscripts written in scripts such as Vattezhuthu, Kolezhuthu, Malayanma, Tamil and Malayalam. These materials provide pathways into the study of language, governance, land relations and knowledge transmission across generations.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-016","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-016","src":"https://www.keralaarchives.org/en/record-holding","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Keralam Museum of History and Heritage","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"The Keralam Museum of History and Heritage presents aspects of Kerala’s past through archaeological objects, manuscripts, sculpture, paintings and other cultural materials.","full":"The Keralam Museum of History and Heritage presents aspects of Kerala’s past through archaeological objects, manuscripts, sculpture, paintings and other cultural materials. Its collections provide opportunities to investigate the region’s artistic traditions, historical communities and connections with wider trade networks. Detailed interpretations should be grounded in collection records and the provenance of individual objects.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-017","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-017","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/keralam-museum-history-thiruvananthapuram/540/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":8.50818,"lng":76.95379,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kathakali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kathakali is a classical performance tradition of Kerala that combines expressive acting, hand gestures, facial movements, elaborate makeup, costume, percussion and vocal music.","full":"Kathakali is a classical performance tradition of Kerala that combines expressive acting, hand gestures, facial movements, elaborate makeup, costume, percussion and vocal music. Its stories draw on literary and epic traditions, while performers develop their skills through sustained training under experienced teachers. Kathakali preserves a rich body of embodied knowledge, performance technique and artistic interpretation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-018","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-018","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/kathakali-kerala-theatre/15","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kalaripayattu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kalaripayattu is a martial art tradition associated with Kerala that brings together physical conditioning, coordinated movement, unarmed techniques and training with weapons.","full":"Kalaripayattu is a martial art tradition associated with Kerala that brings together physical conditioning, coordinated movement, unarmed techniques and training with weapons. Teaching methods and practices vary across lineages, with some incorporating massage and knowledge of marma, or vital points. The tradition offers a lens into embodied learning, discipline, physical culture and intergenerational knowledge transmission.","branch":"health","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-019","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-019","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kalaripayattu/origin/introduction","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Theyyam","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Theyyam is a ritual performance tradition practised in parts of northern Kerala, integrating dance, music, oral narratives, elaborate costumes, headgear and distinctive face and body painting.","full":"Theyyam is a ritual performance tradition practised in parts of northern Kerala, integrating dance, music, oral narratives, elaborate costumes, headgear and distinctive face and body painting. Its performances are embedded in community and shrine traditions, connecting artistic skill with ritual knowledge and collective memory. Responsible documentation should recognise the knowledge and perspectives of participating communities.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-020","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-020","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/theyyam-ritual/1/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Dholavira","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Dholavira","state":"Gujarat","about":"Dholavira, a major Harappan archaeological site in Gujarat, provides evidence of urban planning, water management and specialised craft production in the Indus Civilisation.","full":"Dholavira, a major Harappan archaeological site in Gujarat, provides evidence of urban planning, water management and specialised craft production in the Indus Civilisation. Its reservoirs, drainage systems, stone architecture and settlement layout offer important material for studying how ancient communities organised water, space and resources in an arid environment.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-021","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-021","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1645/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":23.87501,"lng":70.21796,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ajanta Caves","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Phardapur","state":"Maharashtra","about":"The Ajanta Caves in Maharashtra preserve Buddhist monastic spaces, sculpture and celebrated mural paintings created across different historical phases.","full":"The Ajanta Caves in Maharashtra preserve Buddhist monastic spaces, sculpture and celebrated mural paintings created across different historical phases. Their architecture and painted surfaces offer opportunities to study religious life, artistic patronage, narrative traditions and historical painting techniques. The caves also demonstrate how artistic knowledge was expressed through carefully planned spaces, materials and visual storytelling.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-022","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-022","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/242/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":20.55245,"lng":75.69942,"approx":false},
{"name":"Rock Shelters of Bhimbetka","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Obaidullaganj","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"The Rock Shelters of Bhimbetka in Madhya Pradesh preserve evidence of human activity across long periods, including rock paintings and traces of past lifeways.","full":"The Rock Shelters of Bhimbetka in Madhya Pradesh preserve evidence of human activity across long periods, including rock paintings and traces of past lifeways. Their visual imagery and archaeological context offer opportunities to investigate artistic expression, the use of pigments, human-environment relationships and changing cultural practices. Individual paintings require careful dating and contextual interpretation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-024","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-024","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/925/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":22.9384,"lng":77.61333,"approx":false},
{"name":"Buddhist Monuments at Sanchi","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Amkhera","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"The Buddhist monuments at Sanchi in Madhya Pradesh include stupas, gateways, monasteries and sculptural works associated with Buddhist history.","full":"The Buddhist monuments at Sanchi in Madhya Pradesh include stupas, gateways, monasteries and sculptural works associated with Buddhist history. Their carved narratives, inscriptions and architectural features illuminate religious patronage, stoneworking, pilgrimage and visual storytelling. The site provides an important setting for studying how Buddhist ideas and narratives were communicated through architecture and art.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-025","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-025","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/524/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":23.47937,"lng":77.73975,"approx":false},
{"name":"Group of Monuments at Hampi","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Kamalapura","state":"Karnataka","about":"Hampi in Karnataka preserves the remains of the Vijayanagara capital, including temples, royal structures, market streets and water systems.","full":"Hampi in Karnataka preserves the remains of the Vijayanagara capital, including temples, royal structures, market streets and water systems. Its urban landscape illustrates relationships between architecture, hydraulic engineering, craft production, trade and ritual life. The monuments offer a way to examine how a major historical city organised its built environment and connected civic, economic and religious activities.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-027","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-027","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/241/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":15.32941,"lng":76.47554,"approx":false},
{"name":"Group of Monuments at Mahabalipuram","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Chennai Metropolitan Area","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Mahabalipuram, on Tamil Nadu’s Coromandel Coast, is known for rock-cut caves, monolithic monuments and early structural temples associated with Pallava-period architecture.","full":"Mahabalipuram, on Tamil Nadu’s Coromandel Coast, is known for rock-cut caves, monolithic monuments and early structural temples associated with Pallava-period architecture. Its sculptural panels and carved structures reveal skilled stoneworking and distinctive religious imagery. The site provides opportunities to study the development of temple forms, artistic patronage and the region’s coastal historical setting.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-028","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-028","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/249/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":12.6196,"lng":80.19365,"approx":true},
{"name":"Group of Monuments at Pattadakal","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Pattadakallu","state":"Karnataka","about":"Pattadakal in Karnataka preserves a group of temple monuments associated with the Early Chalukya period.","full":"Pattadakal in Karnataka preserves a group of temple monuments associated with the Early Chalukya period. Its buildings demonstrate interactions between northern Nagara and southern Dravida architectural forms, alongside sophisticated stoneworking and sculptural traditions. Inscriptions and architectural details offer opportunities to study religious patronage, temple design and the movement of artistic ideas across regions.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-032","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-032","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/239/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":15.94843,"lng":75.81597,"approx":true},
{"name":"Rani-ki-Vav (Queen's Stepwell)","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Patan","state":"Gujarat","about":"Rani-ki-Vav, the Queen’s Stepwell in Gujarat, combines water architecture with an elaborate sequence of underground levels, pillars and sculptural decoration.","full":"Rani-ki-Vav, the Queen’s Stepwell in Gujarat, combines water architecture with an elaborate sequence of underground levels, pillars and sculptural decoration. Its design illustrates how access to water could be integrated with architectural planning and artistic expression. The stepwell offers opportunities to examine construction, water management and the cultural symbolism associated with water.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-033","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-033","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/922/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":23.85893,"lng":72.10173,"approx":false},
{"name":"Moidams – the Mound-Burial System of the Ahom Dynasty","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Assam","about":"The Moidams of Assam are monumental burial structures associated with the Ahom royal tradition.","full":"The Moidams of Assam are monumental burial structures associated with the Ahom royal tradition. Their earthen mounds, burial chambers and landscape setting provide insights into funerary architecture, social organisation and beliefs surrounding death and ancestry. They also connect archaeological heritage with Ahom historical traditions, including chronicles and continuing community practices.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-035","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-035","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1711/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"National Museum, New Delhi","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"New Delhi","state":"Delhi","about":"The National Museum in New Delhi holds collections spanning archaeology, manuscripts, sculpture, painting, coins, inscriptions and decorative arts.","full":"The National Museum in New Delhi holds collections spanning archaeology, manuscripts, sculpture, painting, coins, inscriptions and decorative arts. These materials offer opportunities to study historical technologies, artistic practices, writing traditions, trade and cultural exchange across regions and periods. Individual objects are most informative when presented with reliable catalogue details, provenance and historically grounded interpretation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-036","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-036","src":"https://www.nationalmuseumindia.gov.in/en/collections/index/11","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":28.61192,"lng":77.21964,"approx":false},
{"name":"Indian Museum, Kolkata","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Kolkata","state":"West Bengal","about":"The Indian Museum in Kolkata brings together archaeological objects, Buddhist art, coins, sculpture, textiles and other material collections.","full":"The Indian Museum in Kolkata brings together archaeological objects, Buddhist art, coins, sculpture, textiles and other material collections. Its holdings provide opportunities to investigate historical craft techniques, artistic traditions, economic history and cultural practices. The museum can support deeper discovery through individual object records that document provenance, dating, interpretation and the rights associated with collection images.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-037","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-037","src":"https://indianmuseumkolkata.org/gallery/about-gallery/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":22.55759,"lng":88.35103,"approx":false},
{"name":"Salar Jung Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Hyderabad","state":"Telangana","about":"Salar Jung Museum in Hyderabad houses a wide-ranging collection of manuscripts, Indian art, textiles, metalwork, paintings, woodcarving and sculpture.","full":"Salar Jung Museum in Hyderabad houses a wide-ranging collection of manuscripts, Indian art, textiles, metalwork, paintings, woodcarving and sculpture. Its materials offer pathways into the study of literature, philosophy, scientific writing, regional craftsmanship and Deccan history. Because the museum also contains international collections, records should distinguish Indian knowledge traditions from broader global collecting history.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-038","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-038","src":"https://www.salarjungmuseum.in/collections.html","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":17.37139,"lng":78.48015,"approx":false},
{"name":"Allahabad Museum, Prayagraj","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Prayagraj","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"Allahabad Museum in Prayagraj preserves archaeological objects, sculpture, coins, manuscripts, paintings, textiles and decorative arts.","full":"Allahabad Museum in Prayagraj preserves archaeological objects, sculpture, coins, manuscripts, paintings, textiles and decorative arts. Its collections provide evidence for studying historical artistic traditions, material technologies, writing, administration and regional cultural exchange. Each object offers a more reliable research starting point when its period, provenance and interpretation are documented rather than inferred from broad collection labels.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-039","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-039","src":"https://theallahabadmuseum.com/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":25.45679,"lng":81.84915,"approx":false},
{"name":"Victoria Memorial Hall","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Kolkata","state":"West Bengal","about":"Victoria Memorial Hall in Kolkata holds art, manuscripts, books, photographs and archival materials related to South Asian history, including Bengal’s artistic traditions and colonial-era encounters.","full":"Victoria Memorial Hall in Kolkata holds art, manuscripts, books, photographs and archival materials related to South Asian history, including Bengal’s artistic traditions and colonial-era encounters. Its collections offer opportunities to study visual culture, historical documentation and the production of knowledge under colonial rule. Interpretations should consider the historical context and collecting practices behind the materials.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-040","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-040","src":"https://www.museumsofindia.gov.in/repository/museum/vmh_kol","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":22.54508,"lng":88.34264,"approx":false},
{"name":"National Mission on Monuments and Antiquities (NMMA) — Antiquities Database","kind":"archive","host":"Digital archives","city":"","state":null,"about":"The National Mission on Monuments and Antiquities Antiquities Database is a digital resource for discovering documentation of historical objects, including sculpture, coins, paintings and other antiquities.","full":"The National Mission on Monuments and Antiquities Antiquities Database is a digital resource for discovering documentation of historical objects, including sculpture, coins, paintings and other antiquities. It can help researchers identify material for further study across regions and periods. Individual entries should be cross-checked for provenance, dating, interpretation and image-reuse conditions before being used as definitive evidence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-DIG-045","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-DIG-045","src":"https://nmma.nic.in/","group":"Digital archives"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"NMMA Built Heritage Database","kind":"archive","host":"Digital archives","city":"","state":null,"about":"The NMMA Built Heritage Database provides a searchable starting point for exploring documented heritage sites and structures across India.","full":"The NMMA Built Heritage Database provides a searchable starting point for exploring documented heritage sites and structures across India. Its records may help identify temples, forts, houses, water structures and other examples of built heritage. Because database entries can vary in completeness, each site’s identity, location, condition and specific relevance to knowledge traditions requires independent checking.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-DIG-046","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-DIG-046","src":"https://nmma.nic.in/nmma/exploreBuilt.do","group":"Digital archives"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"BharatSHRI — Bharat Shared Repository of Inscriptions","kind":"archive","host":"Digital archives","city":"","state":null,"about":"BharatSHRI is an Archaeological Survey of India initiative to improve digital access to inscriptional records, including metadata and digitised estampages.","full":"BharatSHRI is an Archaeological Survey of India initiative to improve digital access to inscriptional records, including metadata and digitised estampages. Inscriptions can provide evidence about historical languages, scripts, grants, administration, religious institutions, trade and community life. The resource is still developing, so individual records must be checked for available images, transcripts, readings and scholarly context.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-DIG-047","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-DIG-047","src":"https://bharatshri.asi.gov.in/FrontEndSearch?lang=en","group":"Digital archives"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Āryabhaṭīya (Aryabhatiya) — mathematical astronomy treatise by Āryabhaṭa","kind":"text","host":"Classical texts","city":"","state":null,"about":"The Āryabhaṭīya is a Sanskrit treatise on mathematical astronomy traditionally attributed to Āryabhaṭa and conventionally dated to 499 CE.","full":"The Āryabhaṭīya is a Sanskrit treatise on mathematical astronomy traditionally attributed to Āryabhaṭa and conventionally dated to 499 CE. Organised into four sections, it addresses mathematical procedures, time reckoning, astronomical calculations and celestial models. It is an important source for studying the history of Indian mathematics and astronomy, technical Sanskrit writing and later scholarly commentary.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TXT-048","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TXT-048","src":"https://sa.wikisource.org/wiki/ARYABHATIYA_OF_ARYABHATA","group":"Classical texts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pāṇini’s Aṣṭādhyāyī — Sanskrit grammatical treatise","kind":"text","host":"Classical texts","city":"","state":null,"about":"Pāṇini’s Aṣṭādhyāyī is a foundational Sanskrit grammatical treatise organised into eight chapters and approximately 4,000 concise rules, or sūtras.","full":"Pāṇini’s Aṣṭādhyāyī is a foundational Sanskrit grammatical treatise organised into eight chapters and approximately 4,000 concise rules, or sūtras. Its technical vocabulary, conventions and rule structures describe aspects of Sanskrit word formation and grammatical organisation. The work is central to the history of linguistics and formal grammatical analysis, with its interpretation shaped by a substantial commentarial tradition.","branch":"language","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TXT-049","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TXT-049","src":"https://sanskritdocuments.org/learning_tools/ashtadhyayi/","group":"Classical texts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Śulbasūtras (Sulbasutras) — Vedic-era ritual geometry text tradition","kind":"text","host":"Classical texts","city":"","state":null,"about":"The Śulbasūtras are a group of Sanskrit texts associated with Vedic ritual traditions that describe geometric procedures used in measuring and constructing fire altars.","full":"The Śulbasūtras are a group of Sanskrit texts associated with Vedic ritual traditions that describe geometric procedures used in measuring and constructing fire altars. Texts attributed to authors such as Baudhāyana and Āpastamba preserve practical methods involving cords, shapes, diagonals and measurement. They offer valuable material for studying the relationship between geometry, ritual practice and early mathematical knowledge.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TXT-050","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TXT-050","src":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-40846-5_114","group":"Classical texts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Edakkal Rock Engravings","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Ambalavayal","state":"Kerala","about":"Edakkal preserves rock engravings within natural formations in Wayanad's Ambukuthi Hills. Human figures, animals and other symbols provide material for studying early visual expression and the archaeological history of the Western Ghats.","full":"The natural rock formations at Edakkal contain engravings of human and animal figures alongside a range of symbols, documented by Kerala Tourism in its account of the Wayanad site. The marks offer material evidence for examining image making, symbolic communication and the relationship between people and rocky landscapes. Their precise chronology and interpretation require archaeological analysis; a tourism account alone cannot establish the meaning or cultural affiliation of every engraving.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-051","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-051","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/destination_detailed_view/edakkal-caves/195","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":11.62666,"lng":76.22811,"approx":true},
{"name":"Marayoor Muniyara Dolmens","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Muniyaras of Marayoor are stone burial chambers formed from upright slabs and capstones. Their surviving construction offers a starting point for studying megalithic building methods, funerary landscapes and regional archaeological traditions in Idukki.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Marayoor's Muniyaras as dolmenoid burial chambers assembled from large stone slabs with a capstone above. Some examples contain a single chamber and others multiple chambers, making their arrangement relevant to questions about construction and funerary practice. The regional concentration should be understood as an archaeological landscape rather than one uniformly dated monument, and details of burial use need comparison with excavation records and specialist studies.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-052","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-052","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/muniyara-idukki-explore-megalithic-relics-at-marayoor/699/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Krishnapuram Palace Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Kayamkulam","state":"Kerala","about":"Krishnapuram Palace at Kayamkulam houses a museum of sculptures, paintings and bronzes. Its Gajendramoksham mural offers a focused example of Kerala's narrative painting heritage within a historic palace setting associated with Travancore patronage.","full":"The Alappuzha district administration identifies Krishnapuram Palace as a palace built under Marthanda Varma at Kayamkulam and highlights its mural of Gajendramoksham. The museum also houses antique sculptures, paintings and bronzes, bringing several forms of material and visual culture into one setting. Together, the mural and collections provide a basis for studying religious storytelling, artistic materials and courtly collecting, while the history and provenance of individual objects require catalogue-level research.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-053","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-053","src":"https://alappuzha.nic.in/en/tourist-place/krishnapuram-palace/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":9.15028,"lng":76.50877,"approx":false},
{"name":"Mattancherry Palace and Murals","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kochi","state":"Kerala","about":"Mattancherry Palace preserves Kerala mural paintings within a building shaped by Portuguese, Dutch and Kochi royal histories. Narrative scenes from the Ramayana and other religious literature connect the palace with regional painting and visual storytelling.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Mattancherry Palace as a building constructed by the Portuguese, renovated by the Dutch and used by the rulers of Kochi for royal ceremonies. Its four wings open toward a central courtyard, while carved wooden ceilings and narrative murals form part of its surviving artistic programme. Ramayana scenes, Krishna narratives and Shaiva subjects connect the palace with regional painting and religious storytelling, while the building also records architectural exchange and courtly patronage.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-054","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-054","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/mattancherry-palace-kochi/178/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.95835,"lng":76.25918,"approx":false},
{"name":"Aranmula Kannadi Metal-Mirror Craft","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Kozhencherry","state":"Kerala","about":"Aranmula Kannadi is a handmade metal-mirror tradition associated with specialist craft families in Aranmula. Casting and prolonged polishing produce a reflective metal surface, linking practical metallurgical skill with ritual objects and intergenerational craft transmission.","full":"Kerala's Industries Department describes Aranmula Kannadi as a metal mirror made in Aranmula and explains that repeated polishing gives the cast plate its reflective surface. The page also records the role of specialist families in maintaining knowledge of the alloy and its working, and notes the mirror's place in auspicious ceremonial arrangements. This makes the craft relevant to the history of metallurgy and embodied workshop learning, without assuming that a published popular account reveals the complete production method.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-055","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-055","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/aranmula-kannadi","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":9.32681,"lng":76.6857,"approx":true},
{"name":"Balaramapuram Handloom Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Balaramapuram is a handloom centre associated with fine cotton fabrics and kasavu borders. Its weaving tradition links loom operation, yarn preparation and textile design with community livelihoods and the history of Travancore patronage.","full":"Kerala Tourism's account of Balaramapuram describes cream-coloured cotton textiles with zari borders and the use of traditional throw-shuttle pit looms. It connects the local weaving community with migration from Tamil Nadu and patronage by the Travancore royal household. The record provides a starting point for studying how workshop skills, loom mechanisms and design choices circulate between generations, while contemporary products and materials should be checked with the weavers themselves.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-056","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-056","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2024/balaramapuram-tradition-of-handloom-sarees-and-cotton-fabrics/1456","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kuthiramalika Palace Museum","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kuthiramalika, also called Puthenmalika, combines a historic Travancore palace with museum collections. Its carved wooden horses, timber structure and sculptural displays connect regional building craft with royal patronage and the material culture of the court.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Kuthiramalika as a palace commissioned by Swathi Thirunal and explains that its name refers to the horses carved on roof-supporting beams. The building uses teak, rosewood, marble and granite, and houses sculptures, paintings, mirrors and Kathakali figures. Its materials and carved elements offer concrete subjects for studying palace craftsmanship and the presentation of Travancore's courtly culture, though individual objects require separate provenance and dating checks.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-057","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-057","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/campaigns/kerala365/kuthiramalika-thiruvananthapuram","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Vaidyaratnam Ayurveda Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Vaidyaratnam Ayurveda Museum interprets the history of Ayurveda through texts, dioramas and multimedia displays. Its library and manuscript presentations connect Kerala's medical heritage with the preservation and public explanation of historical healthcare knowledge.","full":"Located in the Thaikkattussery ancestral-house setting near Ollur, the museum presents accounts of Ayurveda through sculpture, portraits, audiovisual interpretation and a three-dimensional gallery. Kerala Tourism describes a library of texts used by Ashtavaidyas and a digital library of palm-leaf manuscripts, alongside displays about medicine manufacture and treatment practices. These resources are relevant to the history of medical learning and its transmission, while the museum's historical and religious narratives need to be distinguished from contemporary evidence about treatment efficacy.","branch":"health","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-058","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-058","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/vaidyaratnam-ayurveda-museum/586/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kutiyattam Sanskrit Theatre","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kutiyattam combines Sanskrit drama with Kerala performance traditions through codified gesture, eye expression and extended dramatic elaboration. Its rigorous training and performance manuals preserve a distinctive body of theatrical knowledge transmitted by specialist teachers and performers.","full":"UNESCO describes Kutiyattam as a theatre tradition combining Sanskrit classicism and Kerala practices, with eye expression and hand gestures central to its performance language. Actors develop detailed control of breathing, facial muscles and bodily movement through long training, and performance manuals help transmit the repertoire. Traditionally associated with temple theatres called Kuttampalams, the form also illustrates changing access and patronage, making it relevant to both embodied knowledge and the social institutions that sustain theatrical learning.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-060","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-060","src":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/kutiyattam-sanskrit-theatre-00010","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mudiyettu Ritual Theatre","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mudiyettu is a Kerala ritual dance drama recounting the battle of Kali and Darika. Collective preparation, coloured-powder floor drawing and apprenticeship connect theatre with community participation and the transmission of ritual and aesthetic knowledge.","full":"UNESCO's account explains that Mudiyettu brings village participants together for an annual ritual performance centred on Kali's battle with Darika. Performers prepare a coloured-powder image called a kalam before the dramatic enactment, and elders and senior artists involve younger people as apprentices during performances. Its temple-grove settings along several Kerala rivers link knowledge transmission to a recurring community event, incorporating drawing, theatrical action, ritual preparation and shared responsibility for sustaining the practice.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-061","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-061","src":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/mudiyettu-ritual-theatre-and-dance-drama-of-kerala-00345?lang=en","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Bekal Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Bekal","state":"Kerala","about":"Bekal is a coastal fort with a stepped water tank, observation tower and surviving defensive structures. These features provide material evidence for examining coastal fortification, water provision and the organisation of military space in northern Kerala.","full":"Kerala Tourism's heritage account highlights Bekal's water tank with steps, ammunition magazine, observation tower and a tunnel opening toward the south. These features make the fort relevant to practical questions about water storage, surveillance, movement and the arrangement of defensive infrastructure. The structure also belongs to the changing political and maritime history of the Malabar coast, although accounts of its earliest construction and sequence of rulers require more careful comparison than the tourism summary provides.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-063","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-063","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/campaigns/kerala365/bekal-fort-kasaragod-history","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":12.39222,"lng":75.03277,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kerala Folklore Academy","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"Manimala","state":"Kerala","about":"Kerala Folklore Academy promotes traditional arts and folklore from its base in Chirakkal. Its institutional focus connects oral expression, performance, agricultural customs and public cultural programmes with the documentation and transmission of community knowledge.","full":"The Academy identifies itself as a Kerala government institution under the Cultural Affairs Department, established to promote the state's traditional art forms. Its official website connects regional folklore with agricultural practices and associated songs and performing arts, while providing sections for publications, programmes and a museum. As an institutional record it offers a route into the study and presentation of living community knowledge; individual traditions and collection objects should still be documented through their own practitioner and catalogue evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-IKS-064","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-IKS-064","src":"https://keralafolklore.org/en/","group":"Research institutions"},"lat":9.49295,"lng":76.74349,"approx":false},
{"name":"Suranga Tunnel-Well Tradition","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Karnataka","about":"Surangas are horizontal tunnels excavated into laterite hills to collect groundwater and convey it by gravity. Still associated with specialist excavators and farming households, they connect local geological knowledge with water supply and small-scale irrigation.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes surangas as tunnel wells cut into laterite hills in Kasaragod and adjoining parts of Karnataka. They intercept percolating groundwater and carry it to the surface through gravity, supplying households and agriculture without requiring pumping within the tunnel system. Their continuing excavation by skilled practitioners makes them relevant to practical knowledge of terrain, groundwater and construction, although performance depends on local conditions and the source does not establish a single historical origin for the technique.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-065","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-065","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/surangas-kasaragod-ancient-tunnel-wells-and-a-sustainable-water-system/733/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Karnataka","about":"This three-part temple ensemble documents Hoysala architecture and sculpture at Belur, Halebidu and Somanathapura. Carved narrative sequences, architectural composition and artists' inscriptions connect the monuments with stoneworking expertise, religious interpretation and the social history of craft.","full":"UNESCO identifies the Channakeshava temple at Belur, Hoysalesvara temple at Halebidu and Keshava temple at Somanathapura as a serial property representing Hoysala sacred architecture. Its account discusses sequential epic narratives on temple walls and numerous artists' signatures, some recording lineage and titles. These features provide specific evidence for studying sculptural design, religious storytelling and the recognition of craftspeople, with the three components retained here as one ensemble rather than separate additions.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-067","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-067","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/decisions/8419","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Badami Cave Temples","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Badami","state":"Karnataka","about":"Badami's principal cave temples are carved into sandstone and contain Hindu and Jain imagery. Their pillared spaces, ceiling panels and relief sculpture provide material for studying rock-cut construction, iconography and artistic work in the Chalukya region.","full":"Karnataka Tourism describes four principal cave temples at Badami, with the first three presenting Hindu imagery and the fourth associated with Jain worship. Among the documented relief subjects are Trivikrama, Varaha, Anantashayana and Mahavira, set within excavated pillared interiors and carved ceilings. The caves offer evidence for the coordination of architectural excavation and sculptural programmes, and for different religious traditions sharing a regional artistic setting; individual dates and workshop relationships require more detailed archaeological study.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-068","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-068","src":"https://karnatakatourism.org/en/destinations/badami/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":15.91837,"lng":75.68438,"approx":false},
{"name":"Aihole Temple Landscape","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Karnataka","about":"Aihole contains a varied concentration of early temple forms, including an apsidal shrine, rock-cut architecture and Jain monuments. The landscape supports comparison of architectural plans, carved imagery and inscriptions within the wider Malaprabha valley.","full":"Karnataka Tourism's Aihole account identifies the apsidal Durga Temple, Lad Khan Temple, Ravana Phadi cave and Meguti Jain Temple among a wider group of monuments. It also notes the historical importance of the inscription at Meguti. Rather than treating architectural development as a single linear invention, the ensemble can be approached as evidence for varied experiments in plans, construction and religious representation, with each monument requiring its own chronology and analysis.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-069","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-069","src":"https://karnatakatourism.org/en/destinations/aihole/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Banavasi and Madhukeshwara Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Banavasi","state":"Karnataka","about":"Banavasi's historic settlement and Madhukeshwara Temple connect the Kadamba political landscape with Kannada literary memory and regional sacred architecture. Its carvings and continuing cultural associations support exploration of relationships between patronage, language and built heritage.","full":"Karnataka Tourism presents Banavasi as a former Kadamba capital and identifies Madhukeshwara Temple as its central built monument. The account connects the town with the literary memory of Pampa and with cultural events celebrating the region's heritage. The temple and settlement provide a starting point for studying stone carving, patronage and Kannada cultural history, while literary associations and successive building phases should be evaluated through inscriptions, edited texts and architectural evidence rather than accepted from a destination summary alone.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-070","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-070","src":"https://karnatakatourism.org/en/destinations/banavasi/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":14.53897,"lng":75.01298,"approx":true},
{"name":"Shravanabelagola Jain Heritage Landscape","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Karnataka","about":"Shravanabelagola brings together the monolithic Bahubali image on Vindhyagiri, Jain basadis on Chandragiri and continuing ritual practice. Its monumental stonework and sacred landscape connect artistic production with religious memory and long-term community stewardship.","full":"Karnataka Tourism documents the monolithic Bahubali image on Vindhyagiri and the group of Jain basadis and memorials on nearby Chandragiri. It also identifies surviving rock inscriptions and the recurring Mahamastakabhisheka anointing ceremony. The relationship between sculpture, hilltop access, historical writing and ritual use provides material for studying monumental carving and Jain sacred space, while individual inscriptions and temple histories are needed to establish particular construction phases and historical associations.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-071","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-071","src":"https://karnatakatourism.org/en/attractions/shravanabelagola/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Bidar Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Bidar","state":"Karnataka","about":"Bidar Fort preserves fortifications, gateways and courtly buildings associated with the Bahmani capital. Its defensive arrangement and palace spaces provide evidence for studying Deccan construction practices and the exchange of Persianate and regional architectural ideas.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism's account of Bidar Fort describes its defensive walls, moats and bastions alongside palace structures such as Rangin Mahal, Gagan Mahal and Tarkash Mahal. Baths, kitchens, gardens and religious buildings form part of the larger fortified setting. The complex therefore supports questions about the organisation of a Deccan court, defensive planning and architectural exchange, while its several construction and repair phases should be distinguished through monument-specific research rather than assigned to one ruler or date.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-072","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-072","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/karnataka/bidar/bidar-fort","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":17.92527,"lng":77.52662,"approx":false},
{"name":"Yakshagana Theatre Tradition","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Karnataka","about":"Yakshagana combines dramatic narration, music, dance, dialogue and elaborate costume in coastal Karnataka. The Bhagavatha's storytelling and the coordinated work of performers and musicians sustain a repertoire of epic episodes through live theatrical practice.","full":"Karnataka Tourism describes Yakshagana performances as combinations of dance, song, music, dialogue and costume, commonly organised around an episode called a prasanga from the Ramayana or Mahabharata. The Bhagavatha leads narration alongside actors and instrumentalists, with chande and maddale among the documented instruments. The form offers a setting for examining oral interpretation, rhythmic knowledge and the demanding physical skills of performance, while regional styles and changing troupe practices require practitioner-specific documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-073","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-073","src":"https://karnatakatourism.org/en/experiences/yakshagana/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Channapatna Lacquered Wooden Toys","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Channapattana","state":"Karnataka","about":"Channapatna's toy-making tradition shapes lightweight wood on a lathe and applies coloured lac through friction. Polishing, turning and coordinated tool use connect its recognisable toys and household objects with specialised craft knowledge and workshop learning.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism describes Channapatna artisans shaping hale wood on a lathe, applying coloured lac sticks to the turning surface and polishing the result with palm leaves. The craft produces dolls, animals and other toys alongside boxes, bangles and household objects. These operations make the tradition relevant to wood selection, turning, surface finishing and craft adaptation, while material safety, dye composition and product authenticity need checks on individual workshops rather than blanket assumptions about all goods sold under the place name.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-074","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-074","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/karnataka/channapatna-toys-and-dolls","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":12.65937,"lng":77.20389,"approx":true},
{"name":"Janapada Loka Folk Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Karnataka","about":"Janapada Loka collects and presents Karnataka's folk culture through objects, photographs, performance displays and a library. Agricultural implements, pottery equipment and household technologies connect its collections with practical rural knowledge as well as regional performing arts.","full":"Karnataka Tourism describes Janapada Loka's displays of folk performers, its founder's documentation photographs and a library concerned with the state's folk culture. Open-air areas include a bullock cart, wooden chariot, pottery equipment and sugarcane-crushing machinery. The museum therefore offers material for examining rural tools, performance traditions and the processes by which community practices are collected and interpreted, while the age, origin and use of each displayed object must be established from its own documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-075","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-075","src":"https://karnatakatourism.org/en/attractions/janapada-loka","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Central Institute of Indian Languages","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"Mysuru","state":"Karnataka","about":"The Central Institute of Indian Languages studies and supports Indian languages from its Mysuru base. Its stated mission joins scientific language study and interdisciplinary research, providing an institutional connection to the documentation and transmission of linguistic knowledge.","full":"The Institute's official mission identifies the coordination of Indian-language development, scientific language study and interdisciplinary research as central purposes. Its Mysuru-based work places the diversity and interaction of Indian languages within a modern research setting. For an IKS atlas, this makes the institution relevant to the languages through which oral and textual traditions are expressed, while a claim about a particular endangered language, linguistic corpus or historical grammar must be tied to the appropriate project or publication rather than the mission statement alone.","branch":"language","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-IKS-077","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-IKS-077","src":"https://www.ciil.org/","group":"Research institutions"},"lat":12.31939,"lng":76.61983,"approx":false},
{"name":"Karnataka Janapada University","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"","state":"Karnataka","about":"Karnataka Janapada University focuses on folklore and indigenous knowledge traditions from its Gotagodi campus. Its stated emphasis on oral cultural expression and community worldviews makes it a research institution concerned with knowledge beyond formal written canons.","full":"The university's official Kannada introduction describes a commitment to studying and developing desi knowledge traditions in relation to present needs. It gives particular attention to folk worldviews, oral cultural expression and oral discourse, and identifies its campus at Gotagodi in Haveri district. The institution provides a research context for examining how knowledge is voiced, performed and sustained within communities, while the methods and findings of particular studies should be assessed from their own publications and field documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-IKS-078","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-IKS-078","src":"https://www.janapadauniversity.ac.in/","group":"Research institutions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mysuru Palace (Amba Vilas)","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Mysuru","state":"Karnataka","about":"Mysuru Palace combines Indo-Saracenic architecture with carved doors, paintings, stained glass and ceremonial collections. Its halls and courtly objects provide evidence for studying artistic patronage, architectural exchange and the public representation of the Wadiyar court.","full":"The Mysuru district administration describes the present Amba Vilas Palace as a design by Henry Irwin, with a Durbar Hall, sculpted pillars and a marriage pavilion featuring glazed flooring and stained glass. It also identifies carved doors, paintings, a golden howdah and other courtly objects. The building and collections provide a specific setting for examining the interaction of regional crafts and wider architectural influences, while its reconstruction history should be distinguished from that of earlier palaces on the site.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-079","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-079","src":"https://mysore.nic.in/en/tourist-place/mysuru-palace/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":12.30277,"lng":76.65524,"approx":false},
{"name":"Government Museum, Bengaluru","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Bengaluru","state":"Karnataka","about":"Bengaluru's Government Museum houses archaeological and artistic collections that include prehistoric objects, sculpture, musical instruments and paintings. These holdings connect material technologies and visual culture with the study and public presentation of several regional historical traditions.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism's Bengaluru account identifies the Government Museum on Kasturba Road and lists prehistoric artefacts, Vijayanagara, Hoysala and Chalukya sculpture, musical instruments and several schools of painting. This collection provides an entry point for comparing stoneworking, instrument-making and visual representation across different periods and regions. The destination page establishes broad collection scope; detailed knowledge-history research requires museum catalogues, provenance records and object-level analysis rather than assigning every item a local origin.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-080","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-080","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/karnataka/bengaluru","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":12.97487,"lng":77.59577,"approx":false},
{"name":"Vidyashankara Temple, Sringeri","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Sringeri","state":"Karnataka","about":"The Vidyashankara Temple at Sringeri is a stone building combining architectural features described by its custodians as Hoysala and Dravidian. Its apsidal ends, raised plinth and pillared mandapa provide specific material for architectural comparison.","full":"The Sringeri Sharada Peetham's temple account describes Vidyashankara as a stone structure with a high plinth, apsidal east-west ends and a mandapa containing twelve large monolithic pillars. It identifies shrines and sculptural figures around the sanctum and situates the building within a continuing ritual setting. These documented features support study of structural planning, stone-carving and the relationship between worship and architectural space, while popular astronomical interpretations require evidence beyond the consulted description.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-081","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-081","src":"https://www.sringerisharadapeetham.org/temples/sri-vidyashankara","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":13.41571,"lng":75.25148,"approx":false},
{"name":"Keladi Rameshwara Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Karnataka","about":"The Rameshwara Temple at Keladi belongs to the former capital landscape of the Keladi Nayakas. Karnataka Tourism describes a combination of Dravidian and Kadamba features, making it a candidate for studying regional architectural adaptation and dynastic patronage.","full":"Karnataka Tourism identifies Keladi as an early capital of the Keladi Nayakas and highlights the Rameshwara Temple as its principal monument. The official summary describes a combination of Dravidian and Kadamba architectural features. This provides a limited but concrete starting point for examining the region's temple-building traditions and political patronage; detailed claims about materials, carvings, workshops or individual construction phases need a monument survey beyond the introductory destination page.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-082","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-082","src":"https://karnatakatourism.org/en/destinations/keladi/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Keeladi Archaeological Settlement","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Manalur","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Keeladi is an excavated settlement near the Vaigai River with brick structures, terracotta ring wells, roof tiles and craft-related objects. Its material record supports investigation of settlement organisation, water facilities, production and writing in early historic Tamil Nadu.","full":"The Tamil Nadu Department of Archaeology reports brick structures, terracotta ring wells, grooved roof tiles, spindle whorls, beads, metal objects and inscribed pottery from excavations at Keeladi. These finds offer distinct lines of evidence for building methods, water facilities, craft activity and forms of marking and writing. The site should be interpreted through excavated contexts and scientific reports, with the chronology of each sample or feature kept separate from broad claims about the age of an entire civilisation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-083","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-083","src":"https://www.tnarch.gov.in/node/1257","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":9.86389,"lng":78.18833,"approx":false},
{"name":"Adichanallur Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Karungulam","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Adichanallur contains an extensive urn-burial landscape and associated habitation evidence near the Tamiraparani River. Excavated pottery, bronze objects, iron implements and ornaments provide material for examining funerary practice, metalworking and early regional communities.","full":"The Tamil Nadu Department of Archaeology's excavation status report identifies Adichanallur on the Tamiraparani and distinguishes work in burial and habitation areas. Its account summarises urn burials and finds including bronze objects, iron objects, gold ornaments and pottery, alongside stone tools from a lower deposit. The site is relevant to technologies and mortuary practices evidenced by actual objects, while separate deposits and phases must not be combined into one undifferentiated chronology or cultural claim.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-084","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-084","src":"https://www.tnarch.gov.in/Library%20BOOk%20PDF/latest/Ongoing%20Archaeological%20Excavations%20in%20Tamil%20Nadu%20-%20Status%20Report%20%28July%202020%29.pdf","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":8.62639,"lng":77.8703,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kallanai (Grand Anicut)","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kallanai","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Kallanai is a historic stone-and-clay water-diversion structure across the Kaveri. Its role in directing river water toward cultivation makes it relevant to the history of irrigation, river management and the agricultural landscape of the delta.","full":"The Thanjavur Tourism Promotion Council describes the Grand Anicut as a mass of rough stone and clay across the Kaveri, associated with Karikala Chola and irrigation of neighbouring land. This establishes a concrete link between construction and agricultural water management. Its long history invites examination of how diversion structures interact with a river and cultivated landscape, but the original work, later modifications and modern canal systems must be distinguished before making engineering or longevity claims.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-086","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-086","src":"https://thanjavurtourism.org/monuments/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.83076,"lng":78.81884,"approx":false},
{"name":"Sittanavasal Jain Monuments","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Sittanavasal combines a Jain rock shelter, stone beds, Tamil-Brahmi inscriptions and a painted cave temple. The surviving features connect ascetic practice, epigraphy, rock-cut construction and early mural traditions within a single archaeological landscape.","full":"A Ministry of Culture statement identifies Sittanavasal as an ASI-protected monument with Jain ascetic stone beds and Tamil-Brahmi inscriptions in a rock shelter. It separately describes a rock-cut temple and early mural paintings. Together these features offer evidence for religious use of rocky spaces, inscriptional recording and the relationship between architecture and painting, while the different elements require their own dating and conservation histories rather than a single date applied to the entire site.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-087","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-087","src":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2196916&lang=2&reg=3","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thirumalai Nayak Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":null,"about":"Thirumalai Nayak Palace preserves large pillared halls, arches and stucco ornament in Madurai. Its brick-and-mortar construction and surviving ceremonial spaces offer evidence for studying Nayaka-period building practices, courtly display and later architectural conservation.","full":"The Madurai district administration highlights the palace's brick-and-mortar Sorgavilasam, decorated arches, stuccowork and large courtyard pillars. Its account also explains that only part of the earlier palace survives and that restoration altered the condition in which it can be seen today. These features make the monument relevant to historical structural design and ornamental craft, while its surviving fabric should be interpreted alongside loss, reuse and conservation rather than as an intact original royal complex.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-088","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-088","src":"https://madurai.nic.in/tourist-place/thirumalai-nayak-palace/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Vellore Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Vellore","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Vellore Fort is a granite-built enclosure containing secular and religious structures. Its fortifications, palace buildings, temple, mosque and church provide a setting for studying defensive construction and the changing uses of a regional military complex.","full":"The Vellore district administration describes the fort as a rectangular enclosure built from massive cut granite, with palaces and religious buildings inside. Its named components include Jalakanteswara Temple, a mosque, a church and several palace structures, alongside museums established within the complex. This range supports study of stone construction, fortified space and changing patronage and occupation, while individual buildings and collections should be analysed by phase rather than assumed to share one origin or architectural programme.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-089","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-089","src":"https://vellore.nic.in/tourist-place/vellore-fort/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":12.92047,"lng":79.12818,"approx":false},
{"name":"Padmanabhapuram Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Padmanabhapuram","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Padmanabhapuram Palace preserves timber architecture, carved woodwork, painted ceilings and murals associated with Travancore. Its courtyards and varied halls connect regional building traditions with royal use, artistic patronage and the organisation of palace space.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Padmanabhapuram's rosewood carvings, coloured mica windows, murals and the courtyard arrangement of Thai Kottaram, alongside the monolithic pillars of the Navarathri Mandappam. The complex brings timber construction and stone elements into a sequence of royal, ceremonial and residential spaces. It lies in present-day Tamil Nadu while remaining associated with Kerala's heritage administration, so geography, historical kingdom and present custodianship should be kept distinct when interpreting the monument.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-090","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-090","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/padmanabhapuram-palace/244/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.25066,"lng":77.3271,"approx":false},
{"name":"Government Museum, Chennai","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Chennai","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"The Government Museum in Egmore holds archaeological, numismatic, artistic and ethnographic collections. Bronzes, inscriptions, copper-plate grants and sculpture make it a resource for examining metal casting, historical writing, religious imagery and the material record of South India.","full":"The museum's official overview lists galleries for South Indian sculpture, inscriptions, copper-plate grants, hero stones, Jain material and wood carvings, alongside bronze and numismatic collections. These holdings support object-based study of technologies, religious iconography and historical record keeping across several regions and periods. Because the same overview identifies some galleries as temporarily closed, the collection's documented existence should be distinguished from what is currently accessible, and individual objects need catalogue-level provenance and dating.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-091","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-091","src":"https://govtmuseumchennai.org/about-us","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":13.07128,"lng":80.25704,"approx":false},
{"name":"Government Oriental Manuscripts Library and Research Centre, Chennai","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"Chennai","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"This government library preserves palm-leaf and paper manuscripts in several South Asian and West Asian languages. Its subject coverage includes mathematics, astronomy, medical traditions, architecture, music and literature, making it a substantial resource for textual knowledge history.","full":"Tamil Nadu Archaeology describes the library's palm-leaf and paper holdings in Tamil, Sanskrit, Telugu, Kannada, Marathi, Arabic, Persian and other languages. Its listed subjects include mathematics, astronomy, Siddha, Ayurveda, Unani, architecture, music, grammar and history, with consultation and copying governed by library procedures. The collection offers a route into specific knowledge traditions through manuscripts and catalogues, but the consulted page contains both an older Chepauk location and a Kotturpuram correspondence address, so access details need direct confirmation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-093","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-093","src":"https://www.tnarch.gov.in/goverment-oriental-manuscripts-library-and-research-centre","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"lat":13.11256,"lng":80.2082,"approx":true},
{"name":"DakshinaChitra Heritage Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":null,"about":"DakshinaChitra presents South Indian architecture, crafts and ways of life through relocated traditional houses, exhibitions and public programmes. Artisan interaction and workshops connect the museum's buildings and objects with continuing craft practice and educational interpretation.","full":"DakshinaChitra's own programme account explains that traditional houses from different South Indian regions were purchased, dismantled and relocated to the museum, where associated community lifestyles are interpreted. It also describes craft workshops, student learning activities, folk performances and a bazaar connecting artisans and self-help groups with visitors. The museum is therefore relevant to building craft and living practice, while reconstructed streetscapes and interpretation must be distinguished from an undisturbed original settlement or a complete account of any community.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-094","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-094","src":"https://dakshinachitra.net/what_we_do","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kalakshetra Foundation","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"Chennai","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Kalakshetra Foundation is an arts institution offering dance, music and visual-arts education. Its teaching and performance work provides a setting for examining how artistic technique, repertoire and aesthetic ideas are transmitted through organised theoretical and practical study.","full":"Kalakshetra's official college page describes training in Bharatanatyam, vocal and instrumental Carnatic music and visual arts, combining theoretical study with practical work. It identifies educational options through schools, the Rukmini Devi College of Fine Arts and evening courses. The institution provides evidence for the modern organisation of traditional arts teaching and repertoire, while its particular pedagogy should be distinguished from the full diversity of lineages and practitioners associated with each art form.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-IKS-095","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-IKS-095","src":"https://kalakshetra.in/college/rdcfa/","group":"Research institutions"},"lat":12.98857,"lng":80.26243,"approx":false},
{"name":"Central Council for Research in Siddha","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"","state":"Jammu and Kashmir","about":"The Central Council for Research in Siddha is a Ministry of Ayush body conducting research on the Siddha medical system. Its headquarters and research network provide an institutional setting for examining the documentation and investigation of a traditional healthcare knowledge system.","full":"The Council's current NIC-hosted portal identifies CCRS as a Ministry of Ayush body headquartered in Chennai and describes clinical, preclinical and literary research across its institutional network. It states objectives covering knowledge preservation, research collaboration and publication, and links research outputs and Siddha literature translation work. For the Atlas, this provides a route to documented projects and medical texts; the existence of the Council does not establish that every traditional preparation or treatment has demonstrated safety or clinical benefit.","branch":"health","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-IKS-096","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-IKS-096","src":"https://ccrs.ayush.gov.in/","group":"Research institutions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kancheepuram Silk Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Kancheepuram silk weaving combines mulberry silk, zari design and specialised interlocking of body and border. The korvai technique and coordinated shuttle work connect the textile's appearance with skilled loom operation and the transmission of weaving knowledge.","full":"The Development Commissioner for Handicrafts' GI compendium describes Kancheepuram silk using mulberry silk in warp and weft, contrasting borders and three-shuttle weaving locally termed korvai. It explains that the body and border are interlocked during weaving. These technical details make the tradition relevant to loom planning, yarn relationships and design execution, while the materials and methods used in a particular contemporary sari need product-specific checking rather than inference from a geographical name alone.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-097","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-097","src":"https://handicrafts.nic.in/cmsUpload/20170506115215GI_BOOK_FINAL_ilovepdf_compressed.PDF","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":12.96472,"lng":79.98397,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thanjavur Painting Tradition","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Thanjavur","state":"Tamil Nadu","about":"Thanjavur painting is associated with detailed religious imagery and gold-foil embellishment. The tradition connects pictorial composition, decorative surface work and patronage with the continuing production of images depicting deities and mythological scenes in Tamil Nadu.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism's Thanjavur account identifies the city's painting tradition by its intricate detailing, gold-foil embellishment and frequent depictions of Hindu deities and mythological scenes. These characteristics make the paintings relevant to visual storytelling and the handling of decorative materials. The living craft should be investigated through particular artists, objects and documented processes, with contemporary market production distinguished from older paintings and without assuming that every image shares the same materials, technique or date.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-099","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-099","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/tamil-nadu/thanjavur","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":10.65904,"lng":79.20143,"approx":true},
{"name":"Department of Palmleaf Manuscripts, Tamil University","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"","state":null,"about":"Tamil University's Department of Palmleaf Manuscripts collects, preserves, catalogues and publishes manuscript material. Its holdings span subjects including philosophy, medicine, grammar and Sangam literature, connecting textual scholarship with conservation, digitisation and public teaching about manuscript heritage.","full":"The department's official page sets out objectives covering collection, scientific preservation, digitisation, microfilming, palm-leaf archives and annotated publication. It identifies holdings in philosophy, medicine, astrology, grammar and Sangam literature and describes descriptive catalogues and workshops. These activities connect the physical care of manuscripts with reading, interpretation and the wider circulation of knowledge, while manuscript counts and publication totals are institutional snapshots that should be checked against current catalogues before being used quantitatively.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-100","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-100","src":"https://www.tamiluniversity.ac.in/english/faculty/department-of-palmleaf-manuscriptology/","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Amaravati Archaeological Museum and Mahachaitya Site","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Amaravati Town","state":"Andhra Pradesh","about":"The Amaravati Archaeological Museum stands beside the Mahachaitya and preserves material associated with the region's Buddhist heritage. Reliefs, sculptures, inscriptions and other excavated objects connect religious narratives with stoneworking, historical writing and archaeological interpretation.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism describes the museum as adjacent to the Amaravati Mahachaitya and identifies collections of sculpture, reliefs, inscriptions, pottery and coins from regional archaeological work. Buddhist imagery and narrative scenes are a particular focus. Considered together with the surviving stupa site, the museum provides a setting for studying sculptural production and religious communication, while the provenance of an individual object must distinguish Amaravati from surrounding sites and from material now held in other museums.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-101","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-101","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/andhra-pradesh/amaravati/amaravati-archaeological-museum","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":16.57454,"lng":80.35807,"approx":false},
{"name":"Nagarjunakonda Archaeological Museum and Reconstructed Remains","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":null,"about":"Nagarjunakonda's island museum preserves antiquities recovered from the Krishna valley before reservoir submergence. Reconstructed monuments, carved limestone, inscriptions and models connect Buddhist and other historical traditions with archaeological knowledge, architectural interpretation and the history of salvage conservation.","full":"The national Museums of India portal describes excavations undertaken before the Nagarjuna Sagar reservoir submerged the valley, followed by the reconstruction of selected structures on higher ground. The island museum displays carved limestone, sculpture, inscriptions and other recovered objects, with models explaining the former landscape. Its collections allow study of Buddhist narratives, religious architecture and historical writing, while the present island arrangement must be distinguished from the original locations and relationships of the excavated remains.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-102","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-102","src":"https://museumsofindia.gov.in/repository/museum/nkm_hyd","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Salihundam Buddhist Monastic Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"Salihundam preserves Buddhist stupas and monastic remains on a hill beside the Vamsadhara River. Excavated relic caskets, sculptures and religious structures provide material for investigating monastic organisation, sacred architecture and changing forms of Buddhist practice.","full":"The Srikakulam district administration identifies Salihundam on the Vamsadhara and describes stupas, a monastic complex, a chaityagriha, relic caskets and sculptures recovered through excavation. These different classes of evidence support study of religious space, devotional imagery and the handling of relics within a regional Buddhist centre. The site's several phases should be kept distinct, and claims about direct overseas transmission of Buddhism require more specific evidence than the introductory district account supplies.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-103","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-103","src":"https://srikakulam.ap.gov.in/engineering-tourism/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thotlakonda Buddhist Monastery","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Mangamari Peta","state":"Andhra Pradesh","about":"Thotlakonda contains excavated Buddhist stupas, viharas, halls and rock-cut water facilities on a coastal hill. Its combination of religious buildings and water infrastructure supports study of monastic settlement, construction and adaptation to a hilltop environment.","full":"The Visakhapatnam district administration describes Thotlakonda's rock-cut troughs with steps and associates the site's name with these water facilities. Its excavation summary also identifies a mahastupa, viharas, votive stupas, chaitya buildings, halls and inscribed material. The relationship between these features makes the site useful for examining how a monastic community organised worship, residence and water access, while proposed connections to particular Buddhist schools require more detailed study of inscriptions and excavated contexts.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-104","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-104","src":"https://visakhapatnam.ap.gov.in/how-to-reach/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":17.82864,"lng":83.40921,"approx":false},
{"name":"Lepakshi Veerabhadra Temple and Monolithic Nandi","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"Lepakshi combines granite sculpture, a monumental Nandi, temple architecture and painted religious narratives. Its murals and carved pillars provide material for examining Vijayanagara artistic practice, iconography and the coordination of painting and stonework within a sacred complex.","full":"India's UNESCO Tentative List submission describes the Veerabhadra temple's granite sculptures, carved pillars and murals made in a lime medium on plaster. The painted narratives include episodes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata and Shaiva traditions, while inscriptions document aspects of patronage and donations. These complementary sources connect artistic technique with religious storytelling and historical administration, with the temple and nearby monolithic Nandi retained as one ensemble distinct from the existing Hampi record.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-105","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-105","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6607/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kondapalli Painted Wooden Toys","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Kondapalli","state":"Andhra Pradesh","about":"Kondapalli toy makers shape lightweight Tella Poniki wood into figures and miniature scenes, assembling and painting the pieces by hand. Material preparation, natural adhesives and detailed surface work link the craft with community livelihoods and festival display.","full":"The Development Commissioner for Handicrafts describes Tella Poniki wood as the principal material for Kondapalli toys and identifies tamarind-seed paste, sawdust and several kinds of paint among the working materials. Products include animals, mythological figures and miniature village scenes, with festival display forming part of the tradition's use. The craft is relevant to wood selection, assembly and visual representation, while workshop practices vary and neither natural dyes nor a single origin story should be assumed for all contemporary production.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-106","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-106","src":"https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Figurines%26Toys/Kondapalli_Bommallu/KondapalliBommalluWebPage.html","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":16.62371,"lng":80.54688,"approx":true},
{"name":"Srikalahasti Hand-Painted Kalamkari","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Srikalahasti","state":"Andhra Pradesh","about":"Srikalahasti Kalamkari uses a bamboo pen and prepared cotton cloth to create narrative and decorative textiles. Cloth treatment, mordanting, repeated washing and hand painting connect the craft's visual imagery with detailed knowledge of materials and process.","full":"The official handicrafts account describes Srikalahasti Kalamkari as hand-painted textile work using a bamboo pen, treated cotton, myrobalan and colour-fixing materials. Its process combines preparation, drawing, painting, washing and drying, while motifs include religious narratives, plants, animals and secular themes. The craft therefore supports study of dye-related knowledge and the translation of stories into textile images, with hand-painted work distinguished from related block-printed traditions and contemporary substitutions documented at workshop level.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-107","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-107","src":"https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Textile/Other_Textiles_Based/Srikalahasthi_Kalamkari/SrikalahasthiKalamkariWebPage.html","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":13.7518,"lng":79.70023,"approx":true},
{"name":"Durgi Limestone-Carving Tradition","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Durgi","state":"Andhra Pradesh","about":"Durgi artisans carve soft limestone into religious figures, decorative pieces and useful objects. Stone testing, marking, clay modelling, chiselling and finishing connect material judgement with sculptural design and a continuing regional craft-training and cooperative setting.","full":"Palnadu's official ODOP account describes artisans checking limestone for flaws by sound, marking designs, preparing clay models and progressively cutting and finishing the stone with different tools. It also identifies a sculpture school, cooperative organisation and an expanding range of decorative and utilitarian products. These documented operations make Durgi relevant to material selection and sculptural pedagogy, while the account's proposed ancient lineage should be separated from the directly described contemporary workshop process.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-108","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-108","src":"https://palnadu.ap.gov.in/odop2023/","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":16.42324,"lng":79.54311,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kakatiya Rudreshwara (Ramappa) Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Telangana","about":"Ramappa Temple combines sandstone construction, hard-stone carving and a roof tower made with lightweight porous bricks. Its sculptures and relationship with a reservoir and cultivated landscape connect Kakatiya building practice, performance imagery and environmental setting.","full":"UNESCO describes the Rudreshwara temple at Palampet as a sandstone building with carved granite and dolerite beams and pillars, and a stepped tower using lightweight porous bricks. Its sculptural figures depict regional dance customs, while the setting includes a water reservoir and agricultural land. These specific features support study of material selection, structural weight, stone finishing and the relationship between sacred architecture and its wider landscape, without assuming that every popular engineering explanation has been experimentally established.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-109","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-109","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1570/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Warangal Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Khila Warangal mandal","state":"Telangana","about":"Warangal Fort preserves a Kakatiya fortified landscape with successive circular defensive enclosures and substantial surviving ruins. Its layout provides material for studying military planning, the organisation of a capital and the effects of later damage and reuse.","full":"The Hanumakonda district administration describes Warangal Fort as developed under successive Kakatiya rulers after the capital moved from Hanamkonda. Its account identifies three circular layers of fortification and notes extensive damage, with much surviving material concentrated toward the centre. The layout is relevant to defensive planning and political space, while the present arrangement of ruins must be read through destruction, later alteration and conservation rather than assumed to reproduce the full original capital.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-110","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-110","src":"https://hanumakonda.telangana.gov.in/places-of-interest/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":17.95649,"lng":79.61543,"approx":false},
{"name":"Qutb Shahi Tombs","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Hyderabad","state":"Telangana","about":"The Qutb Shahi Tombs form a dynastic necropolis with mausoleums, mosques and a mortuary bath. Dated inscriptions and varied architectural forms support study of Deccan building traditions, funerary practice and relationships between Persianate and regional design.","full":"India's UNESCO Tentative List documentation describes the Qutb Shahi necropolis as a complex of royal and associated tombs, mosques and a mortuary bath. It highlights dated epitaph inscriptions and variations in mausoleum form as evidence for architectural development and family relationships. The site provides a specific record of Deccan masonry, carved stucco and commemorative practice, with epigraphically identified tombs distinguished from buildings whose popular names are not confirmed by inscriptions.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-111","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-111","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/5573","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":17.39737,"lng":78.40016,"approx":false},
{"name":"State Archaeology Museum, Hyderabad","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Hyderabad","state":"Telangana","about":"Hyderabad's State Museum preserves archaeological and historical material in a heritage building at Public Gardens. Its Buddhist and Jain sculpture and wider antiquities collection provide a resource for comparing religious imagery, material craft and regional cultural histories.","full":"The Department of Heritage Telangana lists the State Museum at Public Gardens and describes a heritage building containing Buddhist and Jain sculptures alongside other antiquities. The institution's stated museum purpose is to make archaeological, historical and cultural material available to students, researchers and the public. These collections offer a route into object-based study of religious art and material culture, while the provenance and technical interpretation of individual works need catalogue records and should not be inferred from the museum's location.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-112","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-112","src":"https://tgarch.telangana.gov.in/Museumsinfo.aspx","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":17.39914,"lng":78.47036,"approx":false},
{"name":"Cheriyal Scroll Painting","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Cheriyal","state":"Telangana","about":"Cheriyal painting turns stories into vivid painted scrolls traditionally used with oral narration. Prepared cloth, layered surface treatment and a recognisable red-ground visual vocabulary connect material craft with community storytelling and the transmission of epic and local narratives.","full":"The Development Commissioner for Handicrafts describes Cheriyal scrolls as visual aids used by storytellers and explains the preparation of khadi cloth with starch, tamarind-seed paste, gum and clay. The painted narratives draw on mythology, folklore and community subjects, while contemporary products adapt the imagery to smaller formats. The tradition connects oral performance with pictorial sequencing and surface preparation, although the source's distant origin-date claim requires stronger evidence than the craft process description provides.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-113","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-113","src":"https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Cheriyal_painting/Cheriyal_paintingwebpage.html","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":17.55395,"lng":78.13141,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pochampally Ikat Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Pochampally","state":"Telangana","about":"Pochampally ikat creates patterns by tying and dyeing yarn before it is woven. Aligning the coloured warp and weft demands careful planning and coordination, linking textile design with practical knowledge of resist dyeing, measurement and loom work.","full":"The official handicrafts account explains that Pochampally artisans tie selected sections of warp or weft yarn, dye the exposed areas and then weave the prepared threads into patterned cloth. Double ikat adds the need to coordinate designs in both directions. These operations make the tradition relevant to sequencing, measurement, resist-dye techniques and loom control, while fibres, colourants and contemporary production methods need workshop-specific documentation rather than assumptions based on promotional claims about sustainability or antiquity.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-114","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-114","src":"https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Textile/Other_Textiles_Based/ikkat/IkkatWebPage.html","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":18.42282,"lng":79.55578,"approx":true},
{"name":"Arikamedu Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Veerampattinam","state":"Puducherry","about":"Arikamedu preserves archaeological evidence for a settlement involved in maritime exchange near Puducherry. Imported ceramics, glassware, beads and craft remains connect the site with the material study of trade, production and contact across the Indian Ocean.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism's Arikamedu account identifies the site as protected by ASI and describes imported pottery, glassware, semi-precious stone beads and evidence of craft activity. These finds provide a basis for examining the movement of materials and goods as well as local manufacture within an exchange-connected settlement. Imported objects demonstrate contact rather than automatically proving a foreign colony, and specific production areas and chronologies need comparison with excavation reports rather than reliance on popular labels such as Roman city.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-115","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-115","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/puducherry/puducherry/arikamedu","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":11.90231,"lng":79.81996,"approx":false},
{"name":"French Institute of Pondicherry Manuscript Collection","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The French Institute of Pondicherry preserves palm-leaf manuscripts and paper transcripts, with particular strength in Shaiva texts. Cataloguing, preservation and digital access connect ritual manuals, philosophical traditions and other learned works with continuing research into textual transmission.","full":"The Institute's manuscript page describes collections gathered from temples, priests, monasteries and private owners, including Sanskrit works commonly written in Grantha and materials in other regional scripts. Shaiva Agamas, ritual manuals, devotional literature and traditional medical texts appear among the documented subjects. The collection supports research through physical manuscripts, transcripts and catalogues, with these forms kept distinct and digital access treated as a research service rather than automatic permission to reproduce manuscript photographs.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-116","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-116","src":"https://www.ifpindia.org/resources/manuscripts/","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Puducherry Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Puducherry","state":"Puducherry","about":"Puducherry Museum presents archaeological, historical, geological and craft material from the territory and beyond. Its collections connect regional art and material culture with French-period records and objects, offering a setting for examining cultural exchange and changing technologies.","full":"The Department of Art and Culture describes Puducherry Museum as a multipurpose institution with French India, art-and-craft and geological sections, alongside collections of bronzes, stone sculpture and other objects. Its account includes a hand-operated printing machine recovered from the government press and handmade artefacts associated with both France and Puducherry. These specific holdings support study of craft, printing and exchange, while individual objects require provenance and dating checks beyond the department's general gallery overview.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-117","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-117","src":"https://art.py.gov.in/puducherry-museum","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":11.9344,"lng":79.83495,"approx":false},
{"name":"Lava Dance of Minicoy","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Minicoy","state":"Lakshadweep","about":"Lava is a dance tradition associated with festive occasions on Minicoy. Its island setting links performance with a distinctive Mahl-speaking community, village institutions and shared celebrations, providing a starting point for documenting local forms of embodied cultural knowledge.","full":"The Lakshadweep administration identifies Lava as a traditional dance performed on festive occasions at Minicoy and distinguishes the island's language and cultural practices from those of the northern islands. The same account describes village houses and elected elders as part of its community organisation. This establishes a specific place and social context for the performance tradition, while movement vocabulary, music, costume and modes of teaching require direct practitioner documentation because the consulted official source does not describe them.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-118","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-118","src":"https://lakshadweep.gov.in/tourist-place/minicoy/","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":8.29523,"lng":73.06484,"approx":true},
{"name":"Zonal Anthropological Museum, Sri Vijaya Puram","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Port Blair","state":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands","about":"The Zonal Anthropological Museum presents the life and material culture of Andaman and Nicobar communities. Its institutional interpretation connects objects and cultural history with environmental knowledge, while offering a public resource that can be studied without visiting restricted community areas.","full":"An Anthropological Survey of India museum-development document identifies the Zonal Anthropological Museum in Port Blair and describes displays concerning human evolution and the life and culture of Andaman and Nicobar communities. It also identifies environmental conservation and resource use as themes of interpretation. The museum offers a public setting for studying material culture and how it is represented, while exhibition narratives should be distinguished from present-day community voices and from the current practices of particular groups.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-119","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-119","src":"https://ansi.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/formidable/5/Re-tendering-of-EOI-for-ZAM-Port-Blair-Ground-Floor-28-03-2024-1.pdf","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":11.66438,"lng":92.74023,"approx":false},
{"name":"Nicobari Hodi Canoe-Building Knowledge","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Car Nicobar","state":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands","about":"The hodi is a Nicobari wooden watercraft made through specialist knowledge of trees, hull construction and local maritime needs. Research at Car Nicobar documents inherited skills alongside changing tools and continued use in several forms of fishing.","full":"A study by researchers associated with agricultural and fisheries institutions documents hodi construction at Car Nicobar, including expert selection of suitable trees and skills inherited within Nicobari communities. It records the use of locally obtained timber and the adoption of electrical and mechanical tools alongside earlier hand tools. The watercraft links practical material judgement with maritime work, demonstrating an adapting knowledge tradition rather than a fixed technological relic; the study's field context should not be generalised to every island or builder.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-120","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-120","src":"https://nopr.niscpr.res.in/bitstream/123456789/32045/1/IJTK%201(1)%20161-168.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":9.18706,"lng":92.77301,"approx":true},
{"name":"Lothal Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Dholka Taluka","state":"Gujarat","about":"Lothal preserves the remains of a Harappan settlement with drainage, raised building platforms and evidence of bead manufacture. Its excavated materials connect settlement planning, craft production and long-distance exchange in the ancient Gujarat region.","full":"Lothal's excavated settlement includes a citadel, lower town, drainage arrangements, a warehouse and a bead-making area, as described by the Ministry of Tourism. Semiprecious stone beads and other finds provide material for examining manufacturing techniques and exchange networks. The site's architectural and craft evidence offers a concrete route into Harappan knowledge of urban organisation, materials and production without requiring certainty about every proposed maritime interpretation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-121","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-121","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/gujarat/ahmedabad/lothal","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":22.52268,"lng":72.24931,"approx":false},
{"name":"Champaner-Pavagadh Archaeological Park","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Manchi Haveli, Pavagadh","state":"Gujarat","about":"Champaner-Pavagadh combines settlement remains, fortifications, religious buildings and water-retaining installations within a hill-and-plain landscape. The archaeological park makes regional urban planning and the relationship between water, terrain and architecture visible across successive periods.","full":"UNESCO describes Champaner-Pavagadh as a landscape containing prehistoric and medieval settlement evidence, a hill fortress and the remains of a Gujarat Sultanate capital. Its palaces, residential precincts, mosques, temples and water-retaining installations preserve connected evidence of how a city used its terrain. Much of the former capital remains unexcavated, so the park is valuable both for surviving structures and for questions that future archaeological work may resolve.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-122","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-122","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1101","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":22.48328,"lng":73.53307,"approx":false},
{"name":"Adalaj ni Vav","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Adalaj","state":"Gujarat","about":"Adalaj ni Vav is a multi-storeyed stepwell with carved pillars, three entrances and an octagonal opening. Its passages and landings connect water access with architectural design, ornament and the management of light and air below ground.","full":"Gujarat Tourism describes Adalaj's five-storeyed stepwell as an example of architectural exchange, with ornamental carving on its pillars and walls. Openings above the landings admit light and air, while three entrances lead towards the underground levels and octagonal well. The surviving arrangement allows the study of water-access infrastructure as a social and architectural space, linking masonry, circulation and environmental design in one structure.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-124","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-124","src":"https://gujarattourism.com/central-zone/gandhinagar/adalaj-ni-vav.html","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":23.16714,"lng":72.58014,"approx":false},
{"name":"Sarkhej Roza","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Sarkhej","state":"Gujarat","about":"Sarkhej Roza groups tombs, a mosque and palace buildings around a large tank. Its carved stone screens and connected spaces offer evidence for the relationship between water infrastructure, religious patronage and architectural craftsmanship in the Gujarat Sultanate.","full":"Gujarat Tourism identifies Sarkhej Roza as a mosque, tomb and palace ensemble associated with the spiritual teacher Ahmed Khattu Ganj Baksh. Its buildings cluster around a large tank, and the account highlights geometric stone screens in the mausoleums. The combination of water, courtyards, religious buildings and royal retreat spaces offers a basis for examining how architecture organised both practical needs and commemorative uses within a shared landscape.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-125","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-125","src":"https://gujarattourism.com/central-zone/ahmedabad/sarkhej-roza.html","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":22.99203,"lng":72.50503,"approx":false},
{"name":"Uparkot Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Junagadh","state":"Gujarat","about":"Uparkot is a fortified complex containing rock-cut stepwells, Buddhist caves and later buildings. Its water structures and changing fabric provide material for studying defensive infrastructure, excavation in rock and the repeated adaptation of an urban citadel.","full":"Gujarat Tourism describes Uparkot's high ramparts, Buddhist caves and two stepwells cut into solid rock. The account distinguishes Adi Kadi Vav from Navghan Kuvo, whose stairway spirals around the shaft, and notes an inscription associated with a later ruler. Together these features make the complex relevant to the study of water provision within fortifications, stone-working practices and the accumulation of different building phases at one site.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-126","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-126","src":"https://gujarattourism.com/saurashtra/junagadh/uparkot-fort.html","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":21.52419,"lng":70.4708,"approx":false},
{"name":"Watson Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Rajkot","state":"Gujarat","about":"Watson Museum brings together inscriptions, manuscripts, textiles, sculpture and archaeological objects from Saurashtra. Its collections link material evidence from ancient settlements with regional embroidery, musical instruments, jewellery and other forms of inherited practical and artistic knowledge.","full":"The museum's official Gujarat Tourism account lists manuscripts, inscriptions, coins, sculptures, musical instruments, woodwork, textiles and folk embroidery among its sections. It also identifies Indus Valley objects collected from sites in Saurashtra and clothing or jewellery associated with regional communities. These holdings support comparisons between archaeological manufacture and later craft traditions, provided individual objects are interpreted through their catalogued provenance and not assumed to represent an uninterrupted lineage.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-127","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-127","src":"https://gujarattourism.com/saurashtra/rajkot/watson-museum.html","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":22.30024,"lng":70.80192,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kutch Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Bhuj","state":"Gujarat","about":"Kutch Museum preserves inscriptions, textiles, metalwork, sculpture and displays about the region's communities. Its collections provide routes into the study of historical scripts, local craft practices and the material diversity of Kutch.","full":"Gujarat Tourism describes the museum's Kshatrapa inscriptions alongside examples of the Kutchi script and collections of textiles, weapons, silverware and sculpture. A dedicated section presents community costumes, folk arts and crafts from Kutch. The museum therefore brings epigraphic evidence and ethnographic material into one institution, supporting object-based study of regional communication, making and dress while requiring care about how communities are represented.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-128","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-128","src":"https://gujarattourism.com/kutch-zone/kutch/kutch-museum.html","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":23.24859,"lng":69.66645,"approx":false},
{"name":"Calico Museum of Textiles","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Gujarat","about":"The Calico Museum preserves historical and modern handmade Indian textiles in Ahmedabad. Its collections and publications provide an object-based setting for studying weaving, textile decoration and the technical history of regional cloth-making practices.","full":"Gujarat Tourism identifies the Calico Museum as an institution devoted to historical and technical study of Indian textiles. Its account names Kashmiri shawls and double-ikat fabrics among the collections and describes a publications programme on historical textiles and contemporary craft. The museum therefore offers a route into textile materials, dyeing and weaving, with individual processes and regional attributions best established through its object catalogues.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-129","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-129","src":"https://gujarattourism.com/central-zone/ahmedabad/calico-museum-of-textiles.html","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Lalbhai Dalpatbhai Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Navrangpura","state":"Gujarat","about":"The Lalbhai Dalpatbhai Museum preserves manuscripts, miniature paintings, sculpture, woodwork and other Indian art. Its strong Gujarat and Jain holdings support the study of illustrated texts, iconography and the relationship between written learning and artistic production.","full":"The museum describes a collection spanning sculptures, manuscripts, paintings, drawings, beadwork, textiles and woodwork. Its director's account particularly identifies Gujarat's manuscript and Jain artistic heritage, together with Buddhist works from other Asian regions. This range supports the study of how texts, images and crafted objects communicated religious and cultural knowledge, while also allowing comparisons across regions through documented collection histories.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-130","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-130","src":"https://ldmuseum.co.in/directorsdesk.php","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":23.03317,"lng":72.5499,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kalibangan Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Kalibangan","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Kalibangan is a Harappan archaeological site in northern Rajasthan. Its excavated settlement remains and associated material culture offer evidence for studying early town planning and the ways archaeological investigation reconstructs life in the region.","full":"The Rajasthan Foundation identifies Kalibangan in Hanumangarh district as an Indus Valley civilisation site and describes it as a planned settlement. Its official account illustrates pre-Harappan structural remains, seals and cooking pots. These categories provide a starting point for investigating settlement form, ceramic production and systems of marking or identification, although detailed conclusions about agriculture, chronology and ritual require the excavation reports.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-131","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-131","src":"https://foundation.rajasthan.gov.in/IndusValleyCivilizationinRajasthan.aspx","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":29.46965,"lng":74.13479,"approx":true},
{"name":"Raniji ki Baori","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Bundi","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Raniji ki Baori is a multi-storeyed stepwell with carved pillars, an arched entrance and places of worship. It connects water provision with royal patronage, social gathering and the architectural treatment of a structure extending below ground.","full":"Rajasthan Tourism identifies Raniji ki Baori as a stepwell commissioned by Rani Nathavati and describes its carved pillars, entrance and elephant sculptures. The account explains that Bundi's baoris addressed water needs during dry periods and also served as places for local gathering. Its successive levels and worship spaces provide material for examining how a utilitarian water structure could accommodate social and religious functions.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-133","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-133","src":"https://www.tourism.rajasthan.gov.in/raniji-ki-baori.html","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":25.44021,"lng":75.63943,"approx":false},
{"name":"Amber Palace and Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Jaipur Municipal Corporation","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Amber's fortified palace combines courtyards, audience spaces, residential quarters, gardens and temples. Its sandstone and marble fabric offers material for studying Rajasthani building practice, spatial hierarchy and the organisation of a large royal complex.","full":"Rajasthan Tourism describes Amber as a palace-fort built with sandstone and marble and divided into four principal courtyard sections. The account identifies public audience space, royal quarters, the zenana, gardens and temples, as well as a tunnel connection towards Jaigarh. This combination supports the study of circulation, defence and differentiated domestic or ceremonial spaces within a hill setting, with the surviving fabric requiring phase-specific architectural interpretation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-134","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-134","src":"https://www.tourism.rajasthan.gov.in/amber-palace.html","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":26.98547,"lng":75.85009,"approx":false},
{"name":"Chittorgarh Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Chittauri","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Chittorgarh Fort occupies a hilltop approached through successive gateways and contains a range of historic monuments. Its surviving layout offers evidence for studying defensive access, monumental construction and the relationship between built heritage and regional oral memory.","full":"The Rajasthan Tourism account describes a hilltop fortified area reached by a winding road passing through seven gates. Within it stand monuments and commemorative structures associated with different episodes in the fort's history. These features provide a starting point for studying controlled approaches, defence and commemoration, while the bardic accounts associated with Chittorgarh belong to a separate, valuable field of oral-history investigation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-135","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-135","src":"https://www.tourism.rajasthan.gov.in/content/rajasthan-tourism/en/tourist-destinations/chittorgarh-fort.html","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":24.88171,"lng":74.64669,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kumbhalgarh Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Quila Kumbhalgarh","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Kumbhalgarh's fortifications enclose a landscape containing dams, tanks and stepwells. Its water-retaining works provide a particularly concrete basis for examining rainwater collection and the infrastructure needed to sustain a fortified hill settlement.","full":"The nomination documentation submitted to UNESCO explains that Kumbhalgarh relied on collecting rainwater through dams, wells and stepwells positioned around its terrain. It describes masonry dams spanning narrow passages between hills and stepped wells placed in catchment areas. These surviving works connect the fort's defensive architecture with practical knowledge of runoff, storage and topography, rather than treating its walls as an isolated architectural achievement.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-136","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-136","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/uploads/nominations/247rev.pdf","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":25.15268,"lng":73.58629,"approx":false},
{"name":"Gagron Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Jhalawar","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Gagron is described by Rajasthan Tourism as a hill-and-water fort surrounded by rivers on three sides. Its setting provides a useful example for studying how natural terrain and water boundaries contributed to regional defensive planning.","full":"Rajasthan Tourism identifies Gagron as one of the six forts in the Hill Forts of Rajasthan World Heritage property. The official account emphasises the combination of a hill position and water on three sides. That documented relationship between terrain and enclosure is the principal knowledge connection here; a detailed account of construction phases, hydraulic works or military arrangements would require a site survey and conservation sources.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-137","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-137","src":"https://www.tourism.rajasthan.gov.in/forts.html","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":24.62571,"lng":76.18827,"approx":false},
{"name":"Jaisalmer Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Jaisalmer","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Jaisalmer Fort remains an inhabited fortified settlement with homes, shops and historic havelis. It connects architectural craftsmanship with the continuing use of urban space, making it relevant to the study of building traditions and heritage in everyday life.","full":"Rajasthan Tourism describes Jaisalmer Fort as an inhabited ensemble containing shops, hotels and havelis where families continue to live. It attributes the fort's construction to local craftsmanship and identifies it as a World Heritage site. The combination of historic fabric and continuing occupation offers a basis for studying domestic architecture, urban adaptation and the practical challenges of conserving a fortified settlement that also functions as a community.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-138","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-138","src":"https://www.tourism.rajasthan.gov.in/jaisalmer.html","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":26.91277,"lng":70.91249,"approx":false},
{"name":"Albert Hall Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Jaipur","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Albert Hall Museum preserves paintings, sculpture, textiles, jewellery and other collections in Jaipur. These holdings provide a broad material record of artistic production, dress and decorative techniques, with particular opportunities to study regional painting and craft traditions.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism describes galleries devoted to art, jewellery and coins, together with collections of miniature paintings, sculptures, textiles and weaponry. It also notes the museum building's sandstone and marble construction. The institution offers a route into the study of artistic materials and regional craft through objects, while its diverse collections require object-specific dates and provenance before they can support more detailed knowledge-history claims.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-139","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-139","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/rajasthan/jaipur/albert-hall-museum","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":26.91169,"lng":75.8195,"approx":false},
{"name":"Rajasthan Oriental Research Institute, Jodhpur","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"Jodhpur","state":"Rajasthan","about":"The Rajasthan Oriental Research Institute collects manuscripts in Sanskrit, Prakrit, Apabhramsha and regional languages. Its subject holdings include philosophy, history, Ayurveda, Jyotisha and Shilpa, making it a resource for studying technical as well as literary traditions.","full":"The institute's manuscript introduction describes collections assembled through purchase, transfer and donation, with a headquarters repository at Jodhpur and branches elsewhere in Rajasthan. It identifies Sanskrit, Prakrit, Apabhramsha and Rajasthani material, including works on Ayurveda, Jyotisha, Shilpa, philosophy and religious literature. The collection supports research into manuscript transmission and regional intellectual history, but every textual claim requires an identified manuscript or reliable edition rather than a collection-wide assumption.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-140","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-140","src":"https://www.jkk.artandculture.rajasthan.gov.in/content/ArtandCulture/en/rajasthan-oriental-research-institute/manuscript/introduction.html","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"lat":26.29677,"lng":73.03514,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sarnath Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Sarnath","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"Sarnath combines Buddhist teaching traditions with surviving stupas, monastic remains and archaeological collections. Its monuments and excavated sculpture provide a material setting for studying the transmission of Buddhism, religious patronage and the development of sacred architecture.","full":"The Uttar Pradesh government's Sarnath account identifies the site with the Buddha's first teaching after enlightenment and describes the Dhamek and Chaukhandi stupas, Dharmarajika remains and the location where the Ashokan lion capital was found. These physical remains connect teaching traditions with architecture, sculpture and inscriptions. Studying their individual phases can illuminate how a place of instruction became a layered landscape of pilgrimage and artistic production.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-141","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-141","src":"https://nri.up.gov.in/hi/article/sarnath","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":25.37763,"lng":83.0276,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kushinagar Buddhist Heritage Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Kushinagar","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"Kushinagar is associated in Buddhist tradition with the Buddha's final teaching, death and cremation. Its stupa remains and excavated antiquities provide a setting for studying commemorative architecture, pilgrimage and the material history of Buddhist communities.","full":"The district administration's cultural account places the Buddha's final teaching and Mahaparinirvana at Kushinagar and associates Ramabhar with his cremation and a commemorative stupa. It also notes descriptions by Chinese pilgrims and antiquities recovered through excavation. These different kinds of evidence support study of Buddhist memory and monument building, with textual traditions, travellers' accounts and excavated materials requiring separate evaluation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-142","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-142","src":"https://kushinagar.nic.in/culture-heritage/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":26.73936,"lng":83.89023,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kaushambi Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Gadhva","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"Kaushambi is an excavated ancient urban site investigated by the University of Allahabad. The university's research connects its remains with the history of urbanisation in the Ganga valley, offering material evidence for settlement and social change.","full":"The University of Allahabad identifies Kaushambi as a centrally protected archaeological site where its excavation programme began under G. R. Sharma in 1948. It describes those investigations as significant for understanding urban development in the Ganga valley after the Harappan period. The department also preserves Kaushambi antiquities, including early historic terracotta figures, making the site relevant to the study of urban life and the production and use of images.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-143","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-143","src":"https://www.allduniv.ac.in/faculties/ancient-history","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":25.34023,"lng":81.39117,"approx":false},
{"name":"Fatehpur Sikri","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Fatehpur Sikri","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"Fatehpur Sikri preserves an ensemble of buildings from a Mughal imperial capital. Its related monuments and religious spaces offer evidence for studying coordinated urban planning, court architecture and the organisation of a short-lived royal centre.","full":"UNESCO identifies Fatehpur Sikri as a capital established by Emperor Akbar during the later sixteenth century and occupied as the imperial seat for approximately a decade. The surviving ensemble includes monuments, temples and the Jama Masjid in a coordinated architectural setting. This makes the site useful for studying the relationship between political authority, religious institutions and architectural planning, while detailed accounts of its infrastructure require building-specific documentation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-144","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-144","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/255/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":27.09724,"lng":77.66523,"approx":false},
{"name":"Taj Mahal Complex","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"New Delhi","state":"Delhi","about":"The Taj Mahal complex combines a marble mausoleum with carefully organised architectural spaces and ornamental work. Its inlay, lattice screens and balanced planning provide concrete material for studying geometry, stone craftsmanship and Mughal funerary design.","full":"UNESCO describes the Taj Mahal's balanced plan, central octagonal chamber, marble lattice screen and floral inlay made with precious and semiprecious stones. Its account also distinguishes the marble tomb from the red-sandstone mosque and guest house, and notes the use of brick and lime mortar beneath stone finishes. These details make the complex relevant to the history of design, masonry, surface treatment and collaborative architectural craftsmanship.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-145","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-145","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":28.60476,"lng":77.22348,"approx":false},
{"name":"Agra Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Agra","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"Agra Fort encloses Mughal palaces, audience spaces and mosques within red-sandstone fortifications. Its connected buildings offer evidence for studying the organisation of an imperial city and the relationship between defensive construction and ceremonial architecture.","full":"UNESCO describes Agra Fort as a Mughal fortress enclosing an imperial city within substantial red-sandstone walls. The ensemble includes the Jahangir Palace, Khas Mahal, audience halls such as the Diwan-i-Khas, and mosques. These linked spaces support study of how residence, governance and religious activity were arranged inside a fortified centre, with its successive building phases providing a basis for examining changes in courtly architectural practice.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-146","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-146","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/251/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":27.17983,"lng":78.02082,"approx":false},
{"name":"Rampur Raza Library","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Rampur Raza Library preserves manuscripts, paintings and books associated with Indo-Islamic learning and artistic culture. Its collections offer a route into the study of textual transmission, collecting practices and the relationship between written knowledge and visual art.","full":"The Ministry of Culture describes the Rampur Raza Library as a repository of Indo-Islamic learning, arts and religious works. It identifies paintings, palm-leaf manuscripts and printed books among its holdings. The collection is relevant to the history of reading, copying and preservation, while specific claims about scientific or philosophical texts need manuscript identifiers and catalogue descriptions rather than assumptions based only on the institution's broad scope.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-147","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-147","src":"https://culture.gov.in/ministry/our-groups/libraries-archives","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Saraswati Bhawan Library","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"Varanasi","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"Saraswati Bhawan Library preserves Sanskrit manuscripts alongside a separate printed-book collection. Its manuscript buildings, catalogues and copying facilities support research into the transmission of Sanskrit learning and the preservation of handwritten texts.","full":"The university identifies Saraswati Bhawan as a library originating with the Government Sanskrit College and describes the historic building as reserved for manuscripts. It also reports later preservation accommodation and facilities for microfilming and photocopying. The page links subject catalogues, making the institution a practical starting point for locating Sanskrit textual witnesses and comparing manuscript traditions, with actual content and readings requiring examination of the identified items.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-148","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-148","src":"https://www.ssvv.ac.in/about-library","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"lat":25.33053,"lng":82.99901,"approx":false},
{"name":"Government Museum, Mathura","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"Government Museum, Mathura preserves sculptures, terracottas, bronzes, paintings, pottery and coins. Its archaeological and artistic collections provide material for studying image making, ceramic production and the relationships between objects, historical communities and regional art.","full":"The Uttar Pradesh government's museum directory identifies the Government Museum at Dampier Nagar as an archaeological museum with sculpture, terracotta, bronze, cloth paintings, miniature paintings, pottery and coins. It also records a library, publications and educational activities. This combination makes it useful for comparing different materials and forms of historical evidence, with detailed interpretations of religious imagery or workshop practice depending on the museum's object catalogues.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-149","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-149","src":"https://nri.up.gov.in/en/page/museums","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"State Museum, Lucknow","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Lucknow","state":"Uttar Pradesh","about":"State Museum, Lucknow holds archaeological collections relevant to the study of sculpture, terracotta, coins and inscriptions. ASI's historical acquisition reports document objects from sites including Kaushambi and Ahichchhatra, connecting the museum with regional archaeological research.","full":"ASI's 1961–62 review records acquisitions at the State Museum, Lucknow including stone sculptures, terracottas, beads, coins, seals and sealings. It names a terracotta toy cart, figurine fragments from Kaushambi and Ahichchhatra, and an inscribed seal among the material reported. Such documented collections support research into image making, small-object manufacture and historical systems of marking, while a historical acquisition report does not establish which objects are currently displayed.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-150","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-150","src":"https://nmma.nic.in/nmma/NAS1/nmma_doc/IAR/Indian%20Archaeology%201961-62%20A%20Review.pdf","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":26.84537,"lng":80.95504,"approx":false},
{"name":"Katarmal Sun Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Katarmal","state":"Uttarakhand","about":"Katarmal is a Surya temple complex near Almora, also known as Bara Aditya. It provides a regional example of Himalayan temple architecture and offers a setting for studying sacred building traditions and the spatial organisation of Sun worship.","full":"The Almora district administration identifies Katarmal as a temple dedicated to Surya, also called Bara Aditya, in the hills near Almora. Uttarakhand Tourism describes a principal Sun shrine with additional temples dedicated to other deities. The ensemble supports study of temple grouping and regional sacred architecture, while the presence of Sun worship alone does not establish an astronomical observing function or a measured solar alignment.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-151","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-151","src":"https://almora.nic.in/tourist-place/katarmal-sun-temple/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":29.63232,"lng":79.61456,"approx":false},
{"name":"Jageshwar Temple Complex","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Jageshwar","state":"Uttarakhand","about":"Jageshwar is a group of stone temples near the Jata Ganga stream, with sculpture and inscriptions preserved across the complex. It provides evidence for studying Himalayan temple building, religious patronage and communication through carved stone.","full":"Uttarakhand Tourism describes Jageshwar as a large stone-temple cluster containing images of Shiva, Parvati and other deities. It notes inscriptions on walls and pillars and an ASI museum displaying associated sculpture. The combination of standing architecture, carved images and written records is useful for studying patronage and changing sacred space, while the different components need individual dating rather than a single age assigned to the whole ensemble.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-152","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-152","src":"https://uttarakhandtourism.gov.in/destination/jageshwar","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":29.63748,"lng":79.85434,"approx":false},
{"name":"Archaeological Museum, Gurukula Kangri","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Bihar","about":"The Gurukula Kangri museum brings together archaeological material, sculpture, coins, paintings and manuscripts. Its galleries connect the study of ancient Indian culture with university teaching, regional investigation and the preservation of objects in different materials.","full":"The university museum account describes galleries for protohistory, stone sculpture, terracottas, coins, metal images, paintings, arms and manuscripts. Its manuscript section includes texts in several languages and on different writing materials, while the archaeological holdings include finds from Haridwar and other regions. The institution provides a setting for comparing textual and material evidence, and for examining how archaeological collections are used in the teaching of cultural history.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-153","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-153","src":"https://kgcd.gkv.ac.in/museum/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ramman of Saloor-Dungra","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":null,"about":"Ramman combines ritual, recitation, songs and masked performance in the Garhwal Himalayas. Organised through community roles, it transmits local narratives and religious knowledge through performance, bringing oral and written traditions into an annual village event.","full":"UNESCO describes Ramman as a festival honouring Bhumiyal Devta in the twin villages of Saloor-Dungra. It includes a version of the Rama epic, legends, songs and masked dances, with responsibilities allocated among community and occupational groups. The practice connects storytelling, music, costume and ritual expertise, while its transmission depends on the participation of people who hold distinct responsibilities within the community.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-154","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-154","src":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/ramman-religious-festival-and-ritual-theatre-of-the-garhwal-himalayas-india-00281?RL=00281","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Govind Ballabh Pant Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Govind Ballabh Pant Museum preserves archaeological and artistic collections including sculpture, terracotta, paintings, coins and metal objects. These holdings provide a starting point for studying Kumaon through documented museum objects and regional material culture.","full":"The Ministry of Culture directory identifies this Almora government museum and objects associated with the Katyuri and Chand rulers. Its woodwork, textiles, copper plates and other collections support comparison of regional artistic practices, subject to item-level catalogue verification.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-155","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-155","src":"https://culture.gov.in/files/inline-documents/Directory_of_Indian_Museums_080620231.pdf","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Qutb Minar and its Monuments","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Delhi","state":"Delhi","about":"The Qutb complex contains a masonry minaret, mosques, tombs, carved architectural elements and an iron pillar. Its structures provide material evidence for studying construction, inscriptions and the adaptation of building traditions in the Delhi Sultanate.","full":"UNESCO describes the Qutb ensemble's minaret, mosques, funerary buildings and the Alai Darwaza gateway. Its account highlights carved pillars, inscriptional bands and the reuse of earlier architectural materials, alongside the iron pillar within the courtyard. These elements support study of building technology, epigraphy and architectural exchange, while claims about the pillar's metallurgy require technical studies beyond the general heritage description.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-156","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-156","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/233","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":28.52441,"lng":77.18545,"approx":false},
{"name":"Humayun's Tomb Complex","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Delhi","state":"Delhi","about":"Humayun's Tomb combines a monumental mausoleum with a four-part garden, pools and channels. Its octagonal planning, double dome and stone finishes offer material for studying Mughal design, garden water arrangements and architectural collaboration.","full":"UNESCO describes the garden-tomb as a collaboration between Persian and Indian craftsmen, set in a charbagh with pools linked by channels. The mausoleum has an octagonal arrangement, vaulted spaces, a double dome and red-sandstone cladding with contrasting marble. These features connect geometry and construction with garden planning, providing a documented example of knowledge exchange in the built environment rather than a claim of a single isolated tradition.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-157","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-157","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/232/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":28.59301,"lng":77.24827,"approx":false},
{"name":"Red Fort Complex","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Delhi","state":"Delhi","about":"The Red Fort was planned as the palace-fort of Shahjahanabad, with pavilions joined by a continuous water channel. Its architecture connects defensive enclosure, gardens, residential space and Mughal traditions of design and craftsmanship.","full":"UNESCO identifies the Red Fort as Shah Jahan's palace-fort and describes private pavilions linked by the Nahr-i-Behisht water channel. It explains the design as a fusion of Persian, Timurid, Hindu and wider Mughal architectural traditions and notes later changes under British occupation. The ensemble therefore supports study of water and garden planning, architectural exchange and the adaptation of a political centre through successive periods.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-158","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-158","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/231","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":28.65739,"lng":77.24127,"approx":false},
{"name":"National Archives of India","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"New Delhi","state":"Delhi","about":"The National Archives of India preserves public records documenting administration, society and technological change. Its holdings support research into the recorded history of land, resources and institutions, with access through catalogues and the archive's research arrangements.","full":"The National Archives states that it holds public records acquired from government record-creating agencies, with regular series from the eighteenth century onward. Its account identifies evidence relevant to political and administrative history, socio-economic history, and scientific and technological progress. These collections can support contextual research on how knowledge, resources and practices were documented or governed, provided a researcher identifies specific files and examines their institutional perspective.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-159","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-159","src":"https://www.nationalarchives.nic.in/en/public-records-holdings/public-records","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"lat":28.61529,"lng":77.21761,"approx":false},
{"name":"National Crafts Museum & Hastkala Academy","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Delhi","state":"Delhi","about":"The National Crafts Museum & Hastkala Academy collects objects connected with India's continuing craft and weaving practices. Its collections offer material evidence for studying regional techniques, design and the relationship between craft objects and the people who make them.","full":"The museum's institutional account emphasises practising craft and weaving traditions and describes a collection drawn from states and union territories across India. Its galleries include textiles, while the site also documents a village complex and craft-related displays. These holdings support comparative study of regional materials and making, with the knowledge connection strongest when objects are interpreted alongside named makers, communities and documented techniques.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-160","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-160","src":"https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":28.61391,"lng":77.2423,"approx":false},
{"name":"Tabo Monastery","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Tabo","state":"Himachal Pradesh","about":"Tabo Monastery preserves a connected ensemble of temples, chortens, wall paintings and stucco images. Its architecture and artistic programme provide evidence for studying Buddhist visual instruction, religious space and the material traditions of the western Himalayas.","full":"Himachal Tourism describes Tabo as a monastic complex with nine temples, chortens and rooms associated with monastic life. It highlights wall paintings and stucco statues, as well as a newer chamber used for daily prayers and chanting. These elements bring architecture, imagery and continuing practice together, supporting study of how Buddhist ideas were communicated in built and visual forms while also showing that the complex contains different phases.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-163","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-163","src":"https://himachaltourism.gov.in/destination/spiti-valley/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":32.09365,"lng":78.38279,"approx":false},
{"name":"Key Monastery","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"Key Monastery combines interconnected prayer chambers with murals, thangkas, manuscripts and musical instruments. It remains a place of religious training, linking Buddhist textual learning with performance and the architectural organisation of a hillside monastery.","full":"The Lahaul and Spiti district administration describes Key as a network of rooms, corridors, stairways and prayer chambers on a hill above Kaza. It identifies religious training alongside murals, thangkas, manuscripts, stucco images and wind instruments used during Chham performances. The monastery therefore provides a setting for examining the relationship between texts, visual art, ritual sound and the spaces in which monastic knowledge is taught and practised.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-164","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-164","src":"https://hplahaulspiti.nic.in/tourist-place/key-monastery/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Masroor Rock-cut Temples","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"Masroor consists of rock-cut shrines with towers facing a rectangular pool. The ensemble offers evidence for studying carving as a form of architectural construction and the organisation of a temple group within a shared rock mass.","full":"Himachal Tourism describes Masroor as a group of fifteen rock-cut temples in the Kangra region, with an eastward orientation and a rectangular pool in the courtyard. It identifies shrines associated with Shiva, Vishnu and figures from the Rama tradition. Their combined form supports study of rock excavation, sculptural planning and regional temple design, with the relationship between individual shrines best established through measured architectural documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-165","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-165","src":"https://himachaltourism.gov.in/destination/temples/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kangra Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Kaswara","state":"Himachal Pradesh","about":"Kangra Fort preserves a fortified site associated with the Katoch rulers and subsequent powers. Its surviving foundations, later additions and earthquake damage provide material for studying architectural adaptation and the changing uses of a Himalayan defensive centre.","full":"The district administration describes Kangra Fort as an ASI-protected monument with Katoch foundations and later Mughal additions, including mosque remains. It records successive political control and severe damage in the 1905 earthquake, after which the military occupation ended. These layered remains offer a basis for examining defensive architecture, reuse and structural vulnerability, while the physical evidence should be distinguished from legendary accounts of the dynasty's origins.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-166","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-166","src":"https://hpkangra.nic.in/tourist-place/kangra-fort/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":32.0867,"lng":76.25334,"approx":false},
{"name":"Bhuri Singh Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":null,"about":"Bhuri Singh Museum preserves Pahari paintings, copper plates, manuscripts, stone inscriptions and craft objects. Its collections connect regional political records with image making, woodcarving, textiles and the artistic practices associated with Chamba.","full":"The museum's collection page names Pahari miniature paintings, fountain-stone slabs, copper plates, historical documents, coins, bronzes and manuscripts. It also identifies woodcarving, jewellery, textiles and Chamba Rumal among its holdings. This range supports the study of written records and crafted objects together, allowing questions about patronage, religious imagery and regional making to be pursued through the relevant catalogued pieces.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-167","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-167","src":"https://www.bhurisinghmuseumchamba.in/collection/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chamba Rumal Embroidery","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Chamba","state":"Himachal Pradesh","about":"Chamba Rumal is a pictorial embroidery tradition that uses silk thread and reversible stitching. Its relationship with miniature painting and drawn designs connects textile skill with narrative imagery, pattern transfer and the knowledge shared between designers and embroiderers.","full":"The Development Commissioner for Handicrafts describes Chamba Rumal as embroidery influenced by regional painting, with designs drawn or transferred onto cloth before stitching. Its account identifies untwisted silk floss, double satin stitch producing a reversible effect, and other stitches used for outlines and details. The craft's images draw on local life, nature and narrative traditions, making it relevant to both practical textile knowledge and visual storytelling.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-168","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-168","src":"https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Textile/Embroidery/Chamba_Rumal/ChambaRumalWebPage.html","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":32.69233,"lng":76.16316,"approx":true},
{"name":"Burzahom Neolithic Settlement","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"Burzahom preserves evidence of changing dwellings, tools, pottery and cultivation across several archaeological phases. Its pit dwellings and later structures provide a concrete basis for studying how communities adapted buildings and technologies in the Kashmir valley.","full":"The government submission to UNESCO describes Burzahom's transition from subterranean dwelling pits to structures built at ground level, alongside stone and bone tools and later pottery and metal objects. It also reports botanical evidence of wheat, barley and lentil cultivation. These remains support study of habitation, agriculture and making, while interpretations of exchange or movement through mountain regions need to be tied to the specific archaeological evidence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-169","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-169","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/5917/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Martand Sun Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"Martand is a ruined temple dedicated to Surya near Anantnag. Its courtyard, surviving carved elements and architectural remains provide a regional setting for studying sacred stone construction and the material expression of Sun worship in Kashmir.","full":"The Anantnag district administration identifies Martand as a Kashmiri Hindu temple dedicated to Surya. Its account draws attention to the courtyard, carved surfaces and surviving architectural forms. These remains offer a starting point for studying regional temple design and ornament, while a precise account of construction sequence, patronage and artistic influences requires architectural and inscriptional sources beyond the destination summary.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-170","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-170","src":"https://anantnag.nic.in/tourist-place/martand-sun-temple/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Avantiswami and Avantisvara Temple Ruins","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Jammu and Kashmir","about":"Awantipora preserves two protected ancient temple sites, Avantiswami and Avantisvara. Their surviving buildings and sculptural remains provide material for studying regional temple architecture and the historical built landscape associated with the town.","full":"ASI's list of centrally protected sites names Avantiswami and Avantisvara separately at Avantipura in Pulwama district. The district administration also identifies the town's remains of buildings and sculpture associated with King Awantivarman. Together these official descriptions establish a temple-heritage cluster for further architectural study; detailed reconstructions of plans, dates or carving techniques require site-specific archaeological documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-171","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-171","src":"https://asi.nic.in/admin/whatsnew/download/719","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Parihaspora Archaeological Remains","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Parihaspora","state":"Jammu and Kashmir","about":"Parihaspora preserves remains associated with a Buddhist stupa, monastery, chaitya and temple buildings. Its large-stone construction and mixed religious landscape provide material for examining architectural practice and the organisation of a historic settlement in Kashmir.","full":"The Baramulla district account identifies archaeological monuments at Parihaspora, including remains of a Buddhist stupa and monastery. It also reports Vishnu temples, a royal building and a chaitya from earlier excavation, highlighting construction in large stones. These categories provide a starting point for studying monumental stonework and different institutional spaces within one landscape, while their precise relationships require excavation plans and architectural evidence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-172","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-172","src":"https://baramulla.nic.in/tourist-place/parihaspora/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":34.15152,"lng":74.64382,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sri Pratap Singh Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Sri Pratap Singh Museum preserves archaeological sculpture, manuscripts, metalware, textiles and decorative arts. Its Kashmir collections connect stone carving with writing materials, shawl production and other practical or artistic traditions represented in historical objects.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism describes sculpture from Pandrenthan, Awantipura and Parihaspora alongside manuscripts written on birch bark and Kashmiri handmade paper. Other sections cover metalware, textiles, musical instruments, enamelware and papier-mache. The museum offers a setting for studying how materials were used to transmit texts and make objects, with catalogue research needed to establish the date, origin and technique of individual pieces.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-173","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-173","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/jammu-and-kashmir/srinagar/sri-pratap-singh-museum","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kashmir Papier-mache Craft","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Srinagar","state":"Jammu and Kashmir","about":"Kashmir papier-mache transforms paper-based pulp into shaped and decorated objects. Its preparation, moulding, reinforcement and painting stages combine material reuse with skilled surface treatment, making it a concrete example of transmitted craft knowledge.","full":"The official handicrafts account describes pulp made using scrap paper and other fibres, shaped with wooden or brass moulds and reinforced with cloth. Surfaces are smoothed with a chalk-and-glue preparation before decoration. These stages connect knowledge of fibre behaviour, binding, shaping and painted ornament, while the craft's products and working practices continue to vary between workshops and makers.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-174","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-174","src":"https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Paper/Paper_Crafts/kashmir_paper_mache/KashmirPaperMacheWebPage.html","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":34.07474,"lng":74.82044,"approx":true},
{"name":"Alchi Monastic Complex","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Ladakh","about":"Alchi preserves Buddhist wall paintings, woodcarvings and sculpture associated with artistic exchange across the western Himalayas. Its surviving visual programme provides material for studying religious imagery, architectural decoration and connections between Kashmir and Ladakh.","full":"Ladakh Tourism identifies Alchi as a surviving monastic complex with Indo-Kashmiri wall paintings, carved wood and sculpture. Its account explicitly connects the art with early trans-Himalayan Buddhist exchange. The complex therefore supports study of how artistic techniques and religious imagery moved between regions, with individual paintings and buildings requiring their own chronology and conservation history before detailed technical conclusions are drawn.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-175","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-175","src":"https://www.tourism.ladakh.gov.in/explore-experiences","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Lamayuru Monastery","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Lamayuru","state":"Ladakh","about":"Lamayuru is a historic monastery within Ladakh's Buddhist cultural landscape. Its continuing Yuru Kabgyat festival connects the built religious centre with recurring community practice, providing a route into the study of monastic space and transmitted ceremonial traditions.","full":"The Leh district administration identifies Lamayuru as an eleventh-century monastery and names its annual Yuru Kabgyat festival. The monastery's historical and continuing religious roles make it relevant to the study of Himalayan institutional landscapes and the relationship between architecture and ceremony. Detailed claims about teaching collections, individual paintings or ritual meanings require evidence from the monastery and specialist research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-176","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-176","src":"https://leh.nic.in/tourist-place/lamayuru/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":34.2829,"lng":76.77416,"approx":false},
{"name":"Hemis Monastery Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Hemis Monastery Museum preserves manuscripts, Buddhist thangkas, artefacts and weapons within a religious institution. Its holdings offer material for studying Buddhist textual and visual traditions alongside other objects associated with monastic life.","full":"The Ministry of Culture directory identifies Hemis Monastery Museum as a religious museum with manuscripts, Buddhist thangkas, artefacts and weapons. These holdings support study of monastic textual and visual culture, with individual works requiring catalogue-level dating and provenance.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-177","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-177","src":"https://culture.gov.in/files/inline-documents/Directory_of_Indian_Museums_080620231.pdf","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Central Institute of Buddhist Studies","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"Leh","state":"Ladakh","about":"The Central Institute of Buddhist Studies maintains teaching, library and manuscript-related facilities at Choglamsar. Its Tibetan, general and reference collections support Buddhist studies and the preservation and study of Ladakh's textual heritage.","full":"The institute's annual report describes its Choglamsar campuses and the Sherab Gyasched Tsal library, organised into general, Tibetan and reference sections. It also records manuscript resource and conservation centres supported by the National Mission for Manuscripts. These facilities connect teaching with the preservation and study of Buddhist, Tibetan and Ladakh-related texts, making the institute relevant to the continuing institutional transmission of knowledge.","branch":"veda","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-IKS-178","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-IKS-178","src":"https://cibs.ac.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/annual-report-2022-23-compressed.pdf","group":"Research institutions"},"lat":34.10479,"lng":77.59877,"approx":false},
{"name":"Leh Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Leh","state":"Ladakh","about":"Leh Palace is a multi-storeyed former royal residence built with earth, wood and stone. Its differentiated residential and service spaces provide material for studying Himalayan building practice and the organisation of a palace within Ladakh's historic urban landscape.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism describes Leh Palace as a Namgyal royal residence with upper residential quarters and lower spaces used for storage and stables. Its account identifies a combination of mud, wood, sand and stone, together with prayer rooms, chambers and murals. These features offer a basis for investigating local construction materials, vertical organisation and the integration of domestic, ceremonial and religious uses in a Himalayan palace.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-179","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-179","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/ladakh/leh/leh-palace","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":34.16595,"lng":77.5862,"approx":false},
{"name":"Rakhigarhi Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Narnaund","state":"Haryana","about":"Rakhigarhi preserves Harappan settlement evidence including houses, drainage, pottery, tools and inscribed seals. Its excavated materials provide concrete avenues for studying urban planning, craft production and the use of different materials within an ancient settlement.","full":"Haryana Tourism describes Rakhigarhi as an interconnected mound complex with a planned Harappan township, mud-brick and fired-brick houses, and drainage. Finds include pottery, blades, shell and terracotta bangles, semiprecious-stone beads, copper objects and inscribed seals. These remains support study of how settlement infrastructure and specialist making were organised, while precise functional interpretations and the chronology of particular deposits require excavation-level evidence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-180","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-180","src":"https://haryanatourism.gov.in/places_of_interest/ancient-site-of-rakhigarhi/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":29.29132,"lng":76.11074,"approx":false},
{"name":"Banawali Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Haryana","about":"Banawali preserves evidence of early Harappan, Harappan and later occupation, including planned houses, fortifications and varied craft objects. A terracotta plough model connects its material record with questions about agricultural tools and representations of working life.","full":"Haryana Tourism describes Banawali's successive cultural phases and a fortified township with a radial layout during the Harappan phase. Its reported finds include ceramics, beads, bangles, weights, inscribed seals, copper fish-hooks, charred barley and a terracotta plough model. This range offers evidence for studying planning, agriculture, measurement and craft production together, while models and isolated objects must be interpreted within their excavation contexts.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-181","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-181","src":"https://haryanatourism.gov.in/places_of_interest/ancient-site-of-banawali/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Agroha Archaeological Mound","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Haryana","about":"Agroha's archaeological mound preserves a fortified settlement with brick buildings, coins and religious remains. Its position on a historical trade route makes it useful for studying urban exchange, building practice and the material organisation of a regional centre.","full":"Haryana Tourism identifies Agroha as a settlement on a route connecting Taxila and Mathura and describes excavations revealing a fortified township. It reports baked-brick residential and community buildings, a Buddhist stupa, a Hindu temple and coin finds associated with the settlement. These features support questions about circulation, urban building and religious institutions, while political interpretations drawn from coins need numismatic and archaeological corroboration.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-182","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-182","src":"https://haryanatourism.gov.in/places_of_interest/ancient-mound-agroha/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kalayat Ancient Brick Temples","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Kalayat","state":"Haryana","about":"Kalayat preserves two ancient brick temples beside a pond, with carefully arranged carved brickwork. Their surviving fabric offers material evidence for studying early regional temple construction, ornament and the effects of later alterations on historic buildings.","full":"Haryana Tourism describes two surviving temples at Kalayat, built with carved bricks and fine mortar, and identifies their arrangement of bricks as a distinctive feature. It notes that one structure is better preserved while the other has undergone substantial modification. The pair is useful for studying how brick could carry both structural and ornamental roles, and for comparing surviving older fabric with later changes.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-183","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-183","src":"https://haryanatourism.gov.in/places_of_interest/ancient-bricks-temple/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":29.67106,"lng":76.29524,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kunal Archaeological Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Dholu","state":"Haryana","about":"Kunal preserves early settlement evidence including pits, wattle-and-daub buildings and later mud-brick construction. Its tools and ceramics provide material for studying changing habitation practices, cultivation and the development of craft skills in the region.","full":"Haryana Tourism describes archaeological phases at Kunal with early pits supporting wattle-and-daub huts, followed by changes in building practice including moulded mud-bricks. Its account identifies ceramic assemblages, bone tools, chalcedony microblades, copper arrowheads and fish-hooks, together with evidence of agriculture and domestication. These remains provide a basis for investigating how materials and techniques changed over time, with phase associations requiring confirmation from excavation reports.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-184","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-184","src":"https://haryanatourism.gov.in/places_of_interest/ancient-site-of-kunal/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":29.62172,"lng":75.65892,"approx":false},
{"name":"Archaeological Museum and Excavation Site, Rupnagar","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Bihar","about":"Rupnagar's archaeological museum presents finds from the nearby excavated settlement, ranging from Harappan to later periods. Its ceramics, seals, metal implements, coins and sculpture support study of material change within a long-occupied location.","full":"The district administration describes the Rupnagar museum as preserving finds from excavation near Ropar, including Harappan and Painted Grey Ware material and objects of later periods. Named exhibits include a steatite seal, copper and bronze implements, coins and the Vina Vadini image. The combined site-and-museum record supports study of settlement continuity, craft and visual culture, while the sequence should be reconstructed through excavation reports and catalogued objects.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-185","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-185","src":"https://rupnagar.nic.in/tourist-place/archeological-museum/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sanghol Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Punjab","about":"Sanghol Museum preserves and displays material recovered from local archaeological excavations. As a Punjab government institution associated with a settlement's excavated remains, it supports the study of regional material culture and how archaeological knowledge is presented to the public.","full":"The Fatehgarh Sahib district administration identifies Sanghol Museum as a unit of Punjab's Department of Cultural Affairs, Archaeology and Museums. It explains that the museum was created to preserve and display finds from excavations at Sanghol and to make the region's long cultural history accessible. The institution therefore provides a starting point for investigating local archaeology, with specific claims about sculptures, dates or manufacturing techniques requiring an object catalogue or excavation report.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-186","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-186","src":"https://fatehgarhsahib.nic.in/tourist-place/sanghol-museum/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Maharaja Ranjit Singh Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Bhattian Bet","state":"Punjab","about":"Maharaja Ranjit Singh Museum occupies a former royal residence and preserves paintings, manuscripts, coins and arms. These collections connect Sikh court history with material evidence for visual representation, documentary practice and the manufacture of historical objects.","full":"The Amritsar district account identifies the museum's Ram Bagh building as a former summer palace adapted for museum use. Its collections include paintings of court and camp, coins, manuscripts, arms and armour. These holdings support study of courtly representation and documentary culture alongside the design and production of material objects, while individual pieces need their own provenance and technical interpretation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-187","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-187","src":"https://amritsar.nic.in/tourist-place/maharaja-ranjit-singh-museum/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":30.95764,"lng":75.82287,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thathera Utensil-making of Jandiala Guru","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Jandiala","state":"Punjab","about":"The Thatheras of Jandiala Guru practise the shaping of brass and copper utensils through hammering and controlled heating. Their work transmits knowledge of metal behaviour, tools and form through the production of bowls, plates and larger vessels.","full":"UNESCO's inscription decision describes metal cakes flattened into sheets and hammered into curved shapes to make brass and copper utensils. Heating and shaping require careful temperature control using small wood-fired stoves with bellows. This craft provides a concrete example of knowledge transmitted through coordinated hand skills, tool use and observation of materials, with different vessel forms requiring decisions about thickness, curvature and joining.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-188","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-188","src":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/decisions/9.COM/10.19","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":31.56086,"lng":75.02761,"approx":true},
{"name":"Bhai Kahn Singh Nabha Library, Punjabi University","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"","state":"Punjab","about":"Punjabi University's library preserves manuscripts and special collections related to Punjab's history, languages and literature. Catalogues and digitisation activity make it a starting point for studying textual transmission, personal archives and regional intellectual life.","full":"The university's library page describes a collecting focus on Punjab history and culture, Punjabi language and literature, and Sikhism. It identifies personal collections containing books, newspapers, diaries and manuscripts, and links manuscript catalogues and digitisation activities. These holdings support research into how written knowledge circulated through authors, scholars and institutions, while individual texts need catalogue identifiers and careful assessment of their provenance.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-189","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-189","src":"https://www.punjabiuniversity.ac.in/pages/PagePunjabi.aspx?dsenc=Library","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Government Museum and Art Gallery, Chandigarh","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Chandigarh","state":"Chandigarh","about":"The Government Museum and Art Gallery preserves Gandhara sculpture, Pahari and Rajasthani miniature paintings, coins and metal images. Its collections offer material for comparing sculptural and painted traditions across regions through the study of objects and their documented histories.","full":"Chandigarh Tourism identifies Gandhara sculpture, Pahari and Rajasthani miniature paintings, stone images, coins and metal images among the museum's principal collections. The institution's stated interpretation and educational purposes connect the preservation of art with public learning. Its holdings support study of religious imagery, artistic materials and regional styles, with the strongest knowledge-history claims depending on individual object records and collection provenance.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-190","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-190","src":"https://chandigarhtourism.gov.in/museums/government_museum_art_gallery","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":30.74895,"lng":76.78744,"approx":false},
{"name":"Elephanta Caves","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Raigad","state":"Maharashtra","about":"Elephanta preserves rock-cut sanctuaries and large sculptural panels associated with Shiva on Gharapuri Island near Mumbai. Its pillared halls, sanctum and narrative reliefs document relationships between architectural planning, stone carving and religious imagery in western India.","full":"The Elephanta ensemble occupies an island with archaeological remains from several periods, while its principal rock-cut sanctuaries are associated with Shaiva worship. Cave 1 combines a pillared interior, a central shrine and large relief panels representing forms and narratives of Shiva, including the celebrated three-faced image. The arrangement of columns, entrances, sculptural subjects and a sanctum accessible from multiple sides offers material evidence for studying how stone-working skills and religious concepts were integrated within a single architectural composition.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-191","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-191","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/244/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":18.96352,"lng":72.93132,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kanheri Caves","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"R/C Ward","state":"Maharashtra","about":"Kanheri is a Buddhist rock-cut monastic complex within Mumbai's Sanjay Gandhi National Park. Its prayer spaces, residential caves, sculptures and inscriptions preserve evidence for monastic life, artistic work and the circulation of Buddhist ideas in western India.","full":"Kanheri brings together rock-cut spaces used for residence, meditation and collective Buddhist worship within the wooded hills of present-day Mumbai. Maharashtra Tourism identifies chaityas, Buddha and Bodhisattva sculptures, carvings and inscriptions among the features that document the complex's religious and artistic history. Examining these different kinds of evidence together supports study of monastic organisation, the practical shaping of living rock and the visual and written forms through which religious communities communicated their beliefs and recorded their presence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-192","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-192","src":"https://maharashtratourism.gov.in/cave/kanheri/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":19.22422,"lng":72.8847,"approx":false},
{"name":"Karla Caves","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Karla","state":"Maharashtra","about":"Karla is a Buddhist rock-cut complex near Lonavala, known for its chaitya hall and sculptural decoration. Donative inscriptions connect its religious architecture with monks, nuns, traders and other patrons, providing evidence for the social organisation of construction.","full":"Karla's rock-cut Buddhist spaces include a chaitya hall with a monolithic stupa and carved architectural decoration. The Ministry of Tourism's account also notes inscriptions recording donations from monks, nuns, traders, rulers and villages, making the complex relevant to the study of patronage as well as construction. These records allow its architectural forms to be considered alongside the economic relationships that supported monastic establishments and the ways communities documented gifts, resources and religious commitments in durable written form.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-193","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-193","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/maharashtra/pune/karla-caves","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":18.78374,"lng":73.47032,"approx":false},
{"name":"Bhaja Caves","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Maharashtra","about":"Bhaja's Buddhist rock-cut caves near Lonavala include a chaitya hall, monastic living spaces and carved reliefs. The ensemble offers evidence for early approaches to excavated religious architecture and the representation of human, animal and divine figures in stone.","full":"At Bhaja, a chaitya containing a stupa is accompanied by rock-cut residential spaces with small cells and verandas. Maharashtra Tourism describes reliefs depicting people, animals, musicians and religious subjects, together with architectural forms that recall timber construction. The relationship between the prayer hall and the simpler living quarters provides a concrete basis for examining monastic requirements, while the carved surfaces preserve evidence for the sculptors' visual vocabulary and their adaptation of structural forms to excavated stone architecture.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-194","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-194","src":"https://maharashtratourism.gov.in/cave/bhaja/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Daulatabad Fort (Devgiri)","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Daulatabad","state":"Maharashtra","about":"Daulatabad is a fortified hill complex also known as Devgiri, shaped by successive rulers in the Deccan. Its defensive planning and the twisting Andheri passage provide material evidence for the use of terrain, circulation and restricted access in military architecture.","full":"Daulatabad's fortifications occupy a commanding hill position and preserve the accumulated work of successive Deccan ruling powers. Maharashtra Tourism highlights the Andheri passage, a dark and winding route whose configuration formed part of the fort's defensive arrangements, alongside the complex's military architecture. The site can support investigation of how builders combined natural topography with controlled approaches and difficult internal circulation, while its changing political history provides context for alterations and reuse rather than a single, uniform construction tradition.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-195","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-195","src":"https://maharashtratourism.gov.in/fort/devgiri-daulatabad/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":19.94259,"lng":75.21286,"approx":false},
{"name":"Shaniwar Wada","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Pune","state":"Maharashtra","about":"Shaniwar Wada was the seat of the Peshwas in Pune. Surviving enclosure walls and fortified gateways, together with the recorded palace spaces and fountain, preserve evidence for the organisation of an urban political residence and its security arrangements.","full":"Shaniwar Wada served as the Peshwas' headquarters and formed a major political residence within Pune. The district administration describes named gateways, palace halls and a fountain, while explaining that the destructive fire of 1828 left the fortifying walls and substantial protective doors as prominent survivals. Reading those remains alongside the documented internal spaces helps distinguish the ceremonial and domestic functions of the residence from its defensive perimeter, offering a basis for studying urban palace planning and historical building practices.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-196","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-196","src":"https://pune.gov.in/en/tourist-place/shaniwarwada/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":18.51938,"lng":73.85536,"approx":false},
{"name":"Paithani weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Paithan","state":"Maharashtra","about":"Paithani is a handwoven silk textile tradition named after Paithan. Mulberry silk and zari are worked on simple looms using a tapestry technique, connecting the finished sari with detailed practical knowledge of yarn, pattern formation and manual weaving.","full":"The Development Commissioner for Handicrafts' compendium identifies mulberry silk yarn and zari as the principal materials of Paithani saris and fabrics. It describes production on pit or throw-shuttle frame looms without designing devices, using a tapestry weaving technique that requires substantial manual work. These material and technical details make Paithani relevant to studying how weavers translate an intended pattern into interlaced yarn, manage different threads and preserve specialised design knowledge through the repeated practice of making cloth.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-197","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-197","src":"https://handicrafts.nic.in/CmsUpload/12222017102212GI%20BOOK%20FINAL%202-5-17_resized.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":19.58356,"lng":75.43026,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mahadeva Temple, Tambdi Surla","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Goa","about":"The Mahadeva Temple at Tambdi Surla is a small basalt temple dedicated to Shiva in Goa's forested interior. Its carved columns, deity panels, sanctum and pillared hall preserve evidence for regional stone building and sculptural traditions associated with Kadamba-period heritage.","full":"The Goa government describes Tambdi Surla's Mahadeva temple as a compact building of carved grey-black basalt with an east-facing sanctum and a small pillared mandapa. Panels show Shiva, Vishnu and Brahma with their consorts, while the hall contains a Nandi and a column base bearing a dynastic emblem. Its scale, slab roofing and carved architectural members offer tangible evidence for studying the selection and working of stone and the relationship between structural arrangement and religious imagery in Goa.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-199","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-199","src":"https://www.goa.gov.in/places/tambdi-surla-temple/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Goa State Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Goa","about":"Goa State Museum presents antiquities and cultural objects through thematic displays on Goan history and society. Its culture gallery includes traditional musical instruments, lamps and representations of local dress, offering material starting points for the study of regional practices.","full":"The Directorate of Museums defines Goa State Museum's purpose as collecting, preserving and thematically exhibiting antiquities, art objects and other materials relevant to Goa's history and culture. Its Glimpses of Goan Culture display includes lamps, traditional musical instruments, models of regional dress and a representation of the Tambdi Surla temple. Such exhibits provide entry points to the study of everyday material practices, performance and regional religious architecture, while the museum setting makes questions of selection, interpretation and conservation equally relevant.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-200","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-200","src":"https://museum.goa.gov.in/glimpses-of-goan-culture/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Goa Chitra Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Benaulim","state":"Goa","about":"Goa Chitra is an ethnographic museum focused on Goa's agrarian past, with traditional farming tools, household implements and craft equipment. Objects collected by Victor Hugo Gomes are accompanied by information and oral histories gathered from community elders during the collecting process.","full":"Goa Chitra's institutional account describes agricultural implements including oil grinders, ploughing tools and clay utensils, displayed against the setting of a functioning farm. The collection extends across farming, fishery, weaving, pottery and other everyday occupations, with object narratives supplemented by oral histories gathered from community elders. This combination of tools and remembered use is relevant to understanding how knowledge of materials, cultivation and household production was put into practice, while also allowing the museum's interpretation of that knowledge to be examined critically.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-201","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-201","src":"https://goachitramuseums.com/index.php/goa-chitra/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":15.26482,"lng":73.94134,"approx":false},
{"name":"Udayagiri Caves, Vidisha","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Madhopur","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Udayagiri near Vidisha preserves Gupta-period rock-cut sanctuaries, sculptural imagery and inscriptions. The combination of carved religious forms and texts on the surrounding rock provides evidence for the interaction of patronage, religious concepts and stone-working practices in central India.","full":"The Vidisha district administration identifies Udayagiri as a group of rock-cut caves and sculptures associated with the Gupta period. Its description draws attention to Hindu imagery and inscriptions carved near the cave temples, while also noting conch-shaped inscriptions on the rocks. Studying architecture, sculpture and writing together makes the site useful for examining the public expression of religious and political ideas, the technical shaping of rock surfaces and the different kinds of evidence available for reconstructing a historical sacred landscape.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-202","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-202","src":"https://vidisha.nic.in/en/tourist-place/udaigiri-cave/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":23.53998,"lng":77.77198,"approx":false},
{"name":"Bhojeshwar Mahadev Temple, Bhojpur","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"Bhojpur's unfinished Shiva temple preserves large stone construction alongside architectural drawings cut into nearby rock. Carved masonry blocks and remains of lifting ramps make the site relevant to studying how builders connected design, material preparation and the practical organisation of temple construction.","full":"India's UNESCO Tentative List submission describes Bhojeshwar as an unfinished temple associated with Raja Bhoj, with a square sanctum, tall columns and a large monolithic lingam. The surrounding rocks bear drawings of plans and architectural members, including pillars, lintels and doorways, while unused carved blocks and earthen ramps survive nearby. These related remains provide unusually direct evidence for comparing proposed designs with built fabric and examining how architectural knowledge was communicated and implemented through quarrying, carving, lifting and assembly.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-203","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-203","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6733/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mandu historic architectural ensemble","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Mandu's hilltop ensemble includes palaces, mosques, gateways and water features associated with the medieval capital of Malwa. Buildings such as Jahaz Mahal, set between artificial lakes, provide evidence for the connection between architectural composition, landscape and courtly life.","full":"Mandu occupies a hilltop setting in the Vindhya ranges and preserves an extensive group of palaces, gateways, religious buildings and reservoirs. Madhya Pradesh Tourism describes Jahaz Mahal as a two-storeyed palace between Munj Talao and Kapur Talao, with open pavilions, terraces and balconies over the water. Considering these buildings alongside their water settings supports study of architectural planning, landscape design and regional court culture, while other monuments such as Jami Masjid and Hoshang Shah's tomb document different functions within the historic settlement.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-204","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-204","src":"https://www.mptourism.com/destination-mandu.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Gwalior Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Gwalior","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Gwalior Fort occupies a sandstone hill above the city and contains palaces, temples and rock-cut Jain images. Its varied buildings and sculptural surfaces document several regional approaches to fortification, sacred architecture, decorative treatment and the reuse of an established hilltop complex.","full":"The Gwalior fort complex combines a fortified hilltop setting with religious and palatial structures rather than a single building type. Madhya Pradesh Tourism describes rock-cut Jain images along its approach, the carved Sas Bahu temples, Teli ka Mandir and the coloured animal decoration of Man Singh Palace. The range of materials, carved surfaces and architectural arrangements supports comparison of different working traditions within one landscape, while successive additions make the site useful for examining how established defensive and ceremonial spaces were adapted over time.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-205","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-205","src":"https://www.mptourism.com/destination-gwalior.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":26.2236,"lng":78.16654,"approx":false},
{"name":"Bagh Caves and murals","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"The Bagh Caves are Buddhist rock-cut monuments beside the Baghni River, with surviving evidence of mural painting. Monastic spaces, carved architecture and painted scenes offer a basis for studying the preparation of surfaces, visual storytelling and regional Buddhist artistic practice.","full":"India's Tentative List submission describes Bagh as a group of Buddhist caves excavated in sandstone on the southern slopes of the Vindhyas. Residential cells, assembly spaces, verandas and shrines were accompanied by murals, with Cave 4 known as Rang Mahal receiving particular attention for its architectural and artistic features. The surviving paintings include religious, courtly and performative subjects, enabling study of narrative composition and social imagery alongside plaster preparation, pigment use and the relationship between painted surfaces and monastic architecture.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-206","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-206","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/7043/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Gond painting of Patangarh","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Bajag Tahsil","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Patangarh is associated with Gond painting, whose subjects draw on nature, stories and community life. Artists construct forms through detailed patterns of lines and dots, while work on paper and canvas extends a practice also associated with domestic wall painting.","full":"Madhya Pradesh Tourism's account of Patangarh describes artists developing images through an outline, a chosen infill pattern and the addition of colour. Nature, myths and legends supply subjects, while repeated lines and dots give individual forms their visual texture and rhythm. The movement from house walls to portable paper and canvas makes this tradition relevant to both inherited visual knowledge and contemporary artistic decisions, including how artists adapt familiar motifs, materials and working sequences for changing audiences and livelihoods.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-207","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-207","src":"https://www.mptourism.com/patangarhs-gond-paintings.html","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":22.75112,"lng":81.47206,"approx":true},
{"name":"Maheshwari handloom weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Maheshwar","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Maheshwar is a handloom centre associated with Maheshwari saris and fabrics. Striped or checked bodies, patterned borders and pallus reflect specialised weaving knowledge, while continuing production links the town's textile heritage with present-day artisan work and changing choices of fibre.","full":"Madhya Pradesh Tourism describes Maheshwari weaving as a textile tradition associated with the patronage of Ahilyabai Holkar in the eighteenth century. Its account identifies striped and checked layouts, zari borders and motifs such as waves, mat patterns and cotton flowers, with silk, cotton and blended fabrics used in current production. These features support study of how regional design vocabularies are organised within a woven structure and how weavers adapt inherited patterns and material combinations while maintaining a recognisable textile identity.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-208","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-208","src":"https://www.mptourism.com/handloom-heritage-of-madhya-pradesh.html","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":22.17743,"lng":75.58735,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sirpur archaeological ensemble","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Rajasthan","about":"Sirpur on the Mahanadi preserves temple and Buddhist monastic remains within a historic settlement landscape. Brick architecture, decorative carving and excavated religious spaces provide evidence for regional building practices and the interaction of Hindu and Buddhist communities in central India.","full":"Sirpur's archaeological landscape includes the brick-built Laxman Temple, the Baleshwar Mahadev and Surang Tila temple remains, and the excavated Tiwardev Buddhist monastery. A government heritage-tour account describes decorative elements and sculptural craftsmanship at Laxman Temple and the structural remains of the monastery. Taken together, these different religious establishments provide a concrete basis for comparing construction materials, architectural arrangements and visual programmes, while the settlement context supports investigation of cultural exchange and the coexistence of religious institutions along the Mahanadi.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-209","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-209","src":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2310030&lang=2&reg=48","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Tala Devrani-Jethani temple site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":null,"about":"Tala is an archaeological temple site near the Maniari River, known for the Devrani-Jethani remains and sculptural carving. Its ruined structural members and excavated images provide evidence for regional stone-working, temple composition and the visual expression of Shaiva traditions.","full":"The Bilaspur district account identifies Tala with the Devrani-Jethani temples and describes carved pillars, entrance decoration and architectural spaces including the sanctum, vestibule and hall. Archaeological finds and surviving sculptural work are central to its presentation of the site's religious history. These remains allow the study of carved architectural members in relation to temple layout, while also demonstrating why fragmentary fabric and excavated images need to be documented together before reconstructing the appearance, use or development of a ruined sacred complex.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-210","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-210","src":"https://bilaspur.gov.in/en/tourist-place/tala/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Bhoramdeo Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Bodla Tahsil","state":"Chhattisgarh","about":"Bhoramdeo is a Shiva temple near Kawardha, with an east-facing plan and a carved pillared hall. Its entrances and sculpted structural members offer evidence for regional temple construction and the integration of architectural support with decorative stone-working in Chhattisgarh.","full":"The Kabirdham district administration identifies Bhoramdeo as a Shiva temple near Kawardha and describes its east-facing orientation and three entrances leading into the hall. The hall's central and peripheral pillars carry carved decoration and support the roof, making the relationship between structure and ornament especially relevant. The temple provides a material starting point for studying how regional builders organised approach, assembly and worship spaces, and how sculptors worked architectural members within an active religious setting in central India.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-211","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-211","src":"https://kawardha.gov.in/en/tourist-place/bhoramdev-templekawardha/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":22.11544,"lng":81.14826,"approx":false},
{"name":"Bastar Dhokra metal casting","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Kondagaon","state":"Chhattisgarh","about":"Bastar Dhokra is a non-ferrous metal craft using the lost-wax casting process. Its continuing practice connects knowledge of modelling and metal casting with regional forms, while contemporary design projects show how the technique is applied to new functional and sculptural objects.","full":"The Ministry of Culture's craft publication identifies Dhokra from Bastar as a non-ferrous metal casting practice using the lost-wax technique. It also presents a contemporary Dhokra Stambh in cast bronze, composed of four basket-like units whose surfaces and forms can be rearranged for use as stools. This documented example connects a continuing casting tradition with new design decisions, providing a basis for examining the relationship between inherited making skills, surface texture, scale and the changing functions of crafted objects.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-212","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-212","src":"https://www.indiaculture.gov.in/sites/default/files/pdf/Sustain_The_Craft_Idiom_16012024.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":19.58858,"lng":81.66287,"approx":true},
{"name":"Vikramshila Mahavihara","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Bihar","about":"Vikramshila at Antichak preserves the remains of a Buddhist centre of learning associated with the Pala period. A brick stupa within a square monastery provides evidence for the spatial relationship between collective residence, religious study and ritual movement in a monastic institution.","full":"Bihar State Tourism describes Vikramshila as a Buddhist centre of learning founded under the Pala ruler Dharmapala at present-day Antichak. Its account of the ruins identifies a central brick stupa laid in mud mortar within a square monastery, with terraces and paths for circumambulation. The surviving plan offers evidence for examining how a learning institution combined residential and ritual spaces, while its association with Buddhist scholarship makes the archaeological remains relevant to the history of organised teaching and intellectual exchange.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-213","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-213","src":"https://bstdc.bihar.gov.in/vikramshila.htm","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Barabar and Nagarjuni cave ensemble","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Makhdumpur","state":"Bihar","about":"The Barabar and Nagarjuni hills preserve a related group of rock-cut caves associated with Mauryan patronage and the Ajivika community. Excavated chambers and dedicatory records provide evidence for early stone-working, religious endowment and the shaping of spaces within natural rock formations.","full":"The Bihar government's cave initiative identifies four caves on Barabar Hill and three on nearby Nagarjuni Hill, associating their patronage with Ashoka and Dasaratha and their dedication with the Ajivikas. The individual chambers and inscriptions preserve material and written evidence for early rock excavation and religious gifts. Treating the two hills as one related ensemble allows their shared context to be explored while retaining the differences between individual caves, patrons and stages of work that archaeological and epigraphic study can establish.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-214","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-214","src":"https://barabarcavetourism.bihar.gov.in/About","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":25.00558,"lng":85.0633,"approx":false},
{"name":"Vaishali archaeological landscape","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Vaishali","state":"Bihar","about":"Vaishali's archaeological landscape includes Buddhist stupas, the lion pillar at Kolhua and the Abhishek Pushkarni tank. These different remains provide starting points for studying religious commemoration, stone working, settlement history and the relationship between ceremonial traditions and constructed water places.","full":"Bihar Tourism's Buddhist circuit brochure presents Vaishali through its stupas, the polished sandstone lion pillar at Kolhua and the Abhishek Pushkarni tank. It connects these places with Buddhist traditions and the history of the Lichchhavis, while noting excavated relic caskets and the preservation of archaeological finds in the local museum. The landscape supports comparative study of commemorative building, carved stone and ceremonial water places, provided that traditional accounts of events are distinguished from conclusions established through excavation or inscriptions.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-215","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-215","src":"https://tourism.bihar.gov.in/content/dam/bihar-tourism/e_brochures/buddhist/buddha-circuit-eng.pdf","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":25.74917,"lng":85.39722,"approx":true},
{"name":"Madhubani (Mithila) painting","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Madhubani","state":"Bihar","about":"Madhubani painting is a Mithila visual tradition associated with geometric patterning and the use of varied hand tools. Work formerly made on plastered domestic walls and floors now also appears on cloth, handmade paper and canvas, linking household practice with portable art.","full":"Bihar Tourism describes Madhubani painting as a practice traditionally carried out on freshly plastered mud walls and floors, with later work extending to cloth, handmade paper and canvas. Its account lists fingers, twigs, brushes and nib pens among the tools, together with natural dyes and pigments and characteristic geometric patterns. These details provide a basis for studying surface preparation, colour and mark-making knowledge, as well as the changes involved when a domestic visual tradition becomes portable and supports artisan livelihoods.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-216","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-216","src":"https://tourism.bihar.gov.in/en/experiences/art-and-craft/painting/madhubani-or-mithila-painting","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":26.35031,"lng":86.22964,"approx":true},
{"name":"Patna Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Danapur","state":"Bihar","about":"Patna Museum was established in 1917 to preserve historical material from Bihar. Its collection categories include archaeological objects, terracotta, sculpture, paintings, instruments and textiles, making it a repository for studying regional material culture and the ways that cultural evidence is collected and interpreted.","full":"Bihar Tourism records the establishment of Patna Museum in 1917 for historical objects found around Patna and describes a collection spanning archaeology, sculpture, terracotta, painting, textiles and instruments. These categories offer different kinds of evidence for artistic production, religious imagery and everyday material life rather than a single knowledge tradition. The institution is relevant as a place where objects can be compared through their materials, techniques and recorded find contexts, while changes in displays make current object locations an essential part of further research.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-217","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-217","src":"https://tourism.bihar.gov.in/en/destinations/patna/patna-museum","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":25.61265,"lng":85.13351,"approx":false},
{"name":"Khuda Bakhsh Oriental Public Library","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"Danapur","state":"Bihar","about":"Khuda Bakhsh Oriental Public Library preserves manuscripts and printed collections supporting research in history, literature, religion and Unani medicine. Established from a personal collection opened to the public in 1891, it documents South Asian intellectual traditions through texts in several languages and materials.","full":"The Ministry of Culture describes the library as a public institution that grew from Khuda Bakhsh's donated manuscript collection and opened in 1891. Its holdings include manuscripts on paper, palm leaf, cloth and other supports, with Arabic, Persian and Urdu among the principal textual languages. Research, publication and preservation activities cover fields including literature, comparative religion, history and Tibb or Unani medicine, making the library relevant to the study of intellectual exchange, manuscript production and the transmission of knowledge within South Asia.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-218","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-218","src":"https://culture.gov.in/khuda-baksh-oriental-public-library-patna","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"lat":25.61935,"lng":85.16258,"approx":false},
{"name":"Maluti temple village","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Jharkhand","about":"Maluti is a village of terracotta-decorated temples in Dumka district near the Bengal border. Narrative panels and early Bengali inscriptions connect its built heritage with regional artisan practice, religious imagery and the use of writing on temple surfaces in the settlement.","full":"The Jharkhand government describes Maluti as a temple village with terracotta work, including exterior panels depicting episodes from the Ramayana. Its account also identifies early Bengali inscriptions and the relationship of the village's temples with the artistic traditions of neighbouring Bengal. The cluster provides evidence for studying how brick and terracotta architecture carried religious stories and written information within a village setting, while its conservation history raises practical questions about maintaining decorated surfaces and groups of closely related buildings.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-219","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-219","src":"https://cm.jharkhand.gov.in/node/569","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sohrai-Khovar painting","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Hazaribagh","state":"Jharkhand","about":"Sohrai-Khovar painting comprises mural traditions associated with harvest and marriage in the Hazaribagh region. Women use locally available coloured soils to create lines, dots, plant forms and animal figures, connecting domestic surfaces with seasonal life, community practice and material knowledge.","full":"A Government of India GI brochure describes Sohrai-Khovar as mural art practised by women in the Hazaribagh area during harvest and marriage seasons. The account identifies locally available coloured soils as painting materials and a visual vocabulary of lines, dots, animal figures and plants, while noting that work now also appears beyond mud-house walls. These practices provide evidence for studying earth pigments, domestic surface decoration and the relationship between visual expression, seasonal events and the continuing transmission of community knowledge.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-220","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-220","src":"https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2021/nov/doc2021112441.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":24.0867,"lng":85.49122,"approx":true},
{"name":"Dr. Ramdayal Munda Tribal Welfare Research Institute Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Jharkhand","about":"The Tribal Museum at Ranchi's Dr. Ramdayal Munda institute uses dioramas to depict traditional livelihoods of Jharkhand's communities. It forms part of a research institution whose library, studies and cultural preservation activities provide context for interpreting the material and represented practices on display.","full":"The institute's official account identifies its Tribal Museum as a display of dioramas depicting the traditional livelihoods of thirty-two tribes of Jharkhand. The museum sits within a campus devoted to research, training, publication and the preservation of tribal art and culture, supported by a library and other institutional facilities. Its relevance lies in documenting and interpreting community practices and livelihoods, while the use of constructed scenes also invites examination of how museum representations select, arrange and communicate knowledge about living societies.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-221","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-221","src":"https://www.trijharkhand.in/en/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Bishnupur temple ensemble","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Bishnupur","state":"West Bengal","about":"Bishnupur's brick and laterite temples display several regional roof and tower arrangements. Single-tower, five-tower and joined hut-roof forms provide material evidence for how Bengal's builders combined local architectural vocabularies with the requirements of sanctums, porches and ritual circulation in temple ensembles.","full":"The UNESCO Tentative List entry submitted by the Archaeological Survey of India describes Bishnupur temples built in brick and laterite, including single-tower Eka-Ratna forms and the five-tower Shyam Rai temple. It also distinguishes the joined roof forms of Jor Bangle from Rasmancha's pyramidal roof and surrounding galleries. Comparing these arrangements offers a concrete basis for studying regional architectural classification, the translation of roof forms into masonry and the organisation of sanctum, porch and circulation within different temple designs.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-222","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-222","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/1087/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":23.07168,"lng":87.32678,"approx":false},
{"name":"Santiniketan educational and artistic landscape","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":null,"about":"Santiniketan embodies Rabindranath Tagore's experiment in education, art and communal life. Open-air teaching, buildings, landscapes and continuing festivals connect a modern institution with interpretations of Indian traditions, local materials and artistic exchanges across Asia and beyond in its educational setting.","full":"UNESCO describes Santiniketan as the residential school and artistic centre established by Rabindranath Tagore in 1901, with teaching beneath trees as part of an educational vision joining intellectual and aesthetic development. Buildings, gardens, artworks and festivals preserve evidence of experimentation with materials and with Indian and other Asian artistic traditions. The ensemble offers a specific example of how inherited ideas and local practices were reconsidered within a modern educational community rather than simply continued unchanged from an ancient institutional model.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-223","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-223","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1375/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Purulia Chhau dance","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Purulia","state":"West Bengal","about":"Purulia is one of the regional traditions of Chhau, a dance form that combines storytelling, martial movement and performance. Masks, rhythmic accompaniment and episodes from epics or folklore connect embodied training with the transmission of narratives through community and festival settings.","full":"UNESCO's account distinguishes the Purulia, Seraikella and Mayurbhanj styles of Chhau, noting that Purulia and Seraikella use masks. Chhau enacts epic, folkloric and abstract themes through movements related to martial practices, animal gaits and everyday activity, accompanied by reed instruments and drums. Purulia's participation in this shared tradition makes it relevant to studying embodied knowledge and narrative performance, while the regional distinction remains essential when examining masks, movement vocabulary, instruction and the social settings in which dancers learn and perform.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-224","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-224","src":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/chhau-dance-00337?lang=en","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":23.2021,"lng":86.3229,"approx":true},
{"name":"Bengal Patachitra scroll painting","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Pingla","state":"West Bengal","about":"Bengal Patachitra uses painted scrolls and images to communicate mythological, epic and other narratives. Multi-panel arrangements connect visual sequencing with storytelling, while the tradition's material forms preserve knowledge of how pictures organise and convey stories for different audiences across the region.","full":"The West Bengal government's arts account identifies scroll painting as a means of narrating oral and written epics, with both single images and long vertical multi-panel scrolls called Patachitra. The National Portal also describes work on cloth or paper, with mythology, folklore and contemporary themes among its subjects. This combination makes the tradition relevant to studying the organisation of narrative in images and the ways painters adapt a recognisable visual medium to inherited stories as well as changing social topics.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-225","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-225","src":"https://wb.gov.in/our-culture-arts-and-crafts.aspx","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":22.27276,"lng":87.60286,"approx":true},
{"name":"Udayagiri and Khandagiri Jain caves","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Udayagiri","state":"Odisha","about":"The twin hills of Udayagiri and Khandagiri contain rock-cut residences associated with Jain ascetics. Sculptural reliefs, excavated living spaces and the Hathigumpha inscription provide complementary evidence for architectural practice, religious patronage and written historical memory in the Bhubaneswar region of Odisha.","full":"Bhubaneswar's official heritage circuit describes the Udayagiri and Khandagiri caves as excavated residences for Jain recluses and identifies major features including Ranigumpha, Ganeshgumpha and Hathigumpha. The latter bears the inscription associated with Kharavela, linking a physical rock-cut setting with an important written record. Considering inscriptions, carved scenes and ascetic living spaces together supports study of how religious communities organised residence and how patrons and craftspeople used stone surfaces to convey narratives and preserve information within a shared sacred landscape.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-227","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-227","src":"https://visit.bhubaneswar.me/explore/heritage-circuits/monks-caves-kings-a-jain-circuit","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":18.08351,"lng":81.55136,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ratnagiri Buddhist monastery site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Odisha","about":"Ratnagiri preserves Buddhist monastic remains, carved entrances, sculptural panels and numerous votive stupas. Part of Odisha's Buddhist Diamond Triangle, the site offers material evidence for monastic learning, devotional practice and the skilled production of architecture and religious images in eastern India.","full":"Odisha Tourism presents Ratnagiri as a Buddhist centre associated with Mahayana and Vajrayana traditions and describes its monastery, sculpted entrances, panels and votive stupas. These surviving features bring together institutional spaces and smaller devotional constructions within one archaeological setting. Their relationship offers a basis for studying how monastic communities combined residence, teaching and worship, while the carved stonework allows questions of iconographic knowledge, artistic training and patronage to be examined through the individual objects and architectural members preserved at the site.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-228","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-228","src":"https://odishatourism.gov.in/en/places/ratnagiri-monastery","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Lalitgiri Buddhist archaeological complex","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Odisha","about":"Lalitgiri preserves Buddhist monasteries, stupas, chaitya remains and archaeological finds within Odisha's Diamond Triangle. Sculptures, inscriptions and relic caskets provide different kinds of evidence for religious practice, material craftsmanship and the development of monastic institutions in the region over time.","full":"Odisha Tourism identifies Lalitgiri with excavated monasteries, stupas and chaityas, together with sculptures, inscriptions and relic caskets recovered through archaeological work. Its position within the Diamond Triangle connects it geographically with Ratnagiri and Udayagiri without making the three sites one institution. The combination of buildings and portable finds is relevant to studying Buddhist ritual and monastic organisation, while the caskets and written evidence require careful attention to archaeological context before conclusions are drawn about the identities of people or relics associated with them.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-229","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-229","src":"https://odishatourism.gov.in/en/places/lalitagiri-buddhist-complex?p_l_back_url=%2Fen%2Ftourist-places%3Fdelta%3D60&p_l_back_url_title=Tourist+Places","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Raghurajpur craft village","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Raghurajpur","state":"Odisha","about":"Raghurajpur is a village of practising artists associated with Pattachitra painting and other crafts. Painted narratives, palm-leaf engraving, masks and toys connect household workshops with the transmission of visual skills, while Gotipua performance forms another part of the village's cultural life today.","full":"Odisha Tourism describes Raghurajpur as a working craft village where Pattachitra painting is accompanied by palm-leaf engraving, stone carving, wooden toys and mask making. Painted house walls and artist workshops place these practices within everyday village space, and Gotipua performance adds a related but distinct strand of cultural activity. The village offers a specific setting for examining how materials, narrative subjects and making techniques are taught and adapted within households, while direct artisan work remains central to the interpretation of its heritage.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-230","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-230","src":"https://odishatourism.gov.in/en/places/raghurajpur-craft-village","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":19.88537,"lng":85.82654,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pipili appliqué craft","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Pipili","state":"Odisha","about":"Pipili is associated with Chandua, an appliqué tradition that stitches coloured cloth into patterned objects. Its connections with Jagannath temple rituals and chariot decoration link textile making with ceremonial requirements, while contemporary products extend the craft into household furnishings and other uses.","full":"Odisha Tourism describes Pipili's Chandua work as the stitching of coloured cloth pieces into floral, animal, mythological and geometric designs. Canopies and umbrellas connect the practice with Jagannath temple rituals and Ratha Jatra chariot decoration, while lampshades, bags and cushion covers represent other applications. This range makes the craft useful for studying how cutting, joining and pattern organisation respond to both ceremonial specifications and changing markets, and how family-based making skills are maintained through repeated production and adaptation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-231","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-231","src":"https://odishatourism.gov.in/en/places/pipili?p_l_back_url=%2Fen%2Ftourist-places%3Fdelta%3D40%26start%3D2&p_l_back_url_title=Tourist+Places","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":20.08895,"lng":85.82359,"approx":true},
{"name":"Odisha State Museum manuscript collection","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"","state":"Odisha","about":"Odisha State Museum preserves manuscripts on palm leaf and other writing supports, including illustrated and unusually shaped examples. Its manuscript resource work connects the collection with the survey and documentation of regional holdings, offering evidence for texts, book-making and material preservation practices.","full":"The National Mission for Manuscripts describes the museum's holdings on palm leaf, bamboo leaf, paper, ivory and bark, alongside illustrated works and manuscripts shaped like fans, fish and other forms. Styluses and varied manuscript covers provide further evidence for the physical production and handling of written knowledge. The museum's Manuscript Resource Centre also undertakes survey and documentation of institutional and private collections, linking preserved objects with the wider task of locating, cataloguing and sustaining Odisha's manuscript heritage.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-232","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-232","src":"https://www.namami.gov.in/manuscript-resource-centres","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Assam State Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Guwahati","state":"Assam","about":"Assam State Museum preserves manuscripts, inscriptions, sculpture and other regional material evidence. Its manuscript gallery includes texts on Sanchipat, Tulapat and palm leaf, while stone and copper-plate inscriptions provide complementary sources for studying languages, writing practices and historical cultural life in Assam.","full":"The Directorate of Museums describes Assam State Museum's manuscript gallery as holding works in old Assamese, Tai, Burmese and other languages, written on Sanchipat, Tulapat and palm leaf. Some Assamese manuscripts include coloured illustrations, while the epigraphy gallery presents stone and copper-plate inscriptions from different historical periods. Together these holdings provide evidence for comparing scripts, writing materials and textual imagery with inscribed records, supporting research into regional knowledge transmission through both portable books and more durable forms of public or legal writing.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-233","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-233","src":"https://museums.assam.gov.in/portlets/galleries-of-assam-state-museum","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":26.18529,"lng":91.75238,"approx":false},
{"name":"Sualkuchi silk weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Sualkuchi","state":"Assam","about":"Sualkuchi is a weaving centre on the north bank of the Brahmaputra associated with Assam's silk textiles. Muga, pat and eri are connected with its textile economy, while garments such as mekhela-chadar link specialised handloom knowledge with regional clothing and exchange.","full":"The Kamrup District Census Handbook identifies Sualkuchi as a silk-weaving village and describes production associated with muga, pat and eri silk. It notes the importance of mekhela-chadar and gamosas among the products woven there, placing textile skills within an established settlement of looms and cottage industry. The location provides a focused setting for studying how knowledge of silk yarn and woven garment formats supports a regional craft economy, while changes in production should be documented against the handbook's historical reporting date.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-234","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-234","src":"https://kamrup.assam.gov.in/sites/default/files/public_utility/Census-2011-District_Census_Handbook_Part-XII-B.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":26.16991,"lng":91.57085,"approx":true},
{"name":"Samaguri Satra mask-making tradition","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Assam","about":"Samaguri Satra on Majuli is associated with mask making for Bhaona performance. Distinct forms and colours identify dramatic characters, connecting craft production with religious storytelling and instruction within the wider network of Assam's Vaishnava satras and their continuing cultural practice.","full":"The Majuli district administration identifies Samaguri Satra as a centre of mask-making knowledge transmitted through successive generations of satriya bhakats. Its account explains that differing mask forms and colours help audiences distinguish characters in Bhaona, locating the craft within the educational and cultural functions of Vaishnava satras. The tradition is therefore relevant to both object-making and performance: a mask's visual design becomes a practical means of communicating identity, narrative and religious ideas when used within an enacted story.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-235","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-235","src":"https://majuli.assam.gov.in/tourist-place-detail/274","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Rang Ghar royal pavilion","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Assam","about":"Rang Ghar is a two-storeyed Ahom royal pavilion associated with public recreation and traditional sports. Its octagonal base and roof recalling an inverted royal boat provide evidence for a distinctive architectural form linked with viewing, gathering and courtly cultural life in Assam.","full":"The Sivasagar district administration describes Rang Ghar as an Ahom pavilion built for royal and public enjoyment of recreational and sporting events. It records an octagonal base, two storeys, arched openings and a roof shaped like an inverted royal long boat with projecting makara forms. These features allow the building to be studied as a designed viewing structure rather than simply a fortified monument, connecting architectural form with the organisation of spectatorship, games and festive gatherings in the Ahom cultural landscape.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-236","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-236","src":"https://sivasagar.assam.gov.in/tourist-place-detail/276","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Tawang Monastery Centre for Buddhist Cultural Studies","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"Bhopal","state":"Madhya Pradesh","about":"Tawang Monastery maintains an educational institution known as the Centre for Buddhist Cultural Studies. The school connects monastic life with organised teaching for monks, providing a living setting for the transmission of Buddhist learning within a monastery founded in the seventeenth century.","full":"The Ministry of Culture identifies Tawang as a seventeenth-century monastic institution and describes the establishment of a school for educating its monks. The school began functioning in 1977 and was subsequently named the Centre for Buddhist Cultural Studies, with the ministry supporting the monastery through grants. This documented educational activity makes Tawang relevant as a continuing institution of knowledge transmission, allowing study of how monastic communities organise teaching and sustain religious learning within a historical architectural and social setting.","branch":"veda","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-IKS-237","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-IKS-237","src":"https://culture.gov.in/offering/grantee-bodies/tawang-monastery-arunachal-pradesh","group":"Research institutions"},"lat":23.23613,"lng":77.40094,"approx":true},
{"name":"Jawaharlal Nehru State Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Arunachal Pradesh","about":"Jawaharlal Nehru State Museum presents ethnographic and archaeological material from Arunachal Pradesh. Textiles, headgear, musical instruments, tools, religious objects and cane or wood crafts provide material starting points for studying the state's diverse community practices and the technologies embodied in everyday objects.","full":"The Ministry of Culture's museum directory describes collections illustrating Arunachal Pradesh's community life through clothing, ornaments, musical instruments, weapons, household artefacts and craft objects. It identifies an ethnographic display with traditional art, religious objects, wood carving and cane products, alongside archaeological finds. The museum provides material evidence for examining how communities use and transform locally available materials, while comparisons between object types can illuminate different technical and aesthetic choices without reducing the state's many peoples to one uniform tradition.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MUS-238","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MUS-238","src":"https://www.culture.gov.in/files/inline-documents/Directory_of_Indian_Museums_080620231.pdf","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kangla heritage complex","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Manipur","about":"Kangla was the seat of Manipur's rulers until 1891 and remains a place of cultural and religious importance. Temple remains, moats and other relics provide material evidence for the relationship between political authority, sacred space and regional architectural practice in Imphal.","full":"The Imphal West district administration identifies Kangla as the former seat of Manipur's power and highlights the old Govindajee temple, inner and outer moats and other surviving relics. These elements bring religious and defensive features into the same historic complex, providing a basis for examining how a royal centre organised movement, boundaries and worship. Its importance to Manipuri communities also makes the site relevant to studying the continuing relationship between built remains, political memory and the cultural meanings attached to a former capital.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-239","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-239","src":"https://imphalwest.nic.in/tourist-place/kangla/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ima Keithel women's market","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":null,"about":"Ima Keithel is Imphal's women-run market, where local produce, textiles and handicrafts circulate through daily exchange. Its continuing trading culture offers a setting for studying women's commercial knowledge and the relationship between regional goods, community livelihoods and the social organisation of marketplaces.","full":"The Greater Imphal planning document describes Ima Keithel as an established market operated by women, with goods ranging from local produce and dried fish to traditional handicrafts and clothing. This range places food, textiles and craft within a shared institution of daily exchange rather than an isolated display of objects. The market is relevant to the study of practical commercial knowledge, regional product networks and women's economic participation, while its organisation and changing built setting need to be understood through the experiences of the vendors themselves.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-240","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-240","src":"https://tpmanipur.mn.gov.in/media/filer_public/51/9e/519e0591-9536-40e8-808e-d0d38eed78b1/draft_gis_based_master_plan_for_greater_imphal.pdf","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Jingkieng Jri living root bridge cultural landscape","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Meghalaya","about":"Jingkieng Jri are living root bridges grown from Ficus roots by Khasi communities. Their continuing cultivation joins plant knowledge, community cooperation and the need for rural connectivity, showing how maintained living structures function within Meghalaya's wet forest landscapes and village routes.","full":"India's UNESCO Tentative List submission describes Jingkieng Jri as Ficus-based structures supporting connectivity and livelihoods in Meghalaya's moist forest region. The bridges are grown and maintained through knowledge transmitted across generations of Khasi communities, with cooperation and reciprocity central to the account of their continuing use. This cultural landscape offers a direct example of practical botanical knowledge applied to crossing and access needs, while its living character means that maintenance, growth and collective responsibility are essential parts of the structure itself.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-241","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-241","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6606","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Nartiang megalithic heritage","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Meghalaya","about":"Nartiang preserves standing stones and flat stone monuments associated with Jaintia ancestral and royal memory. Meghalaya Tourism distinguishes menhirs linked with male ancestors and dolmens linked with women, connecting the stone landscape with commemorative practice and the interpretation of community histories.","full":"Meghalaya Tourism describes Nartiang as a collection of megaliths connected with the Jaintia kingdom, ancestral reverence and commemoration. It distinguishes standing moo shynrang from flat moo kynthai stones and notes that additions continued into the nineteenth century. The site therefore offers evidence for a continuing historical tradition of raising and arranging stone monuments, while its meanings depend on community accounts of people and events as well as on the physical form and placement of the stones themselves.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-242","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-242","src":"https://www.meghalayatourism.in/experiences/adventure-%26-outdoor/living-root-bridges/nartiang-megalith/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mizoram State Archives","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"","state":"West Bengal","about":"Mizoram State Archives preserves administrative and historical records assembled from several repositories, including materials in Mizo. The collection provides sources for examining regional history and governance, while its preservation work documents how written evidence is retained and made available for future study.","full":"The Art and Culture Department traces the archives' beginnings to a unit supervised by Mizoram State Museum and its later establishment as a separate institution in 1985. It records the gathering of documents from Aizawl's Deputy Commissioner and repositories in Assam, West Bengal and New Delhi, including former British Residency papers and Mizo-language material. These holdings provide evidence for studying regional administration and cultural history, while their dispersed origins make provenance, record transfer and archival selection important parts of any interpretation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-MAN-243","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-MAN-243","src":"https://mizoculture.mizoram.gov.in/page/mizoram-state-archive","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Cheraw bamboo dance","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":null,"about":"Cheraw is a Mizo dance in which performers step between bamboo poles moved in rhythmic patterns. Coordinated pole movement, sound and footwork connect bodily timing with collective performance, while changing step patterns may evoke movements of birds or swaying trees in the tradition.","full":"IGNCA describes Cheraw through the coordination of dancers with people who open and close horizontal bamboo poles in rhythmic beats. The bamboo's sound marks the timing, while dancers move between and across the poles through varied step patterns, some recalling birds or trees. This precise cooperation provides an example of embodied and shared knowledge transmitted through participation, and the account places the dance within Mizo community traditions while also acknowledging uncertainty about its origins and the changing meanings attached to performance.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-244","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-244","src":"https://ignca.gov.in/divisionss/janapada-sampada/northeastern-regional-centre/miscellaneous-arts-and-crafts-in-mizoram/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kisama Naga Heritage Village","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Nagaland","about":"Kisama is a state-established heritage village near Kohima that presents the cultural traditions of Nagaland's communities. It serves as a shared setting for the Hornbill Festival, connecting public cultural presentation with efforts to preserve dialects, customs and distinct community identities within the state.","full":"Nagaland Tourism explains that Kisama was established on land associated with Kigwema and Phesama, with the state government commissioning the heritage village. Its stated purpose includes preserving ethnic cultural heritage, dialects, customs and traditions, and it provides a common platform during the Hornbill Festival. The site is relevant to studying how community knowledge is represented in a purpose-built public setting, including the choices involved when distinct traditions are brought together for cultural transmission, tourism and intercommunity presentation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-245","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-245","src":"https://tourism.nagaland.gov.in/naga-heritage-village/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Diezephe craft village","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Dhansiripar","state":"Nagaland","about":"Diezephe is associated with handloom weaving and handicraft production near Dimapur. Workshops, craft displays and artisan activity connect practical making skills with local livelihoods, offering a setting for examining the transmission of regional techniques and their adaptation through training and contemporary markets.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism describes Diezephe as a community of weavers and craft workers with weaving workshops, showrooms and a craft museum. It identifies support from the Nagaland Handloom and Handicrafts Development Corporation and opportunities to observe or learn making practices such as weaving and wood carving. These features make the village relevant to studying the relationship between inherited skills, training and entrepreneurship, while direct documentation of individual artisans' work is needed to understand the materials and techniques used in each craft.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-TRD-246","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-TRD-246","src":"https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/nagaland/dimapur/diezephe-craft-village","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":25.80241,"lng":93.69205,"approx":true},
{"name":"Rumtek Dharma Chakra Centre","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Singtam","state":"Sikkim","about":"Rumtek Dharma Chakra Centre is a Kagyupa Buddhist monastery and centre of learning established in 1962. Its architecture and religious collections connect the preservation of a Tibetan Buddhist lineage with monastic education and the adaptation of an institutional tradition within Sikkim's cultural landscape.","full":"Sikkim's Ecclesiastical Affairs Department identifies the Dharma Chakra Centre as a Kagyupa learning institution founded by the sixteenth Karmapa in 1962. Its account describes land provided under the Sikkimese monarchy and a four-storeyed building whose architecture followed the model of Tshurphu monastery in Tibet, with religious images and a commemorative stupa in an adjacent building. The centre offers a specific case for examining how monastic architectural forms, lineage memory and educational functions were re-established in a new regional setting.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-HER-247","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-HER-247","src":"https://sikkimeccl.gov.in/History/Monasteries/East/RumtekDharmaChakra.aspx","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":27.28857,"lng":88.56159,"approx":false},
{"name":"Namgyal Institute of Tibetology","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"Gangtok","state":"Sikkim","about":"The Namgyal Institute of Tibetology combines research, libraries and a museum in Gangtok. Tibetan texts, thangkas, statues and ritual objects support the study of Buddhist and Himalayan knowledge, while publications and teaching connect preservation with continuing scholarly work at the institute.","full":"The institute's account describes its establishment in 1958 to preserve and study texts concerning history, religion, literature and science, with collections later expanded to include thangkas, statues, xylographs and ritual instruments. Two libraries, a museum and research activities support work on Buddhism and the cultures of Sikkim and other Himalayan regions. The institution also reports teaching in Tibetan medicine and Buddhist and Tibetan studies, connecting the preservation of textual and visual materials with organised learning and the development of new research.","branch":"veda","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-IKS-248","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-IKS-248","src":"https://namgyalinstitutesikkim.org/home/","group":"Research institutions"},"lat":27.31574,"lng":88.60483,"approx":false},
{"name":"Unakoti rock-cut sculptures","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Tripura","about":"Unakoti uses cliff faces and boulders as surfaces for large religious reliefs, including forms of Shiva and Ganesha. Smaller sculptures, inscriptions and stream-side ritual places connect the carved landscape with regional visual traditions and continuing religious practice in northern Tripura today.","full":"India's UNESCO Tentative List submission describes Unakoti's reliefs carved into the vertical rock and boulders of a wooded hill landscape. It identifies monumental Shaiva images, Ganesha panels, smaller loose sculptures and an inscription, while noting the importance of streams and pools in the site's ritual setting. The ensemble provides evidence for how sculptors responded to natural geology, scale and local visual conventions, and for the relationship between religious imagery, landscape and the continuing use of a sacred place.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-249","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-249","src":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6628/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pilak archaeological landscape","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Tripura","about":"Pilak preserves archaeological remains associated with Hindu and Buddhist traditions, including terracotta plaques, sculpture and brick stupas. The area provides material evidence for regional artistic production and connections with neighbouring Bengal and other eastern cultural networks across the present-day borders of Tripura.","full":"The South Tripura district administration describes Pilak as an area of Hindu and Buddhist remains, with terracotta plaques, stone images and brick stupas identified through archaeological work. Its account compares aspects of the sculpture and plaques with neighbouring sites and artistic traditions in Bengal and beyond. These finds offer a basis for examining the movement and local adaptation of visual forms, while the distributed archaeological landscape makes find context essential when relating individual objects to particular structures, religious communities or phases of activity.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"IND-ARC-250","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/IND-ARC-250","src":"https://southtripura.nic.in/tourist-place/pilak/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sree Padmanabhaswamy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Sree Padmanabhaswamy Temple combines Kerala and Dravidian architectural forms in the historic centre of Thiruvananthapuram. Stone and bronze work, painted religious imagery and the reclining Vishnu figure connect its built fabric with sculptural, pictorial and ritual traditions.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the temple as a combination of Kerala and Dravidian architecture, with worked stone, bronze and murals inside. Its principal image represents Vishnu reclining on Adisheshan. Painted subjects include Vishnu, Narasimha, Ganapathy and Gaja Lakshmi. These features make the temple a setting for examining how architecture and images communicate religious narratives. Individual surfaces, repairs and objects require separate documentation before assigning dates or makers.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-251","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-251","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thiruvananthapuram/sree-padmanabhaswamy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.48293,"lng":76.94667,"approx":false},
{"name":"Malayinkeezhu Sree Krishna Swamy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Malayinkeezhu Sree Krishna Swamy Temple is a village shrine documented for its heritage structures and continuing ritual practices. Its annual festival follows the Malayalam calendar, connecting a surviving religious place with local ways of organizing ceremonial time and community participation.","full":"The Kerala Tourism temple directory identifies Malayinkeezhu as a Krishna shrine with heritage structures and distinctive rites. It records an eight-day annual festival during the Malayalam month of Meenam. The site can therefore introduce questions about village worship, the use of inherited buildings and the relation between calendars and communal gatherings. Detailed architectural measurements, inscriptions and the history of rebuilding are not supplied in the short directory entry.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-252","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-252","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thiruvananthapuram/malayinkeezhu-krishna","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thiruvallam Parasurama Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"The Parasurama Temple at Thiruvallam stands beside the Karamana River and is described as a black-granite religious structure. Its association with ancestral offerings links a material shrine, a river setting and inherited practices of remembrance within southern Kerala.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the Thiruvallam shrine with Parasurama worship and describes its structure as built in black granite. The account connects the riverbank location with Bali offerings for ancestors. These are useful starting points for studying stone construction and the spatial relationship between ritual and water. Foundation stories involving named sages belong to the site's religious tradition; they should be distinguished from independently dated architectural or inscriptional evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-253","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-253","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thiruvananthapuram/parasurama-thiruvallam","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.44158,"lng":76.95514,"approx":false},
{"name":"Sarkara Devi Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Poothura","state":"Kerala","about":"Sarkara Devi Temple has a two-storeyed rectangular sanctum decorated with deity sculptures. The shrine's Kaliyoottu tradition connects built space with dramatic storytelling about Kali and Darika, while harvest offerings place religious observance within a local seasonal calendar.","full":"The temple account describes a rectangular sanctum with two storeys and sculpted figures including Krishna, Rama, Vishnu and Durga. It also records Kaliyoottu as a dramatic presentation of the Kali-Darika narrative, accompanied by first-harvest offerings. Together these features provide material for examining sculpture, performance and seasonal observance at one site. Historical patronage claims and the dating of particular carvings require corroboration beyond the general tourism description.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-254","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-254","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thiruvananthapuram/sarkara-chirayinkeezhu","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.65402,"lng":76.78594,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thrivikramangalam Mahavishnu Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thrivikramangalam Mahavishnu Temple is documented for a three-storeyed sanctum with tiled roofs and sculptural decoration. The protected shrine at Thamalam offers a compact example for studying the arrangement of stacked roof forms and imagery in Kerala's temple architecture.","full":"Kerala Tourism locates this Vishnu temple at Thamalam and identifies it as a protected monument of the Archaeology Department. Its account highlights the three-storeyed sanctum, tiled roofing and sculpture. These documented features invite examination of roof composition, the placement of images and the relationship between conservation and continuing worship. The page assigns a medieval date, but the individual construction phases and later repairs have not been independently reviewed for this record.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-255","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-255","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thiruvananthapuram/thrivikramangalam-mahavishnu","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Valiyasala Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Valiyasala Mahadeva Temple is also known as Kanthalloor Mahadeva Temple. The official account associates its locality with the historic Kanthalloor Sala, making the site relevant to questions about religious places, regional memory and the history of organized learning in Kerala.","full":"The Kerala Tourism directory identifies Valiyasala as a Shiva temple and gives Kanthalloor Mahadeva Temple as its alternate name. It also places the historic Kanthalloor Sala at Valiyasala. That association provides a starting point for investigating how remembered educational institutions relate to surviving religious geography. The short page does not establish the sala's curriculum, exact location or institutional continuity, so those matters remain questions for inscriptional and historical research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-256","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-256","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thiruvananthapuram/kanthalloor-valiyasala-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.48439,"lng":76.9593,"approx":false},
{"name":"Janardhanaswamy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Janardhanaswamy Temple at Varkala combines a circular sanctum, copper-covered roof and a square pavilion with carved planetary imagery. An inscription reported in the pavilion adds a written source to the study of this coastal religious and architectural setting.","full":"The official temple essay describes a circular sanctum covered by a conical copper roof and a square mandapam whose wooden ceiling carries Navagraha imagery. It reports an inscription dated 1252 on the lower part of the pavilion. These details connect carpentry, roof covering, religious representations of the planets and epigraphy. The inscription's text and its relationship to different building phases require direct scholarly examination rather than inference from the tourism summary.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-257","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-257","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thiruvananthapuram/janardhanaswamy-varkala","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Paliekkara St. George Orthodox Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Paliekkara St. George Orthodox Church preserves narrative murals with Syriac writing beneath painted scenes. Subjects from the life of Christ and the disciples show how text, colour and religious storytelling were combined on the walls of a Kerala church.","full":"Kerala Tourism's account of church murals describes the western-wall paintings at Paliekkara, identifying the Nativity, the Magi, Christ's baptism and the disciples among their subjects. It notes dark colours, prominent blue and Syriac inscriptions below the pictures. The ensemble offers evidence for studying how painted narrative and written language work together within worship. Its pigments, repainting history and the hands responsible for different scenes need conservation-level investigation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-258","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-258","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/christianity/mural-paintings/25","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Attukal Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Attukal Bhagavathy Temple combines Tamil and Kerala architectural elements with carved figures of deities. Its association with the women's Pongala gathering places the shrine within a broader landscape of collective cooking, devotion and the transmission of ceremonial knowledge in the city.","full":"The official temple account describes a combination of Tamil and Kerala architectural styles at Attukal. It identifies carved figures including Kali, Parvathy, Shiva and Vishnu's incarnations, and records Pongala as a ceremony performed by women. The physical shrine can be studied alongside the ways people organize and remember its recurring observances. Attendance superlatives, legendary identifications and the dates of individual sculptural elements are not independently established in this record.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-259","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-259","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thiruvananthapuram/attukal-bhagavathy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kazhakuttom Sree Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kazhakuttom Sree Mahadeva Temple is documented as a Shiva shrine with sloping tiled roofs, carved timber pillars and a ritual hall. These material features connect the site with Kerala's traditions of carpentry and the arrangement of ceremonial spaces within temple buildings.","full":"Kerala Tourism's destination account identifies a Shiva sanctum, an entrance tower and a mandapam with carved wooden pillars and ceilings. It also describes the use of sloping tiled roofs in the complex. These are starting points for studying the relationship between timber work, roof forms and the spaces used for worship. The page supplies no measured survey or analysis of original fabric, so details of construction phases remain open.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-260","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-260","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/sree-mahadeva-temple-kazhakkuttom/239/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kottarakkara Sree Mahaganapathi Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kottarakkara Sree Mahaganapathi Temple is a Shiva shrine known particularly for its Ganapathi sub-shrine. The temple's association with Unniyappam offerings connects religious space with a continuing practice of preparing and sharing a distinctive rice-and-banana food offering in southern Kerala.","full":"The Kerala Tourism temple directory explains that Shiva is the main deity of the Kottarakkara shrine, although its Ganapathi sub-shrine gives the place its widely used name. It also identifies Unniyappam as an offering made with rice and bananas. The record supports investigation of how the naming of a site, daily offerings and community memory intersect. Exact recipes, construction phases and claimed antiquity need documentation beyond the introductory page.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-261","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-261","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kollam/kottarakkara-mahaganapathi","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mukhathala Sreekrishnaswamy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kottamkara","state":"Kerala","about":"Mukhathala Sreekrishnaswamy Temple is noted for its outer wall, copper-covered flagstaff and ceremonial halls. Its lamp structure and pavilion provide identifiable features through which to examine the relationship between enclosure, lighting and processional activity in a Kerala temple.","full":"The official temple account identifies the Anapanthal, Balikkalpura, Vilakkumadam and the pavilion before the sanctum at Mukhathala. It also describes a large outer wall and a copper-covered flagstaff. These named elements give a starting vocabulary for studying how a temple separates and connects activities within its enclosure. Claims about the Balikkalpura maintaining a constant comfortable temperature require environmental measurement; they are not accepted here as demonstrated building performance.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-262","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-262","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kollam/mukhathala-sreekrishnaswamy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.91252,"lng":76.6494,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kottukal Rock-cut Cave Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kottukal Rock-cut Cave Temple contains two excavated cells with Shiva lingas and a central Ganapathi image. The arrangement preserves direct evidence of how sculptors shaped a rock mass into both sacred images and connected spaces for worship in southern Kerala.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the temple at Ittiva and describes two cells, each containing a Shiva linga, placed on either side of a central projection carrying Ganapathi. This layout makes the site relevant to rock excavation, sculptural planning and the conversion of a natural material into architectural space. The brief source does not provide tool marks, excavation stages or a securely argued date; these require archaeological recording and comparison with other rock-cut shrines.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-263","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-263","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kollam/kottukal-rock-cut-cave-temple","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Anandavalleeswaram Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Anandavalleeswaram Mahadeva Temple brings Shiva and Anandavally worship together within a Kollam shrine complex. Its recurring processions, lamp ceremonies and cultural programmes show how religious buildings accommodate a sequence of activities rather than serving only as containers for images.","full":"The temple directory identifies Shiva and Anandavally as principal deities and describes additional images within the shrine. It records a festival sequence involving flag hoisting, processions, ritual bathing and cultural performances. Those observations allow inquiry into the use of temple spaces across a ceremonial cycle. The page's claims that the shrine is uniquely arranged or founded by Parasurama are not treated as independently verified architectural or historical conclusions.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-264","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-264","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kollam/anandavalleeswaram-sri-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Oachira Parabrahma Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Oachira","state":"Kerala","about":"Oachira Parabrahma Temple is an open religious landscape where worship takes place beneath trees rather than within a conventional enclosed shrine. Its distinctive arrangement connects sacred space with vegetation, collective gathering and ceremonial practices that differ from the built sanctums found elsewhere.","full":"The official account describes worship of Parabrahmam without a central idol or covered temple structure. Trees are part of the worship setting. It also records Oachirakkali mock combat and the movement of large bull effigies on wheeled platforms during another festival. These observations make Oachira useful for comparing open and enclosed forms of religious space. Interpretations of the site's origin and the historical development of each ceremony remain matters for further research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-265","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-265","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kollam/oachira","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.13576,"lng":76.51052,"approx":false},
{"name":"Achankovil Sastha Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Punalur","state":"Kerala","about":"Achankovil Sastha Temple is a forest shrine associated with the Ayyappa pilgrimage landscape of southern Kerala. Its setting and links with other Sastha temples make it relevant to the study of remembered routes, religious geography and the relationships between shrines and forest environments.","full":"Kerala Tourism places Achankovil within a forest and identifies it as a Sastha temple associated with Ayyappa worship. The source links it with Kulathupuzha, Aryankavu and Sabarimala. These connections can guide research into the way named shrines form a regional pilgrimage geography. The site's stories about consecration and healing are matters of religious belief. They do not establish construction dates, medical effectiveness or safe responses to poisonous bites.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-266","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-266","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/achankovil-temple-kollam/437/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.09177,"lng":77.12161,"approx":false},
{"name":"Cheriazheekal Shiva–Vishnu Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Cheriazheekal's twin-shrine arrangement accommodates Shiva and Vishnu worship in one temple complex. The official description emphasizes stone carving and renovation, providing a starting point for examining how contemporary craft work contributes to the continued use and appearance of inherited religious places.","full":"The destination account describes separate sanctums for a Shiva linga and a standing Vishnu image. It also states that extensive renovation produced a temple finished in carved stone. This distinction matters: surviving architectural appearance should not automatically be assigned the age claimed in local foundation stories. The complex offers a case for studying renewal, material choice and iconographic arrangement, while the dates and extent of older fabric require a conservation record.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-267","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-267","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/cheriazheekal-temple/313/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kaviyoor Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kaniyanpara","state":"Kerala","about":"Kaviyoor Mahadeva Temple is a structural shrine documented for inscriptions and carved timber decoration. The Census district handbook distinguishes early temple references from later wooden sculpture, providing a basis for studying successive artistic layers rather than assigning one date to the whole building.","full":"The 2011 Census handbook for Pathanamthitta refers to Kaviyoor inscriptions recording gifts in 950 and associates the temple's carved wooden images with a later period. This evidence supports treating the shrine as a building with several historical layers. It is distinct from the nearby Thrikkakkudi rock-cut cave temple. Epigraphic readings, conservation reports and direct examination would be needed to connect particular repairs or carvings with named patrons and dates.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-268","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-268","src":"https://censusindia.gov.in/nada/index.php/catalog/662/download/2296/DH_2011_3212_PART_A_DCHB_PATHANAMTHITTA.pdf","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.3964,"lng":76.61084,"approx":false},
{"name":"Sabarimala Ayyappa Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Sabarimala","state":"Kerala","about":"Sabarimala Ayyappa Temple occupies a hilltop within the Periyar forest landscape. Its eighteen sacred steps, pavilions and metal-covered roof elements connect built space with the embodied sequence of a pilgrimage, offering material for studying movement, thresholds and religious geography in Kerala.","full":"The temple essay identifies a sanctum with a gold-plated roof, pavilions and a Balikkalpura, along with the eighteen steps that form an important approach to the shrine. Its hilltop forest setting is central to how the place is understood. These details support inquiry into the relation between architectural thresholds and pilgrimage practice. The record does not provide current access rules, opening schedules or a determination of the age of each structure.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-269","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-269","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/pathanamthitta/sabarimala","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.43459,"lng":77.08139,"approx":false},
{"name":"Sree Vallabha Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvalla","state":"Kerala","about":"Sree Vallabha Temple preserves carved timber, mural surfaces and a prominent granite Garuda pillar. The combination of a circular sanctum, roof coverings and enclosure walls makes it a useful site for comparing different materials and representational traditions within one Kerala temple complex.","full":"Kerala Tourism highlights the temple's wooden carving, murals, granite enclosure and Garuda pillar. It describes the sanctum as circular and copper-roofed. Together these features provide a starting inventory for investigating the coordination of stone, timber, metal and painted surfaces in temple construction. Stories crediting the pillar to Perunthachan are attributed traditions; the page's inconsistent age claims should not be used as a reliable construction chronology without independent architectural and epigraphic evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-270","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-270","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/pathanamthitta/vallabha-thiruvalla","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.37372,"lng":76.56243,"approx":false},
{"name":"Aranmula Parthasarathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kozhencherry","state":"Kerala","about":"Aranmula Parthasarathy Temple stands beside the Pamba and preserves carved timber and painted narratives. Its architectural decoration draws on the Ramayana and Mahabharata, while its river-linked observances connect the physical shrine with food offerings and ceremonial boats from surrounding settlements.","full":"The official temple essay describes carved roofs and pillars depicting epic stories, together with painted walls. It also links the Krishna shrine with Vallasadya and ceremonial snake boats on the Pamba. The building therefore provides a place to examine narrative imagery alongside the wider geography of ritual participation. This record concerns the temple complex, which is distinct from the Aranmula metal-mirror craft already represented in the register.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-271","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-271","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/pathanamthitta/aranmula-parthasarathy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.32804,"lng":76.68775,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kodumon Chilanthiyambalam","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kodumon Chilanthiyambalam, also called Palliyara Sree Bhagavathy Temple, is associated with local healing beliefs and the memory of Sanskrit dramatist Sakthibhadra. The site provides a starting point for studying how religious places accumulate literary associations and community narratives over time.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Chilanthiyambalam as Palliyara Sree Bhagavathy Temple and connects Kodumon with Sakthibhadra, the author associated with Ascharyachoodamani. It also describes worship connected with beliefs about spider bites. These statements are useful as evidence of how the site is publicly represented, rather than proof of therapeutic effectiveness or the author's exact birthplace. Literary history, temple records and oral accounts would need to be compared before stronger historical conclusions are drawn.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-272","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-272","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/kodumon-chilanthiyambalam/291/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thrikkakkudi Rock-cut Cave Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thrikkakkudi, also called the Kaviyoor rock-cut temple, contains a Shiva shrine excavated from rock and associated sculptural figures. The cave offers direct material for studying the relationship between excavation, architectural form and guardian imagery in Kerala's early religious buildings.","full":"The Kerala Tourism description identifies the rock-cut shrine with Shiva and notes figures including Ganapathy and door guardians. It associates the work stylistically with Pallava models. These are starting points for comparing the carving of architectural space and images from one rock mass. The cave is a separate nearby site from the structural Kaviyoor Mahadeva Temple; details of its dating and sculptural sequence require specialist archaeological comparison.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-273","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-273","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/thekkekudi-cave-temple/315/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Malayalapuzha Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Malayalapuzha Bhagavathy Temple is documented for a rectangular sanctum, an entrance porch and an image made using Kadu Sarkara Yogam. The shrine connects architectural space with a material tradition in which several organic and mineral substances are assembled into a religious image.","full":"The official Sabarimala portal describes the temple's Namaskara Mandapa, Chuttambalam, Balikkalpura and rectangular sanctum with an entrance porch. Its account identifies Kadu Sarkara Yogam as the technique used for the main image and lists ingredients including wood, clay, plant substances and natural glue. This provides a lead for investigating composite image making. It is not a verified recipe, and material identification would require conservators and knowledgeable practitioners.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-274","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-274","src":"https://sabarimala.kerala.gov.in/index.php/node/549","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kodumthara Sree Subramanya Swamy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kodumthara Sree Subramanya Swamy Temple stands beside the Achankovil River and is documented for a circular sanctum with a conical roof. Its form and riverbank setting provide a southern Kerala example for examining temple geometry and relationships between built worship spaces and water.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the shrine with Subramanya worship and describes its circular Srikovil and conical roof. The account also locates the complex on the Achankovil River and names several associated deities. These details offer a starting point for comparing sanctuary forms and the placement of shrines within a river landscape. Foundation stories and early dates quoted in popular accounts require separate corroboration through inscriptions and structural investigation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-275","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-275","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/subrahmanya-temple-kodumthara/590/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ambalappuzha Sree Krishna Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Ambalappuzha","state":"Kerala","about":"Ambalappuzha Sree Krishna Temple is documented for Kerala-style architecture and painted walls within its surrounding Chuttambalam. Its association with Parthasarathy imagery and a milk-rice offering links visual narrative, built enclosure and food preparation in the cultural landscape of Alappuzha.","full":"The official temple essay identifies the presiding image with Krishna as Parthasarathy and describes wall paintings in the Chuttambalam around the sanctum. It also records the Palpayasam offering. These features provide material for studying how narrative identities and ceremonial food relate to temple space. The source offers a broad range for the shrine's foundation; a single date should not be assigned to its paintings, timber work and later renovations.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-276","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-276","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/ambalapuzha-sree-krishna","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.38328,"lng":76.36959,"approx":false},
{"name":"Harippad Subramanya Swamy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Harippad Subramanya Swamy Temple is known for an image of the deity with four arms and a prominent ceremonial flagstaff. Its iconography and architectural marker provide a starting point for studying the visual language through which a Kerala shrine identifies its principal deity.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Harippad's shrine with Subramanya and describes an image carrying a spear and other attributes. It also singles out the flagstaff as a notable feature. These details make the temple relevant to the study of sculptural attributes, ritual objects and the placement of ceremonial vertical elements in sacred space. Claims of exceptional antiquity, statewide size rankings and consecration by Parasurama remain unverified traditions rather than established archaeological conclusions.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-277","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-277","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/harippad-subramanya-swami","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kandiyoor Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Mavelikkara","state":"Kerala","about":"Kandiyoor Mahadeva Temple stands near the Achankovil River and is documented for inscriptions and stone sculpture. Its association with several forms of Shiva worship provides a setting for studying how written records, carved imagery and recurring ritual interpretation coexist within a historic temple.","full":"The temple essay locates Kandiyoor near Mavelikkara and notes stone inscriptions and sculpture. It describes Shiva being worshipped through different identities at different times of day and refers to an enclosure associated with a curved architectural form. The site offers questions about the relationship between material remains and changing ritual interpretation. Accounts of supernatural construction and unspecific references to inscriptions must be separated from evidence established through survey and epigraphic reading.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-278","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-278","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/kandiyoor-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.25317,"lng":76.52979,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thakazhi Dharmasastha Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thakazhi Dharmasastha Temple is associated with Kalamezhuthu Pattu, combining temporary floor imagery with song. The shrine provides a named setting in which to investigate how a religious building accommodates visual work that is repeatedly made, performed with and removed rather than permanently installed.","full":"The official account identifies Thakazhi as an Ayyappa shrine and records Kalamezhuthu Pattu among its observances. It also describes a festival ending with an Arattu procession. These details support study of the relationship between a permanent place, temporary floor drawing, music and ceremonial movement. The source additionally mentions beliefs about an oil offering; this record does not treat those claims as medical evidence or provide instructions for treatment.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-279","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-279","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/thakazhi-dharmasastha","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thuravoor Narasimha–Sudarsana Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thuravoor Temple accommodates Narasimha and Sudarsana sanctums within one Nalambalam. Paired flagstaffs and the Anapanthal make the complex a useful example for examining how two religious centres are coordinated through a shared enclosure and related ceremonial structures in Kerala architecture.","full":"The temple essay describes two sanctums inside one Nalambalam, dedicated respectively to Narasimha and Sudarsana. It also notes two metal-covered flagstaffs and a large Anapanthal used in ceremonies. These features provide a starting point for studying symmetry, shared circulation and the relationship between multiple shrines. The page's traditional foundation dates are not independently checked here, and the two sanctums are recorded as one complex rather than padded into separate entries.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-280","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-280","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/thuravoor-narasimhamoorthy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thrikkuratti Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thrikkuratti Mahadeva Temple is a Shiva shrine in Mannar, a town associated with bell-metal work. The temple's annual Shivaratri observance places it within a local network of religious spaces and craft settlements, offering a starting point for research into the area's material culture.","full":"Kerala Tourism locates the Shiva temple in Mannar and identifies the surrounding town with bell-metal production. It also records Shivaratri as an important annual observance. These facts suggest questions about the relationships among worship, craft settlements and the circulation of ritual objects. They do not establish that particular objects in the temple were made locally. Such connections would need object documentation, makers' accounts and records of commissions.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-281","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-281","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/thrikkuratti-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chengannur Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Chengannur","state":"Kerala","about":"Chengannur Mahadeva Temple is documented for a temple theatre, carved pavilions and a frontage with detailed woodwork. Its Shiva–Parvathy worship and Panchaloha image connect the architectural setting with traditions of timber carving, religious theatre and metal image making in Kerala.","full":"The official temple essay identifies a Koothambalam, mandapams and a carved Mukhamandapam at Chengannur. It also describes the goddess image as made of Panchaloha, an alloy traditionally associated with five metals. These features support study of how performance spaces, timber decoration and metal icons coexist in one religious complex. The source attributes construction to the legendary Perunthachan; that story should be distinguished from independently documented dates and makers.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-282","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-282","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/chengannur-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.32425,"lng":76.61161,"approx":false},
{"name":"Chettikulangara Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Chettikulangara Bhagavathy Temple is a principal setting for Kettukazhcha, in which large decorated structures are brought towards the shrine. The site connects permanent temple space with temporary construction, processional routes and the collective work of surrounding communities during its ceremonial season.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Bhadrakali as the principal deity and describes processions of large decorated Kuthira and Theru structures, alongside figures from epic narratives. It also records Kuthiyottam among the temple's observances. The physical site offers a way to investigate the routes, forecourts and shared labour needed for these activities. The temporary structures are part of a continuing ceremonial practice; their present forms should not be assumed unchanged across centuries.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-283","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-283","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/chettikulangara-bhagavathy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mullakkal Rajarajeswari Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mullakkal Rajarajeswari Temple is an urban religious place documented in Kerala's architectural tradition. Its extended Chirappu festival illustrates how a town shrine becomes a setting for processions and cultural activity, connecting recurring ceremonial time with the everyday fabric of Alappuzha.","full":"The temple directory identifies the shrine with Durga and describes it as built in the Kerala style. It records Mullakkal Chirappu as an extended festival involving processions and cultural programmes. The site therefore supports questions about the use of temple enclosures and nearby urban routes during large gatherings. The page's broad age estimate is not a survey of the existing structures, and the chronology of later construction remains to be checked.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-284","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-284","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/alappuzha/mullakkal","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mannarasala Nagaraja Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Haripad","state":"Kerala","about":"Mannarasala Nagaraja Temple combines serpent shrines, a sacred grove and an ancestral household associated with a female ritual custodian. Its layout and observances offer material for studying relationships among vegetation, domestic space, religious authority and serpent imagery in Kerala's living heritage.","full":"The official account describes the temple, Sarpa Kavu and Illom as connected parts of the Mannarasala religious setting. During Ayilyam observances, images are carried towards the household, and the Mannarasala Amma has a prominent ritual role. These documented relationships support inquiry into how grove and dwelling form part of a worship landscape. Origin stories and claims about fertility or disease belong to belief, rather than demonstrated environmental or medical effectiveness.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-285","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-285","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/mannarasala-sree-nagaraja-temple/63/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.29032,"lng":76.44583,"approx":false},
{"name":"Chakkulathukavu Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Chakkulathukavu Bhagavathy Temple is a shrine associated with Pongala, where women prepare offerings in earthen pots. Its religious gathering provides a named setting for examining how cooking vessels, shared space and the coordination of participants become part of a continuing ceremonial tradition.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the shrine in Alappuzha and describes Pongala offerings prepared by women in earthen pots. It also refers to carved woodwork within the temple. These limited observations offer a starting point for researching the relationship between a permanent worship place and temporary cooking arrangements around a gathering. The page's attendance superlatives and broad promotional descriptions are not independently verified, and no ancient origin or construction date is asserted.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-286","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-286","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/chakkulathukavu-bhagavathy-temple/458/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ettumanoor Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Ettumanoor","state":"Kerala","about":"Ettumanoor Mahadeva Temple is known for mural painting, particularly a Nataraja image on its entrance structure. Its painted surfaces and ceremonial elephant figures connect visual narrative with the making, preservation and periodic display of religious objects in a Kerala shrine complex.","full":"The official temple essay highlights mural art at Ettumanoor and specifically identifies Nataraja on the gopuram. It also records the procession associated with the Ezharaponnana elephant figures. These details support investigation of the different ways sacred imagery appears as a fixed painted surface and as an object carried in public ceremony. A foundation date quoted for the temple does not automatically establish the dates of its murals or processional objects.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-287","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-287","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kottayam/ettumanoor-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.6722,"lng":76.55969,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thaliyil Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Thaliyil Mahadeva Temple is associated with the former Thalikotta enclosure and the Thekkumkoor rulers. Its location in Thazhathangadi connects a religious building with the remembered geography of a fortified settlement, offering a starting point for studying political and sacred landscapes together.","full":"Kerala Tourism places the temple near Uppoottikkavala in Thazhathangadi and explains that Thalikotta was a fort surrounding the shrine. The account identifies the site as important to the Thekkumkoor rulers. These associations invite research into the relationship between a temple, a former defensive enclosure and regional authority. The short page does not map surviving fort fabric or describe its construction, so neither the extent nor the date of that enclosure is established here.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-288","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-288","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kottayam/thaliyil-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.59882,"lng":76.86049,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thirunakkara Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kottayam","state":"Kerala","about":"Thirunakkara Mahadeva Temple preserves murals and a Koothambalam within an urban Kerala-style complex. Painted Shiva and Vishnu narratives and the space for performance make the shrine relevant to the study of how architecture supports several forms of religious communication within one town landmark.","full":"The official temple description notes murals depicting Shiva stories and Vishnu's incarnations, together with a Koothambalam used for cultural performances. It also identifies a flagstaff and associated ceremonial structures. These features offer a starting point for examining the coordination of painted narrative and live performance. The source attributes patronage to the Thekkumkoor ruler; the history of individual buildings, paintings and later conservation work requires evidence beyond the general destination account.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-289","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-289","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kottayam/thirunakkara-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.59042,"lng":76.51879,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thrikkodithanam Mahavishnu Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thrikkodithanam","state":"Kerala","about":"Thrikkodithanam Mahavishnu Temple is documented for stone inscriptions, mural painting and decorated woodwork. Its carved representations of Vishnu's incarnations provide a focused setting for studying how written records and narrative images are incorporated into the fabric of a Kerala religious complex.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies stone carving, wall inscriptions, murals and decorated timber at Thrikkodithanam. It particularly mentions images of the Dasavatara, Vishnu's ten incarnations. These material categories invite comparison between documentary writing and visual forms of religious narration. The temple is associated with Pandava traditions, but those stories should not be used as construction dates. Independent readings of the inscriptions and the study of individual architectural phases remain necessary.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-290","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-290","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kottayam/thrikkodithanam-mahavishnu","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.43915,"lng":76.56569,"approx":true},
{"name":"Vaikom Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Vaikom","state":"Kerala","about":"Vaikom Mahadeva Temple is documented for a sanctum whose external circular appearance differs from its oval plan. Stone roofing and the use of timber in an adjoining chamber make it a place to examine the relationship between plan, structure and materials in Kerala temple architecture.","full":"The official temple account describes an oval Sreekovil that appears circular from outside. It identifies a stone-built sanctum, including its roof, and the use of a single timber piece in the first chamber. These are specific leads for studying the relationship between visible form and construction. They require confirmation through measured survey. Religious accounts placing the image in a mythic age are not evidence for the date of the surviving buildings.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-291","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-291","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kottayam/vaikom-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.74994,"lng":76.39593,"approx":false},
{"name":"Vazhappally Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Changanassery","state":"Kerala","about":"Vazhappally Mahadeva Temple is associated with an important early Malayalam inscription and a layout containing two Nalambalams and flagstaffs. The shrine connects the study of language and administrative memory with the arrangement of enclosed spaces in Kerala's religious architecture.","full":"The temple directory describes two Nalambalams and two flagstaffs at Vazhappally and notes an early inscription associated with the site. These features provide separate leads for architectural and linguistic research. The inscription can inform historical questions only through a reliable reading of its language, script and context. The page's claim that it is the first Malayalam stone inscription is not independently assessed, and legendary foundation traditions are not adopted as dates.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-292","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-292","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kottayam/vazhappally-siva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.45613,"lng":76.52639,"approx":false},
{"name":"Udayanapuram Subramanya Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Udayanapuram Subramanya Temple is documented for carved timber and its relationship with the Vaikom temple's ceremonial cycle. The building offers a place to study Kerala carpentry alongside the ways neighbouring shrines are linked through shared festival geography and inherited religious narratives.","full":"The official temple essay identifies Udayanapuram near Vaikom, highlights its woodcarving and describes its architecture as belonging to the Kerala tradition. It also records a close relationship with the Vaikom Mahadeva Temple and its festivals. These observations support research into carving and connections among religious places. They do not establish the dates of the timber work or justify treating claims of equivalence with another shrine as historical facts.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-293","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-293","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kottayam/udayanapuram-subramanya","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kidangoor Subramanya Swamy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kidangoor Subramanya Swamy Temple is noted for a Koothambalam with carved epic scenes. The building connects the physical provision of a temple theatre with narrative sculpture and the regional memory of carpentry, offering a concrete setting for the study of performance architecture.","full":"DTPC Kottayam identifies carved Ramayana and Mahabharata scenes within Kidangoor's Koothambalam and relates its design to ideas associated with the Natya Shastra. The account attributes the theatre to Perunthachan and gives a traditional explanation of a distinctive timber pillar. The documented theatre and carving are research starting points, while the maker attribution, botanical identification and exact date require architectural survey and comparison with specialist studies.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-294","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-294","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/subrahmanya-swami-temple-kidangoor","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Panachikkad Saraswathi Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Panachikkad Saraswathi Temple is a local religious centre associated with Navaratri and Vidyarambham, the initiation of children into letters. Its continuing use makes it relevant to the study of how a worship place becomes a public setting for learning, music and literary participation.","full":"The Panachikkad Grama Panchayat history identifies the Saraswathi shrine with Navaratri and the Vidyarambham ceremony on Vijayadashami. It also notes the gathering of literary artists and musicians. These statements ground a record about the place's educational and cultural associations without assuming it functioned as a formal school. Temple records and practitioner accounts would be needed to establish how the ceremony, its participants and its spatial arrangements changed over time.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-295","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-295","src":"https://panachikkadpanchayat.lsgkerala.gov.in/en/history/history-grama-panchayat","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Cheppad St. George Orthodox Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Cheppad","state":"Kerala","about":"Cheppad St. George Orthodox Church preserves a group of sanctuary murals with biblical narratives. The painted ensemble offers direct material for studying the interpretation of Christian texts through local visual traditions and the relationships between church interiors, colour and religious instruction in Kerala.","full":"Kerala Tourism's dedicated church account locates Cheppad between Harippad and Kayamkulam and describes murals inside the sanctuary with subjects from the Old and New Testaments. It discusses their relationship with Kerala mural traditions and Persian artistic comparisons. These are useful leads for studying composition and visual religious narration. Exact dates, pigment materials, the extent of repainting and the stylistic comparisons require conservation and art-historical evidence beyond the introductory account.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-296","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-296","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/christianity/cheppad-church/63","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.23771,"lng":76.47151,"approx":false},
{"name":"Karikodu Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Mulanthuruthy","state":"Kerala","about":"Karikodu Bhagavathy Temple is documented for a mirror-like religious image and a temple tank within a Kerala-style complex. These features offer a starting point for examining how distinctive forms of deity representation are accommodated within the architecture and ritual spaces of an inland shrine.","full":"The destination account places the temple at Karikodu near Thodupuzha and describes the main image as Shilakannadi Prathishta, a mirror-like form. It also refers to a tank and traditional architectural features. Those observations provide leads for material and spatial investigation. The precise substance of the image and the histories of its installation, repairs and associated rituals require consultation with temple custodians; the page's broad age estimate is not independently verified.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-297","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-297","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/karikod-bhagavathy-temple/203/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.89664,"lng":76.39994,"approx":true},
{"name":"Annamalai Shiva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Annamalai Shiva Temple at Karikodu is described as a Chola-style shrine with a window divided into nine openings. The source associates this feature with the Navagraha, providing a specific lead for studying architectural pattern and representations of the planets in a Kerala temple.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the temple with Shiva and draws attention to a nine-lattice window interpreted through the Navagraha. It characterizes the building as Chola in style. These observations can guide a comparison of architectural features and religious meanings across regional boundaries. The introductory page does not supply a plan, inscriptional text or stylistic argument, and its broad antiquity claim is not accepted as a secure date for the existing structure.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-298","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-298","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/annamalai-temple-karikodu/450/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Anjuthengu Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Anjuthengu Fort, also called Anjengo Fort, preserves the layout of a coastal military and trading post. Its bastions and gun positions provide material for studying how masonry defences, maritime routes and colonial commercial power were arranged along the Kerala coast.","full":"DTPC Thiruvananthapuram identifies the fort with the British East India Company and describes its bastions and artillery positions facing the surrounding approaches and sea. These features offer a starting point for examining the technical organization of coastal defence and its relationship with trade. The complex is recorded as one site rather than separating its walls and bastions into new entries. Detailed construction phases and later alterations require archaeological documentation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-299","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-299","src":"https://www.dtpcthiruvananthapuram.com/destination/anjengo-fort","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kilimanoor Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Chemmarathumukku","state":"Kerala","about":"Kilimanoor Palace combines Kerala courtyard architecture with the remembered working environment of painter Raja Ravi Varma. The Nalukettu and associated studio make the complex relevant to the relationship between domestic patronage, built space and the practice of painting in nineteenth-century Kerala.","full":"DTPC describes the palace as a group of Kerala-style buildings and identifies both a Nalukettu and Ravi Varma's studio within the complex. It connects the site with the painter's life and work. These features allow investigation of how residential architecture and artistic production shared a setting. The original state of the working room and the history of its contents require further documentation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-300","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-300","src":"https://www.dtpcthiruvananthapuram.com/destination/kilimanoor-palace","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.76268,"lng":76.86543,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kowdiar Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Kowdiar Palace is documented as a twentieth-century Travancore residence combining Kerala and Dravidian architectural elements. Its continuing residential identity provides a case for studying the adaptation of regional building traditions within a later royal commission rather than assuming all palace architecture belongs to antiquity.","full":"The DTPC account dates the palace commission to 1934 and associates it with Sree Chithira Thirunal Balarama Varma. It describes a combination of Kerala and Dravidian styles and identifies the building as a residence of the Travancore family. These details support inquiry into twentieth-century patronage and architectural continuity. Interior access and private residential use should not be confused with a public museum, and detailed construction evidence remains to be reviewed.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-301","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-301","src":"https://www.dtpcthiruvananthapuram.com/destination/kowdiar-palace","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.52401,"lng":76.96322,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kanakakunnu Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Kanakakunnu Palace is documented for architecture combining Kerala and Gothic elements and for its later use as a cultural venue. The palace and its performance grounds make it relevant to the adaptation of a royal setting for public artistic gatherings in Kerala's capital.","full":"DTPC Thiruvananthapuram describes the red-fronted palace through Kerala and Gothic architectural influences and notes chandeliers and furniture within. It identifies the Nishagandhi and Sooryakanthi auditoriums on the grounds and their use for cultural events. The complex offers a case for investigating how changing patronage alters the public use of inherited space. Precise dates, construction materials and the conservation history of individual interiors require further evidence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-302","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-302","src":"https://www.dtpcthiruvananthapuram.com/destination/kanakakunnu-palace","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.51065,"lng":76.9581,"approx":false},
{"name":"Attingal Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Attingal","state":"Kerala","about":"Attingal Palace is documented as a Kerala-style royal residential precinct built with laterite, stone and lime mortar. Its Ettukettu form connects the complex with courtyard planning, while its relationship with the royal family's temple illustrates how domestic and ceremonial spaces were organized together.","full":"The Kerala Town Planning heritage survey identifies the palace with the women of the Travancore royal family and describes its main building as an Ettukettu. It names laterite blocks, stone and lime mortar among the construction materials and places Thiruvarattukavu Temple within the precinct. These details offer leads for studying courtyard form, masonry and the relationship between royal residence and worship within a shared precinct.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-303","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-303","src":"https://townplanning.kerala.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Heritage-series-Thiruvananthapuram-website.pdf","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.69171,"lng":76.80486,"approx":false},
{"name":"Adityapuram Surya Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Ayamkudy","state":"Kerala","about":"Adityapuram Surya Temple is a shrine dedicated to the Sun at Iravimangalam near Kaduthuruthy. Its religious image and calendrical observances offer a named location for studying the representation of solar divinity and the relationship between worship and seasonal time in Kerala.","full":"The Kerala Tourism destination account identifies a Surya shrine at Adityapuram and records important observances during Medam and Vrischikam. It also preserves local stories about the image and its consecration. These details offer a point of entry into the study of religious representations of the Sun and ritual calendars. Claims about the stone image absorbing oil or the site's uniqueness require separate material and comparative investigation; no scientific explanation is assumed here.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-304","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-304","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/surya-temple-adityapuram/489/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.73676,"lng":76.48222,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mahatma Ayyankali Hall","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mahatma Ayyankali Hall, formerly Victoria Jubilee Town Hall, is a historic public assembly venue in Thiruvananthapuram. Its changing name and use connect a colonial-era building with the history of social reform, public speech and cultural participation in Kerala's capital.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the building by both its former VJT Hall name and its later Ayyankali name. It describes the venue's use for meetings, concerts, fairs and other public events and connects it with Ayyankali's advocacy for oppressed communities. The hall therefore provides a place to examine how public architecture carries changing social meanings. Claims about its earliest legislative use and commemorative chronology need corroboration and are not repeated as established facts here.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-305","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-305","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/vjt-hall-thiruvananthapuram/434/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Vizhinjam Rock-cut Cave","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Vizhinjam","state":"Kerala","about":"Vizhinjam's rock-cut cave was excavated within a boulder and preserves Shiva-related carvings. The ASI inventory associates its sculptural style with Pallava and Pandya comparisons, making it relevant to the study of carving, regional artistic exchange and the creation of religious space from living rock.","full":"The ASI monument inventory hosted by IGNCA identifies the boulder-cut cave at Vizhinjam and describes Shiva as Kirata and a Shiva–Parvathy representation. It proposes a date around the eighth century through stylistic comparison. These observations support research into architectural excavation and sculptural connections across southern India. The proposed chronology is an archaeological interpretation rather than an exact construction record, and individual carved surfaces still require direct documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-306","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-306","src":"https://ignca.gov.in/Asi_data/88324.pdf","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":8.38182,"lng":76.99161,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thangassery Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kollam","state":"Kerala","about":"Thangassery Fort preserves fragments of a Portuguese coastal defensive structure later used by Dutch and British powers. Its gateway, walls and tower remains connect material evidence with the history of artillery, seaborne trade and the changing control of Kerala's maritime settlements.","full":"The Kerala Tourism heritage essay describes the fort's original towers, bastions and seafront entrance, then traces changes of possession among European powers. It identifies an arched gateway, wall fragments and tower remains in the surviving site. These features provide a basis for studying the relationship between coastal topography and military design. The record concerns the fort, rather than separately listing each ruin, and its construction sequence needs archaeological confirmation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-307","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-307","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2020/thangassery-fort-ruins-legacy/1026","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.88171,"lng":76.56849,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kaduthuruthi Valiapalli","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kaduthuruthi Valiapalli is a historic church documented for a large stone cross at its entrance. The monument provides a focused example for studying how a carved religious marker defines the approach to a worship place and carries community memory in Kerala's Christian landscape.","full":"The official destination page locates the church on the route between Ettumanoor and Vaikom and draws attention to a large cross described as sculpted from a single stone. That feature is a concrete starting point for investigating carving and the role of forecourt monuments in church settings. The page's early foundation claim is not independently verified. The cross's date, workshops and relationship with later building phases need more detailed documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-308","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-308","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/kaduthuruthi-valiapalli/481/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Punalur Suspension Bridge","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Punalur","state":"Kerala","about":"Punalur Suspension Bridge spans the Kallada using iron chains, masonry supports and a timber deck. The nineteenth-century structure connects Travancore's transport history with the practical coordination of imported metal, local construction work and river crossing, making it a distinctive example of Kerala's engineering heritage.","full":"Kerala Tourism's bridge essay describes the structure as a combination of iron, timber and masonry, with chains anchored through large wells. It places the bridge within nineteenth-century Travancore and notes its later conservation as a monument. These details support study of load-bearing systems and the material infrastructure of transport. The source's picturesque explanation of animal exclusion is not needed to establish the bridge's engineering significance or its changing use.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-309","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-309","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2020/punalur-suspension-bridge/1022","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.01805,"lng":76.9276,"approx":false},
{"name":"Aruvithura St. George Forane Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Aruvithura","state":"Kerala","about":"Aruvithura St. George Forane Church stands beside the Meenachil and is documented for a Gothic-style cruciform building and a prominent bell tower. The site connects architectural orientation and religious images with the continuing identity of a Syrian Christian community in Kottayam.","full":"DTPC Kottayam describes the present church as a cruciform building completed in the twentieth century, with a tall Manimalika at its front. The account also records an older St. George image preserved and displayed during festivals. These features allow comparison between inherited objects and a later building setting. Early community foundation dates and stories about the image's arrival must be distinguished from the date of the present structure.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-310","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-310","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/aruvithura-church","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.68574,"lng":76.77782,"approx":false},
{"name":"Changanassery Pazhayapalli Mosque","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Changanassery Pazhayapalli Mosque preserves tombs associated with local religious figures and is a setting for a large communal feast. The place connects built memorial space with food preparation, offerings and the collective work through which an annual religious gathering is sustained.","full":"DTPC Kottayam identifies the mosque, its preserved Maqbaras and the Thangal Adiyanthiram feast. The account records devotees contributing ingredients for a communal meal. These details support inquiry into the relationship between a religious building, memorial practices and the organization of shared resources. They do not establish an exact age for the current structure or demonstrate uninterrupted continuity of every ritual; those questions require mosque records and local historical research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-311","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-311","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/pazhayapalli-mosque-changanassery","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Puthuppally St. George Orthodox Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Puthuppally","state":"Kerala","about":"Puthuppally St. George Orthodox Church is documented for a carved granite cross, painted interiors and bell towers. The combination of stone and metal crosses with architectural decoration provides material for studying the ways Syrian Christian religious imagery was made, displayed and renewed in Kerala.","full":"DTPC describes a granite cross with patterned recesses in its base, a bronze cross with a turtle-shaped base, wall paintings and two bell towers. It also records the use of a golden cross in a festival procession. These are concrete leads for comparing permanent exterior markers with objects moved in ceremony. The age and interpretation of each cross need object-specific evidence; the community's foundation history should not date every surviving element.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-312","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-312","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/st-george-orthodox-church-puthupally","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.55198,"lng":76.56745,"approx":false},
{"name":"Bharananganam St. Mary's Forane Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Bharananganam","state":"Kerala","about":"St. Mary's Forane Church at Bharananganam forms part of a pilgrimage setting beside the Meenachil associated with Saint Alphonsa. The church and nearby memorial spaces provide a starting point for studying how religious biography shapes the use and interpretation of a built local landscape.","full":"DTPC Kottayam places the Forane Church on the Meenachil River and associates the nearby shrine and convent with Alphonsa's life and remembrance. The account identifies preserved residential space as part of that memorial geography. This entry concerns the church setting rather than creating a separate record for each nearby memorial. Dates and the provenance of individual objects need church or conservation records, while devotional claims are not treated as historical or medical proof.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-313","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-313","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/st-marys-forane-church-bharananganam","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.70154,"lng":76.72697,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kudamaloor St. Mary's Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kottayam","state":"Kerala","about":"Kudamaloor St. Mary's Church preserves an older church alongside a later building and is documented for a distinctive painting of Christ. This layered setting supports study of how religious communities retain earlier structures, images and biographical associations while adapting their space to changing needs.","full":"DTPC Kottayam states that an older church was retained when a larger building was constructed in 1971. It also describes a Christ painting and a baptismal font associated with Alphonsa. These features allow inquiry into the reuse of buildings and the preservation of meaningful objects. The page's legendary account of royal foundation and its estimated painting age require independent corroboration; they are not accepted here as dates established through technical or archival study.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-314","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-314","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/st-marys-church-kudamaloor","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.6222,"lng":76.51407,"approx":false},
{"name":"Manarcad St. Mary's Cathedral","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Manarcadu","state":"Kerala","about":"Manarcad St. Mary's Cathedral is associated with inscribed memorial stones, a stone cross and repeated rebuilding. The combination of written traces, surviving objects and a later church setting makes it relevant to the study of community memory and the changing material form of a religious place.","full":"The DTPC account reports memorial stone inscriptions at Manarcad and describes architectural changes over successive renovations. It also identifies a stone cross and the cathedral's eight-day Marian observance. These details offer separate leads for epigraphic, architectural and community-history research. Dates proposed for the memorials should be checked against published readings, and the presence of an old object must not be taken to establish the age of the current building.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-315","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-315","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/st-marys-church-manarkad","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.59993,"lng":76.58496,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thazhathangadi Juma Masjid","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kummanam","state":"Kerala","about":"Thazhathangadi Juma Masjid is documented for timber construction, carved windows and written Arabic decoration. A stone washing tank and courtyard shadow clock add material evidence for studying the coordination of carpentry, water provision and timekeeping within a Kerala mosque's religious setting.","full":"DTPC highlights timber carving, roof motifs, a staircase and Arabic verses on the wall between the mosque's inner and outer parts. It also identifies a large stone tank and a shadow clock in the courtyard. These named features offer a starting point for studying practical and decorative elements together. The dating and operation of the clock, the tank's construction and the history of particular woodwork require direct documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-316","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-316","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/thazhathangadi-juma-masjid","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.59857,"lng":76.50572,"approx":false},
{"name":"Erumeli Vavar Juma Masjid","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Vavar Juma Masjid at Erumeli is a stopping place associated with the Sabarimala pilgrimage. The mosque's relationship with nearby Ayyappa shrines and ceremonial routes makes it relevant to the study of shared religious geography, movement and narratives connecting communities in Kerala.","full":"DTPC Kottayam describes Ayyappa pilgrims visiting the mosque before continuing towards Sabarimala. It also records nearby Ayyappa temples and the Chandanakudam and Pettathullal observances. These relationships can be studied through routes, gathering spaces and the changing use of the built setting. Accounts of Vavar's identity differ in religious tradition; no single version is presented here as an independently established biography or proof of a dated historical encounter.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-317","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-317","src":"https://www.dtpckottayam.com/destination/vavar-juma-masjid-erumeli","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kottayam Cheriyapally","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kottayam","state":"Kerala","about":"Kottayam Cheriyapally combines church architecture with elements associated with Kerala temple building and Portuguese forms. Its brick walls, granite pillars and narrative murals offer a setting for examining material craftsmanship and the adaptation of religious imagery across local and overseas architectural traditions.","full":"The Kerala Tourism Kumarakom account identifies the building's large brick walls, granite pillars and murals, describing a combination of Kerala and Portuguese architectural influences. It notes biblical and non-biblical painted subjects and a festival associated with agricultural seeds. These observations support research into building practices and the relationship between worship and local livelihoods. The contribution of particular workshops and the restoration history of painted surfaces need more precise documentary and conservation evidence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-318","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-318","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kumarakom/cheriyapally-kottayam.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.59757,"lng":76.5101,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kottayam Valiyapally","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Muttuchira","state":"Kerala","about":"Kottayam Valiyapally is documented for a Persian cross with Pahlavi writing and paintings on its altar and ceiling. The site links a local Knanaya church setting with evidence for written language, carved religious symbols and wider historical connections across the Indian Ocean.","full":"The official account identifies Valiyapally with the Knanaya community at Thazhathangadi and highlights Pahlavi inscriptions on a Persian cross. It also records altar and ceiling paintings. These are material leads for examining script, religious symbolism and the movement or reuse of objects. The account's tradition linking the cross to Saint Thomas is not treated as proven provenance; its date and journey require epigraphic and art-historical investigation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-319","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-319","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kumarakom/valiapally-kottayam.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.76301,"lng":76.49071,"approx":false},
{"name":"Niranam St. Mary's Orthodox Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Niranam","state":"Kerala","about":"Niranam St. Mary's Orthodox Church is a religious place associated with Kerala's Saint Thomas traditions and repeated rebuilding. Its architecture and community memory provide a starting point for studying how a church maintains a sense of continuity while its physical fabric changes over time.","full":"The destination account locates the church near Thiruvalla and describes elements associated with Kerala and Syrian Christian architectural traditions. It also records repeated rebuilding and an apostolic foundation tradition. The combination is relevant to the study of how religious history is represented through present structures. The inherited story of Saint Thomas is distinguished here from the dating of the surviving building, which requires architectural and documentary evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-320","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-320","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/niranam-pathanamthitta/129/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.34284,"lng":76.52746,"approx":false},
{"name":"Parumala St. Peter and St. Paul's Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Parumala","state":"Kerala","about":"Parumala Church contains the tomb of Saint Geevarghese Mar Gregorios and stands beside the Pamba. Its memorial role and annual commemoration connect a church building with religious biography, pilgrimage movement and the ways communities preserve the memory of a teacher in Kerala.","full":"The official Pathanamthitta brochure identifies the church on the Pamba and names the tomb of Mar Gregorios, commonly called Parumala Thirumeni. It records the annual Ormaperunnal associated with his remembrance. These facts support inquiry into how burial, biography and recurring gatherings shape the use of a religious site. The brochure is not a measured account of the building; construction phases and later changes need separate archival and architectural research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-321","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-321","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/images/ebooks/pdf/pathanamthitta.pdf","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.32478,"lng":76.53669,"approx":false},
{"name":"Manjinikkara Dayara Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kozhencherry","state":"Kerala","about":"Manjinikkara Dayara Church is a hilltop pilgrimage place associated with the tomb of Patriarch Mor Ignatius Elias III. The site connects Kerala's Syrian Christian history with wider ecclesiastical networks and provides a setting for studying how memorial architecture and recurring gatherings sustain religious remembrance.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the church near Omallur and associates the tomb with Mor Ignatius Elias III, who died during his visit to India in 1932. It also records the Orma Perunnal gathering. These are leads for investigating religious networks and the built setting of remembrance. The account does not establish the age of each architectural element, and devotional claims concerning the tomb are not treated as historical or therapeutic evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-322","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-322","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/manjinikkara-dayara-church/55/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.24898,"lng":76.74296,"approx":false},
{"name":"Edathua St. George Forane Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Edathua","state":"Kerala","about":"Edathua St. George Church stands beside the Pamba and is documented for a sculpted altar and distinctive representations of Saint George. Its images and festival traditions provide material for studying the adaptation of Christian iconography within Kerala's riverine church communities.","full":"Kerala Tourism's Christianity account describes the St. George image, its horse and the sculpted altar setting at Edathua. It also records the festival's processional life and Tamil-language worship. These details support inquiry into image making, multilingual participation and connections across regional boundaries. Stylistic labels and stories about the movement of older statues need art-historical corroboration; they should not be accepted as proof of the objects' age or provenance.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-323","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-323","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/christianity/edathua-church/66","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.36705,"lng":76.4752,"approx":false},
{"name":"Champakulam Kalloorkadu St. Mary's Basilica","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Champakulam Kalloorkadu St. Mary's Basilica stands beside the Pamba and belongs to Kerala's Syro-Malabar Christian tradition. Its associations with the Chembakassery rulers and regional ceremonies make it a place to investigate relationships among religious institutions, river settlements and community networks in Alappuzha.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the Basilica of St. Mary at Champakulam as a Syro-Malabar church on the Pamba and records connections with the Chembakassery rulers. It also links the church with regional ceremonial life. These statements offer a starting point for researching the institution's place in the surrounding river-settlement network. The page's implausible apostolic chronology is not adopted; foundation dates, architectural phases and individual objects require independent church and historical documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-324","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-324","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/champakulam-church/316/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Arthunkal St. Andrew's Basilica","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Arthunkal","state":"Kerala","about":"Arthunkal St. Andrew's Basilica is a coastal church associated with the Feast of Saint Sebastian and a procession to the seashore. The setting connects a religious building with maritime settlement and ceremonial routes, offering material for studying how worship extends across a coastal landscape.","full":"The official festival account identifies the church's Portuguese missionary associations, subsequent stone reconstruction and Saint Sebastian observances. It describes taking the image towards the seashore and back. These are starting points for studying the relationship between a permanent church and a recurring route through its surrounding settlement. The dates of individual buildings and images require separate evidence, and beliefs about protection or healing are not accepted as medical conclusions.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-325","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-325","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/campaigns/kerala365/arthunkal-perunnal-alappuzha","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.66112,"lng":76.30042,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thumpoly St. Thomas Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thumpoly","state":"Kerala","about":"Thumpoly St. Thomas Church is a coastal parish landmark associated with Portuguese-period religious history and an annual Marian observance. Its local place-name traditions and ceremonial role offer a starting point for studying the relationship between a church, settlement identity and community memory.","full":"The official festival inventory identifies the church with Saint Thomas and the Blessed Virgin Mary, records an annual celebration and preserves traditions linking Thumpoly's name to the parish. It also associates the place with Portuguese religious activity. These statements ground an introductory record about settlement memory and continuing worship. They do not independently date the surviving church or establish the origin of its name; architectural phases and documentary references need further research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-326","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-326","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/1000festivals/assets/uploads/pdf/1530690314-0.pdf","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.52029,"lng":76.31833,"approx":false},
{"name":"Sivagiri Mutt","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Varkala","state":"Kerala","about":"Sivagiri Mutt preserves places associated with Sree Narayana Guru's residence, teaching and remembrance. The campus connects religious architecture with Kerala's social-reform history, offering a physical setting through which to investigate the relationship between learning, equality and the public life of a modern teacher.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the Guru's resting place, residence and Sharada shrine within the Sivagiri campus and links the site with the Sree Narayana Dharma Sangham. These features allow study of how a teacher's ideas are remembered through buildings and recurring gatherings. The relationship between domestic space, memorial and shrine forms part of the campus's educational history. Dates and the changing use of each structure need institutional and architectural documentation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-327","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-327","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/varkala/sivagiri-mutt","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.73908,"lng":76.73247,"approx":false},
{"name":"Beemapally Dargah Shareef","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Beemapally Dargah Shareef contains tombs associated with Beema Beevi and her son and is a setting for the Chandanakudam observance. The site connects memorial space, coastal community identity and processional offerings in the religious landscape of Kerala's capital.","full":"The destination account identifies the Dargah with Beema Beevi and Maheen Aboobaker and describes their tombs and annual commemoration. It also preserves narratives about their arrival from Arabia. These are useful leads for studying how family memory, pilgrimage and public ceremony are connected at a coastal shrine. Claims of miraculous healing or exact lineage are matters of devotion and tradition, not independently demonstrated medical or genealogical facts.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-328","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-328","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/beemapalli/87/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Vettukad Madre de Deus Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Vettukad Madre de Deus Church is a coastal pilgrimage centre associated with the Feast of Christ the King. Its annual observances provide a named setting for studying how religious images, public processions and the organization of a gathering connect a church with its surrounding community.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism festival listing identifies the Madre de Deus Church within the Latin Catholic Archdiocese of Trivandrum and describes the Christ the King feast. It records worship, special prayers and large public participation. These observations support research into the spatial and material organization of a recurring coastal gathering. The source's miracle narratives and attendance estimates are not independently verified, and the present building's phases require a separate architectural history.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-329","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-329","src":"https://utsav.gov.in/public/view-event/vettukadu-feast-1","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Palayam Juma Masjid","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Palayam Juma Masjid developed from a smaller nineteenth-century mosque in the centre of Thiruvananthapuram. Its continuing prayer-hall setting and history of renovation provide a starting point for studying how an urban religious building responds to a changing neighbourhood and congregation.","full":"The official Kerala Tourism photo record identifies the Palayam mosque's earlier form in 1813 and notes subsequent changes and renovations. This gives a limited but traceable basis for examining the continuity of an urban worship place through alteration. The photograph and caption are not a measured architectural history. Details of materials, designers and the chronology of particular additions require mosque records and conservation evidence before stronger construction claims are made.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-330","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-330","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/islam-in-kerala/photo-gallery/palayam-juma-masjid-prayer-hall/3228","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.50357,"lng":76.95056,"approx":false},
{"name":"Karumadikkuttan Buddha Site","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Karumadikkuttan is a Buddha image carved in black granite and preserved at Karumadi near Ambalappuzha. The sculpture provides material evidence for studying Buddhist religious imagery in Kerala, while its present protection raises questions about the conservation and public interpretation of an archaeological object in place.","full":"The Alappuzha district administration identifies the figure as a black-granite Buddha and notes protection by the Kerala Archaeology Department. Those observations ground a record about a surviving sculptural object and its site. The page offers an approximate date, but the history of discovery, damage and placement needs specialist documentation. Religious stories of healing associated with the figure are not evidence of medical effectiveness and are not endorsed here.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-331","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-331","src":"https://alappuzha.nic.in/en/tourist-place/karumadikuttan/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mavelikkara Buddha Monument","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Buddha monument at Mavelikkara is a preserved sculptural landmark maintained by Kerala's Archaeology Department. It provides a named site for investigating Buddhist material remains and the ways an older religious image is incorporated into the public memory and everyday landscape of a present town.","full":"The Mavelikkara Village Office account identifies the Buddha statue and Mandapam as a local monument maintained by the Archaeology Department. It associates the area with a history of Buddhism but does not provide a formal archaeological context for the sculpture. The object can therefore introduce questions about style, relocation and public display, while its date, findspot and sequence of conservation require documentation beyond the village profile.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-332","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-332","src":"https://landrevenue.kerala.gov.in/core/Office_websites/indexor.php?nm=123Mavelikkaravillageoffice","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Poonjar Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Poonjar","state":"Kerala","about":"Poonjar Palace is documented for timber architecture, varied building materials and a collection including grain measures, palm-leaf work and furniture. The complex connects a royal domestic setting with material records of administration, measurement, transport and artistic practice in central Travancore.","full":"The Kerala Tourism palace essay describes wood construction and the use of granite, laterite and clay tiles. It records furniture, grain measures, palm-leaf engravings and a palanquin among the contents, alongside carved stone lamps in the associated religious setting. These objects invite study of everyday knowledge as well as patronage. The palace and its collection are treated together, while individual dates, makers and provenance require catalogue-level investigation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-333","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-333","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2023/poonjar-palace-kottayam/1362","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.67871,"lng":76.81424,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thanneermukkom Salinity Barrage","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thanneermukkom Barrage crosses Vembanad between Thanneermukkom and Vechoor to regulate saline-water entry towards cultivated land. Its shutters and navigation locks provide a specific case for studying modern water engineering within a long-used wetland landscape, including the competing needs of cultivation, movement and ecology.","full":"Kerala's Irrigation Department describes staged construction of the barrage, its salinity-control purpose and the provision of shutters and navigation locks. It also discusses later work on the central section. The structure can be studied as a modern intervention in a landscape shaped by farming and waterways. It is not presented as an ancient indigenous technology or an unqualified environmental success; ecological effects and operational decisions require hydrological and community evidence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-334","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-334","src":"https://www.irrigation.kerala.gov.in/thanneermukkom","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Alappuzha Town Canal Network","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Alappuzha's town canal network includes the Vadai, Commercial and connecting waterways used in the settlement's water-transport system. The channels and their maintained banks provide a specific urban setting for studying the relationship between drainage, navigation, engineering works and the everyday use of water in Kerala.","full":"The Kerala Irrigation Infrastructure Development Corporation describes work on named town canals, including Vadai, Commercial and several connecting channels. Its project account records dewatering, cleaning, deepening and bank strengthening. These operations reveal the continuing work needed to maintain an inherited waterway network. The entry concerns the town canals rather than the entire Kuttanad agricultural system; the original excavation dates and the effects of recent interventions require separate historical and environmental investigation.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-335","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-335","src":"https://kiidc.kerala.gov.in/beautification-of-canals-in-alappuzha-2/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"St. Francis Church, Fort Kochi","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kochi","state":"Kerala","about":"St. Francis Church preserves a timber-framed tiled roof and a gabled facade within Fort Kochi's colonial streetscape. Its changing Portuguese, Dutch and British associations make the building a place to examine architectural adaptation and the material record of maritime contact.","full":"Incredible India describes St. Francis Church as a Portuguese Franciscan foundation, with a timber-framed gable roof covered by tiles and pinnacles flanking the facade. The building passed through different European administrations while remaining embedded in Kochi's religious landscape. Vasco da Gama's former burial location is marked inside. The surviving structure offers evidence for construction and commemoration, while its successive rebuilding phases require comparison with conservation records.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-336","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-336","src":"https://www.prod.incredibleindia.gov.in/content/incredible-india-v2/en/destinations/kochi/st-francis-church.html","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.96595,"lng":76.24107,"approx":false},
{"name":"Paradesi Synagogue","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kochi","state":"Kerala","about":"The Paradesi Synagogue at Mattancherry brings a brass-railed reading platform, glass chandeliers and a multilingual clock tower into one place of worship. Its fittings and inscriptions provide material for studying Jewish religious life, craftsmanship and exchange along the Malabar coast.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the Paradesi Synagogue as a Jewish heritage monument with a brass-railed pulpit, Belgian glass chandeliers and a clock tower whose faces use different numeral systems. The reading platform and interior organisation connect the building with the communal reading of scripture. These architectural and material features provide a starting point for investigating Kochi's Jewish communities without reducing their histories to a single, unchanging tradition.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-337","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-337","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/judaism/synagogue/heritage-monument","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.95748,"lng":76.2593,"approx":false},
{"name":"Santa Cruz Basilica","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Santa Cruz Basilica combines Gothic and Indo-European architectural features with painted ceilings, stained glass and an elaborately decorated altar. The church records how Christian narrative painting and building design were adapted by artists and craftspeople working in Kochi.","full":"The Kerala Tourism account distinguishes the earlier cathedral from the reconstructed basilica and identifies its twin spires, stained glass, painted interiors and altar paintings associated with Antonio Moscheni. A granite pillar from the earlier building survives as a relic. Rather than representing one untouched period, the church records rebuilding and changes of patronage. Its visual programme invites close study of the transmission of Christian stories through architectural ornament and painting.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-338","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-338","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kochi/santa-cruz-basilica-fort-kochi.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chinese Fishing Nets, Fort Kochi","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kochi","state":"Kerala","about":"The shore-based Chinese fishing nets at Fort Kochi use timber and bamboo frames, ropes and stone counterweights to lower and raise a broad net. Their operation makes balance, coordinated labour and practical fishing knowledge visible within a working coastal landscape.","full":"Kerala Tourism explains that these cantilevered nets work through balance: stones tied to ropes counterweight a timber-and-bamboo frame carrying the net. A team lowers it into the water and lifts the catch after a short interval. The installation links material selection, leverage and coordinated movement with observations of coastal conditions. Accounts of the precise person and date of introduction vary, so the mechanism is documented separately from origin legends.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-339","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-339","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kochi/chinese-fishing-nets-kochi.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.96819,"lng":76.24217,"approx":false},
{"name":"Dutch Cemetery, Fort Kochi","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kochi","state":"Kerala","about":"The Dutch Cemetery preserves granite and laterite tombs, an inscribed entrance and epitaphs associated with Dutch and British residents of Kochi. These funerary objects form a material archive for studying stoneworking, languages and community histories within a colonial port.","full":"The Kerala Tourism description identifies the enclosed cemetery, the year inscribed on its entrance pillar, and tombs made from granite and red laterite. Some memorials carry inscriptions in old Dutch script. The records connect people who lived and died in Kochi with wider maritime histories. Reading the cemetery as an archive requires attention to weathered inscriptions, burial registers and the unequal social world represented by colonial commemoration.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-340","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-340","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kochi/dutch-cemetery-fort-kochi.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.96419,"lng":76.23827,"approx":false},
{"name":"Bolgatty Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Ernakulam","state":"Kerala","about":"Bolgatty Palace is a former Dutch residence overlooking the Kochi backwaters, later adapted as a heritage hotel. Its tiled roofs, large halls, pillars and timber-framed windows provide a setting for examining colonial domestic architecture and the reuse of historic buildings.","full":"Kerala Tourism traces Bolgatty Palace from a Dutch residence through its use by British governors to its management as a heritage hotel by KTDC. The account highlights large halls, strong pillars, tiled roofing and wooden-framed glass windows. These features can be studied as responses to residence, display and climate in a waterfront setting. Later hospitality alterations should be distinguished from the palace's earlier architectural fabric and documented construction phases.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-341","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-341","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kochi/bolgatty-palace-kochi.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.98512,"lng":76.26758,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thrikkakara Vamanamoorthy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thrikkakara Vamanamoorthy Temple is associated with Onam and the Vamana narrative. Engravings, ceremonial observances and the shared festival feast connect this shrine with visual storytelling, seasonal memory and the organisation of collective ritual life in Kerala.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Vamana as the presiding deity and describes the temple's close association with Onam. Its account notes engravings around the sanctum and a feast served during the celebrations. Together, the physical shrine and recurring observances offer evidence for examining how stories, calendrical knowledge and community participation are transmitted. The age assigned to particular engravings must be established independently rather than inferred from the antiquity of the associated narrative.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-342","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-342","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/ernakulam/thrikakkara-vamanamoorthy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chottanikkara Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kanayannur","state":"Kerala","about":"Chottanikkara Bhagavathy Temple is a continuing place of worship where the goddess is addressed in different forms over the day. Its daily sequence and Makam observance offer a focused setting for studying ritual time, naming and devotional traditions.","full":"The Kerala Tourism page describes Rajarajeswari worshipped as Saraswathy in the morning, Lakshmi at noon and Durga in the evening. It also identifies Makam Thozhal as a major observance linked to the Malayalam calendar. These are accounts of religious practice, useful for exploring how daily and annual cycles organise a sacred site. Historical building phases and the meanings assigned by different participants require further documentation beyond this introductory description.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-343","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-343","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/ernakulam/chottanikkara","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":9.93313,"lng":76.3931,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thiruvairanikulam Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thekkumbhagom","state":"Kerala","about":"Thiruvairanikulam Mahadeva Temple contains carved wooden figures and a pavilion ceiling illustrating divine battles, festivals and planetary imagery. Its seasonal opening of the goddess shrine connects architectural decoration with an annual cycle of ritual access and remembrance.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes wood carvings of a vyali in the sanctum and narrative images on the Namaskara Mandapam ceiling, including Deva-Asura battles and the Navagraha. The goddess shrine is associated with a limited annual opening during Nadathurappu. The site brings together carpentry, visual narrative and calendrical practice. A detailed record would need to distinguish individual panels, conservation interventions and present ritual arrangements without assuming that all belong to the same historical period.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-344","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-344","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/ernakulam/thiruvairanikulam-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.11987,"lng":76.41666,"approx":false},
{"name":"Ernakulathappan Shiva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Ernakulathappan Temple is a Shiva shrine in central Ernakulam with sculptured sanctum walls and copper-tile ceiling decoration. The building provides a place to examine how metalwork, carved imagery and regional temple planning are combined in an urban religious setting.","full":"Kerala Tourism highlights the temple's Kerala architectural style, sculptures on the sanctum walls and ceiling carvings made with copper tiles. The presiding deity is Shiva, known locally as Ernakulathappan. These specific material features provide a basis for studying relations between metal sheeting, sculptural surfaces and the arrangement of sacred space. The source does not establish the authorship or date of each decorative element, which remain matters for architectural and conservation research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-345","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-345","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/ernakulam/ernakulathappan","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Poornathrayeesa Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Poornathrayeesa Temple at Tripunithura combines copper roofing, brass-covered surfaces, granite and carved timber. Its two-storeyed gateway and pillared pavilion make it a useful site for examining how several craft materials are coordinated within Kerala temple construction.","full":"The Kerala Tourism account describes a Vishnu temple with copper plates, wooden panels and granite tiles. It notes brass sheets around the sanctum, a copper-plated roof and a gateway pavilion supported on carved wooden pillars. These details make the building a record of interactions between carpentry, metal sheathing and stonework. Its setting in the former Cochin royal centre also invites research on patronage, while specific workshop histories and rebuilding sequences remain unverified.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-346","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-346","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/ernakulam/poornathrayeesa","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Uliyannoor Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Uliyannoor Mahadeva Temple is noted for a circular sanctum whose wooden beams converge beneath the roof. Its association with the legendary master carpenter Perunthachan connects an observable structural form with stories about skilled design and architectural knowledge.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the circular sreekovil and describes sixty-eight wooden beams converging beneath a single dome above the sanctum. The temple is dedicated to Shiva and is popularly attributed to Perunthachan. The roof assembly provides a concrete starting point for studying timber geometry, while the attribution belongs to architectural tradition. Measured drawings and conservation records are needed before drawing conclusions about structural dating, proportions or the identity of its builders.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-347","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-347","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/ernakulam/uliyannoor-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Iringole Sacred Grove and Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Iringole Kavu places a Bhagavathy shrine within a substantial wooded grove near Perumbavoor. The relationship between temple and forest offers a setting for studying sacred-landscape stewardship, local narratives and the ways religious practice can shape the use of land.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the Iringole Bhagavathy Temple as lying within a forested area near Perumbavoor and associates the deity with a Krishna narrative. The grove and shrine should be documented together as a cultural landscape rather than as an isolated building. The record invites examination of customary care, access and environmental knowledge, while avoiding assumptions about species diversity or conservation success without a site-specific ecological survey and community consultation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-348","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-348","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/ernakulam/iringole-kavu","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Adi Shankara Shrine at Kalady","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kalady","state":"Kerala","about":"The Adi Shankara shrine at Kalady commemorates the philosopher within a riverbank pilgrimage landscape. Its memorial form and relationship with nearby worship spaces offer a starting point for studying how philosophical lineages are anchored in places and shared narratives.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Kalady on the Periyar as the traditional birthplace of Adi Shankara and describes a shrine built in his memory near the river. The site connects Advaita Vedanta with pilgrimage and the commemoration of a teacher. The riverbank setting places its memorial architecture in relation to water and pilgrimage movement. Stories associated with the nearby ghat are preserved as tradition, not literal biographical proof.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-349","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-349","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/kaladi-angamali-ernakulam/184/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.16861,"lng":76.43512,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kallil Bhagavathy Rock Shrine","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kallil Bhagavathy Temple occupies a massive rock formation reached by a long flight of steps. The relationship between rock, carved sacred space and continuing worship provides a distinctive case for examining construction choices within Kerala's religious landscapes.","full":"The Kerala Tourism account identifies Kallil as a rock-cut shrine dedicated to Bhagavathy, with steps leading to the sacred space beneath a large rock. Its material setting is central to the site's identity. Examining the formation and shrine together can help distinguish adapted natural surfaces from deliberately worked stone. Proposed early dates and histories of religious transformation require archaeological documentation beyond the introductory tourism article used for this discovery record.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-350","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-350","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2024/kallil-bhagavati-temple-ernakulam-a-spiritual-haven/1409","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Palakkad Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Palakkad","state":"Kerala","about":"Palakkad Fort combines granite walls, bastions, a moat and a controlled entrance in a square defensive plan. Associated with Hyder Ali and later administrations, it provides material evidence for examining military construction and the strategic organisation of an inland gateway.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes a square fort with granite walls, bastions and a moat, tracing its construction under Hyder Ali and its subsequent uses. The enclosure and entrance arrangements reveal how terrain, surveillance and movement were coordinated in a fortified site. The fort grounds later became a public gathering space. Its layered history should be read through successive alterations, rather than assigning every surviving feature to a single ruler or construction campaign.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-351","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-351","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/palakkad-fort/22","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":10.76366,"lng":76.65681,"approx":false},
{"name":"Jainimedu Jain Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Jainimedu Jain Temple preserves a granite-built place of worship dedicated to Chandraprabha in Palakkad. Its Tirthankara and Yakshini images provide evidence for studying Jain religious presence, sculptural traditions and connections between Kerala and neighbouring regions of southern India.","full":"The Kerala Tourism account identifies granite construction, Chandraprabha as the principal deity, and images associated with several Tirthankaras and Yakshinis. It also records the temple's relationship with Jain settlement in Palakkad. The site supports research on religious mobility and the arrangement of sacred images. Accounts of founders, migration and later damage require corroboration, while current worship and conservation conditions should not be assumed from an introductory historical description.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-352","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-352","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/jain-temple-jainamedu/28","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kalpathy Agraharam","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Palakkad","state":"Kerala","about":"Kalpathy Agraharam comprises streets of closely related houses leading toward temples beside the Kalpathy River. The settlement brings domestic planning, migration history and a shared chariot route together, showing how a neighbourhood can sustain both everyday life and ceremonial movement.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the settlement as a combination of Chathapuram, Govindarajapuram, Old Kalpathy and New Kalpathy, with similar houses lining streets that lead to temples. It associates the area with settlers from Thanjavur and the annual chariot festival. The built arrangement invites study of thresholds, streets and collective space. Particular migration dates and the chronology of individual houses need documentary checking rather than being assumed uniform across the entire neighbourhood.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-353","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-353","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/kalpathy-agraharam/27","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.78615,"lng":76.65114,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thiruvegappura Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"koorkaparamba","state":"Kerala","about":"Thiruvegappura Temple combines three principal devotional centres and three flagstaffs within one complex. Its koothambalam, or temple theatre, makes the site relevant to the study of sacred planning, performance spaces and the coordination of ritual activities.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Shiva, Vishnu and Sankaranarayana within the same complex and notes three flagstaffs alongside a koothambalam. The coexistence of these features provides a basis for examining how a temple accommodates different forms of worship and performance. Its calendar includes several distinct observances. A measured architectural record and local performance histories would help establish the relationships between the theatre, shrines and later alterations without relying on origin legends as chronology.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-354","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-354","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/palakkad/thiruvegappura","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.88018,"lng":76.11655,"approx":false},
{"name":"Panniyur Varahamurthy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kumbidi","state":"Kerala","about":"Panniyur Varahamurthy Temple is associated with unfinished structures and stories of the master carpenter Perunthachan. The surviving work and its narrative setting provide a place to explore how architectural skill, incompletion and memory are represented in Kerala's temple traditions.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes visible unfinished structures within the Panniyur complex and links them to a legend about Perunthachan being prevented from completing a splendid temple. The presiding deity is Varahamurthy. The account is useful for documenting the relationship between built fabric and stories about expertise. It does not establish who designed the surviving structures, why work stopped, or their precise construction dates; those questions require architectural examination and documentary comparison.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-355","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-355","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/palakkad/panniyur-varahamurthy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.82649,"lng":76.05471,"approx":false},
{"name":"Killikkurissi Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Killikkurissi Mahadeva Temple at Lakkidi is noted for its carved wooden architecture. The Shiva shrine offers a focused starting point for examining the place of timber workmanship in Kerala's sacred buildings and the stories attached to their foundation.","full":"The Kerala Tourism description places the Shiva temple in Lakkidi and specifically draws attention to its carefully carved wooden architecture. It also records a traditional association with Sage Suka. The documented timber work provides a material starting point for examining tools, motifs and maintenance, although the introductory account does not identify individual workshops. The foundation story should be considered separately from evidence for the age of particular beams, panels or rebuilding phases.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-356","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-356","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/palakkad/killikkurissi-mahadeva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thiruvalathur Randu Moorthy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thiruvalathur Randu Moorthy Temple combines worship of Annapoorneswari and Mahishasura Mardini with a koothambalam and a large mizhavu. The historic riverside complex connects sacred planning with the architectural and instrumental requirements of Kerala's performance traditions.","full":"Kerala Tourism locates the temple beside the Sokanasini and identifies two forms of the Mother Goddess as its principal deities. It highlights both a temple theatre and a large mizhavu percussion instrument. Read together, these features offer a basis for studying the material setting of performance within a sacred complex. The source does not establish the instrument's maker, age or present use, so those details remain open to specialist and community documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-357","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-357","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/palakkad/thiruvalathur-randumoorthy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Olappamanna Mana","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Vellinezhi","state":"Kerala","about":"Olappamanna Mana at Vellinezhi is an ancestral house associated with Kathakali, literature, music and Sanskrit learning. The residence provides a setting for examining how domestic space and patronage supported the transmission of artistic and scholarly knowledge in Kerala.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Olappamanna Mana as an ancestral Namboodiri residence with a long association with Kathakali, percussion, music, literature and Vedic education. Its account connects the house with performers and writers, including Pattikkamthoti Ravunni Menon. The site therefore invites study of how training and patronage were organised within a household setting. Detailed architectural measurements, family records and performer histories are needed to establish particular episodes of that transmission.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-358","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-358","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/olappamanna-mna-palakkad/559/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.90336,"lng":76.34419,"approx":false},
{"name":"Vadakkumnathan Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kottappuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Vadakkumnathan Temple combines stone and timber construction, carved wooden figures and mural painting within a walled precinct in Thrissur. Its surviving fabric and conservation history provide a substantial setting for studying Kerala temple design and the care of historic materials.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies wood and stone as principal materials and highlights murals, wooden sculptures and intricate carpentry at Vadakkumnathan. The temple occupies a large walled precinct in the centre of Thrissur and has received recognition for architectural conservation. Its fabric can be examined through the relationships between roofs, enclosures and decorated surfaces. Conservation documentation is essential for distinguishing old material, repairs and replacements rather than assuming an entirely unchanged ancient structure.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-359","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-359","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/vadakkumnathan","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.52436,"lng":76.21448,"approx":false},
{"name":"Guruvayur Krishna Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Guruvayur","state":"Kerala","about":"Guruvayur Krishna Temple brings a nalambalam, lighting pillar and painted sanctum into a continuing ritual complex. Its spatial organisation, mural surfaces and calibrated offerings provide material for studying relationships between architecture, visual narrative and everyday devotional practice.","full":"The Kerala Tourism page identifies Kerala-style construction, a surrounding nalambalam, a balikkal and a deepastambham. It also notes murals around the sanctum and the weighing ceremony called thulabharam. These features connect spatial design and painted imagery with repeated acts of worship. A closer architectural record should separate surviving fabric from later alterations, and accounts of offerings should describe cultural practice without implying that every participant assigns the same meaning to it.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-360","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-360","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/guruvayoor","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.59429,"lng":76.03941,"approx":false},
{"name":"Mammiyur Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mammiyur Mahadeva Temple is noted for murals depicting divine figures and episodes from Hindu narratives. The Shiva shrine near Guruvayur offers a focused setting for examining religious iconography and the relationship between painted surfaces and continuing worship.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Shiva worshipped as Uma Maheshwara, with Parvathy beside him, and highlights mural paintings containing mythological episodes. The site provides material for studying how narrative and devotional images occupy temple interiors. Identifying particular scenes, pigments and painting phases would require a panel-by-panel record. The short introductory account supports discovery of the site but does not establish workshop authorship, original colour recipes or the full history of its conservation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-361","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-361","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/mammiyoor","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Koodalmanikyam Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Koodalmanikyam Temple at Irinjalakuda combines a circular copper-roofed sanctum, carved gateway and koothambalam. These features make it a significant place to examine the coordination of temple carpentry, metal roofing, performance space and the devotional geography of Bharata worship.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Bharata as the temple's deity and describes an impressive eastern gateway, delicate carvings, a circular sanctum with copper roofing and a temple theatre. It also places the shrine within the Nalambalam pilgrimage. The architectural elements and pilgrimage connection can be examined together without treating them as one historical layer. Measured documentation and conservation records are needed to establish how the gateway, theatre and sanctum developed over time.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-362","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-362","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/koodalmanikyam","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Triprayar Rama Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Triprayar Rama Temple preserves wood carvings, murals and sculptures depicting the Ramayana. The shrine links religious storytelling with crafted surfaces and a wider network of ceremonial visits, making it relevant to the study of Kerala's visual and ritual knowledge.","full":"The Ministry of Tourism's account identifies the four-armed Rama image and describes wood carvings, murals and sculpture illustrating Ramayana scenes. It also records the shrine's connection with the gathering of deities at Arattupuzha. These details support a reading of the temple as a place where narrative, image making and ceremonial movement meet. The dates and makers of individual artworks need specialist documentation rather than being inferred from the age of the associated epic.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-363","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-363","src":"https://utsav.gov.in/public/view-event/sree-rama-navami","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thiruvanchikulam Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Sringapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Thiruvanchikulam Mahadeva Temple contains a sixteen-pillared prayer pavilion and murals illustrating Hindu narratives. The shrine near Kodungallur connects regional temple construction, painted storytelling and royal devotional histories within the wider Muziris heritage landscape of central Kerala.","full":"The Muziris Heritage account describes Kerala-style architecture, a Namaskara Mandapam with sixteen pillars and a collection of mural paintings. It links Mahadeva worship with the Cochin royal family and the history of the Chera region. These associations provide directions for architectural and historical research. Particular building phases and mural dates should be checked independently, and devotional accounts of ceremonies should remain distinct from evidence about the age of the surviving fabric.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-364","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-364","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/muziris/religious-sites/thiruvanchikulam-mahadeva-temple","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.21808,"lng":76.20111,"approx":false},
{"name":"Peruvanam Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Cherpu","state":"Kerala","about":"Peruvanam Mahadeva Temple juxtaposes circular and multi-storeyed square sanctums within one precinct. Its carved woodwork, murals and distinctive elevated shrine make it a place to study variations in Kerala temple geometry, while its surroundings support major percussion traditions.","full":"INTACH Thrissur's heritage-walk account identifies two Shiva shrines: a circular Irattayappan sanctum with carvings and murals, and the multi-storeyed Madathilappan shrine with an octagonal upper form. It also describes intersecting approaches to the elevated shrine. These contrasts provide concrete material for comparing sacred geometry and circulation. The report connects the complex with Pooram traditions, while foundation legends and claims about the first performance of particular musical forms need separate historical investigation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-365","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-365","src":"https://www.intachthrissur.org/peruvanam-walk/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.43737,"lng":76.212,"approx":false},
{"name":"Kadavallur Rama Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kokkur","state":"Kerala","about":"Kadavallur Rama Temple is recognised for carved wooden bracket images around its sanctum. These figurative elements connect the shaping of architectural supports with sculptural expression, offering material evidence for studying Kerala's temple carpentry and the conservation of decorated wood.","full":"The National Monuments Authority inventory lists twenty-nine wooden bracket images on the outer wall of the Vishnu temple's sanctum at Kadavallur, together with other works of art in the shrine. The protected elements provide a specific material focus for studying carved timber rather than treating the building only as a general monument. Close recording can investigate figure types, joints, weathering and repairs. Individual sculpture dates and workshop identities require further architectural and conservation evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-366","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-366","src":"https://nma.gov.in/showfile.php?lang=1&level=1&lid=1276&ls_id=965&nma_type=0","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.72998,"lng":76.06954,"approx":true},
{"name":"Eyyal Burial Caves","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Pannithadam","state":"Kerala","about":"The burial caves at Eyyal form part of Thrissur's megalithic landscape. Their survival within a settled village offers material for investigating funerary rock cutting and the practical challenges of conserving archaeological remains beside everyday homes, institutions and local roads.","full":"The National Monuments Authority's site documentation locates the protected burial caves in Eyyal village and places them within a wider regional distribution of megalithic monuments. It describes their setting among residences, vegetation and local institutions. The caves provide a starting point for examining the relationship between worked rock and funerary practice. A fuller interpretation requires excavation records and measured documentation; present settlement features should not be projected back onto the ancient landscape.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-367","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-367","src":"https://www.nma.gov.in/showfile.php?lang=1&level=2&lid=2025&ls_id=1635&nma_type=0","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":10.66855,"lng":76.11777,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kattakampal Burial Cave","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kattakampal Burial Cave contains several chambers reached from a shared court cut into laterite. Its plan reveals deliberate spatial organisation and skilled rock working, while its position beside a village market makes archaeological conservation part of a living settlement.","full":"The National Monuments Authority describes a multi-chambered megalithic cave whose outer court provides access to chambers in different directions. Steps are cut into laterite, and the chambers contain benches beneath horizontal ceilings. The document also records the monument's location along a commercial road. These observations support investigation of rock-cutting techniques and funerary planning, while excavation evidence is needed before assigning specific activities or social meanings to individual internal features.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-368","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-368","src":"https://www.nma.gov.in/showfile.php?lang=1&level=2&lid=2023&ls_id=1633&nma_type=0","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Arattupuzha Sastha Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thrissur","state":"Kerala","about":"Arattupuzha Sastha Temple is a Kerala-style shrine closely associated with a regional gathering of deities and percussion performances. The temple offers a setting for studying how sacred buildings, shared routes and neighbouring communities sustain a recurring ceremonial landscape.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the shrine's traditional architectural style and its close relationship with Arattupuzha Pooram. The significance of the site extends beyond an isolated structure to the movement of deities and participants between neighbouring temples. This makes spatial organisation and cultural participation useful research themes. Claims that the temple or festival is the oldest of its kind require historical corroboration, while present event arrangements should be documented through local custodians and performers.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-369","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-369","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/arattupuzha","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.4183,"lng":76.22996,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thiruvilwamala Vilwadrinatha Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Pambady","state":"Kerala","about":"Vilwadrinatha Temple at Thiruvilwamala contains two square, double-storeyed sanctums associated with Rama and Lakshmana. The hilltop complex offers a setting for comparing shrine forms, stone images and the arrangement of sacred buildings within a prominent landscape.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes two square, double-storeyed sreekovils and associated anakkottil structures at this hilltop temple. Rama and Lakshmana are identified as the presiding deities, with a distinctive stone image of Rama. The paired shrine arrangement provides evidence for studying relationships between deity, building form and circulation. Local stories about the nearby landscape should be recorded as tradition, while material identification and construction chronology require architectural or conservation analysis.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-370","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-370","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/thiruvilwamala-vilwadrinatha","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.7406,"lng":76.43108,"approx":false},
{"name":"Cherpu Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Cherpu","state":"Kerala","about":"Cherpu Bhagavathy Temple is a Durga shrine associated with both Peruvanam and Arattupuzha Pooram. Its connections with neighbouring sacred places offer a way to study a temple as a node within a shared ceremonial and community landscape.","full":"The Kerala Tourism entry identifies the temple's Durga dedication and its important role in two regional Pooram celebrations. The site can therefore be approached through the relationships between a local shrine and the wider network in which its deity participates. This record does not infer undocumented architectural details from that festival role. Building phases, procession routes, custodial histories and the experiences of different participants remain topics for site-specific and community-led investigation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-371","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-371","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/cherpu-bhagavathy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.43229,"lng":76.19993,"approx":false},
{"name":"Chowallor Shiva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Chowallor Shiva Temple combines a circular, two-storeyed sanctum with murals depicting Mahabharata episodes. Its sculptural elements and decorated walls offer a focused example of how built form, narrative painting and sacred iconography meet in Kerala temple design.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes a round two-storeyed sanctum and mural paintings on its outer walls, including scenes from the Mahabharata. It also identifies a tall balikkallu and stone images of the Sapthamathrukkal. These details support study of the relationships between narrative surfaces, sculptural groups and the temple's spatial core. The traditional age assigned to the building should be distinguished from evidence for the dating and conservation history of particular painted or sculpted elements.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-372","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-372","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/chowallor-shiva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Paramekkavu Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Paramekkavu Bhagavathy Temple is a major shrine in the ceremonial landscape of Thrissur Pooram. Its relationship with Vadakkumnathan Temple and the movement of processions between them provide a setting for examining public space, ritual coordination and the making of festival material culture.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Devi Vaishnavy as the temple's principal deity and describes its participation in Thrissur Pooram, including the procession toward Vadakkumnathan. The relationship between the two shrines shows how ceremonial space extends into the town. Documentation can connect building thresholds, streets and gathering grounds with the craft and organisational work required by the event. Its setting close to the historic temple precinct makes routes and gathering places central to understanding the site.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-373","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-373","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/paramekkavu-bhagavathi","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Cheraman Juma Masjid","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Lokamaleswaram","state":"Kerala","about":"Cheraman Juma Masjid preserves wooden interior elements within a repeatedly renovated mosque associated with Kerala's early Muslim history. Its architecture and lamp traditions provide a place to examine religious exchange while keeping foundation narratives distinct from evidence for surviving building phases.","full":"The Kerala Tourism account describes a blend of regional and Islamic architectural features and notes retained wooden steps, ceiling elements and an oil lamp. The mosque is associated with the Cheraman Perumal narrative and early connections across the Arabian Sea. These traditions form part of the site's cultural history, but do not independently establish the date of each surviving element. Interpretive facilities at the complex present this historical memory alongside continued worship.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-374","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-374","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/islam-in-kerala/pilgrim-centres/cheraman-masjid","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.21286,"lng":76.20209,"approx":false},
{"name":"Mala Synagogue","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Malapallipuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Mala Synagogue preserves an anteroom, tall prayer hall, central reading platform and upper women's gallery. The building offers material for studying Kerala Jewish religious space, timber circulation and the consequences of migration for the care and use of a community landmark.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the synagogue's azara, double-height sanctuary, central tebah and west-wall ark. A wooden balcony and stair lead to the upper area associated with women's seating and a second reading platform. The account also distinguishes a traditional early foundation from later rebuilding and the departure of the local congregation. These features support architectural study and community history without assuming that every surviving part dates from the same phase.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-375","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-375","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2022/mala-synagogue/1216","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.2403,"lng":76.26296,"approx":false},
{"name":"Palayur St. Thomas Church","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Palayur St. Thomas Church is a pilgrimage landmark associated with the apostolic traditions of Kerala Christianity. Its annual observances and walking route from Thrissur offer a place to study how sacred geography, communal memory and continuing worship are maintained.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the church near Chavakkad and describes annual observances dedicated to St. Thomas, together with the Lenten walking pilgrimage from Thrissur. The association with an apostolic foundation belongs to the community's religious tradition and should not be used to date the present building. The site invites documentation of pilgrimage routes, devotional objects and successive construction phases, with community testimony considered alongside material and written evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-376","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-376","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/event/palayur-church-festival/55","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kudakkallu Parambu, Cheramanangad","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kudakkallu Parambu at Cheramanangad contains several forms of megalithic funerary monument, including umbrella and hood stones. Their worked laterite shapes and close grouping provide evidence for examining stone construction, burial practices and the organisation of an archaeological landscape.","full":"DTPC Thrissur describes a group of megalithic burials with both kudakkallu and thoppikkallu forms. The umbrella structures combine shaped supporting blocks with a broad capstone, while other forms place a cap directly over the ground. Comparing these arrangements can inform research on cutting, assembly and funerary distinctions. Exact monument counts and cultural interpretations vary between introductory accounts, so a measured inventory and excavation evidence should guide further interpretation of the site.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-377","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-377","src":"https://www.dtpcthrissur.com/destination/kudakkallu-parambu","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ariyannur Umbrella Stones","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Ariyannur Umbrella Stones are megalithic burial monuments made from shaped laterite supports and broad capstones. Distinct from the nearby Vishnu temple, they provide material evidence for examining the geometry, assembly and funerary significance of Kerala's umbrella-stone tradition.","full":"Kerala Tourism distinguishes the Ariyannur temple from a nearby group of monumental umbrella stones and describes capstones carried by inclined laterite supports. The burial monument is protected by the Archaeological Survey of India. Its construction offers a basis for examining how stone was cut and fitted to create a stable covered form. The religious identity, exact dates and meanings of individual burials cannot be established from shape alone and require archaeological evidence.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-378","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-378","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/ariyannur-thrissur/78/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chowannur Burial Cave","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Chowannur Burial Cave contains a rounded chamber, vaulted roof, side benches and small circular blocks interpreted as vessel stands. The rock-cut funerary structure offers unusually legible evidence for examining how working stone created an organised interior for burial practices.","full":"The Kerala Revenue Department's village record describes an eastern recessed entrance, a chamber with curved sides and a hemispherical vault, benches to the north and south, and circular blocks along the west. It identifies the cave as a centrally protected megalithic monument. These details allow investigation of spatial planning and rock-cutting techniques. The proposed use of the blocks as vessel stands remains an interpretation and should be checked against archaeological documentation and comparable sites.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-379","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-379","src":"https://landrevenue.kerala.gov.in/core/Office_websites/about_village.php?nm=1526Chowannurvillageoffice","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kodungallur Kurumba Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Anappuzha","state":"Kerala","about":"Kodungallur Kurumba Bhagavathy Temple is associated with a carved wooden image of Kali and the Bharani and Thalappoli observances. The shrine provides a setting for studying sacred sculpture, oracle traditions and the use of a town-centre religious precinct.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes a large wooden Kali image and identifies the presiding goddess as Sree Kurumba. The account highlights Bharani, with the participation of oracles, and the separate Thalappoli observance. These features bring carved image making into relation with collective performance and ritual time. The record concerns the physical shrine and its cultural setting; the history of particular customs and the experiences of participating communities require further documentation beyond the introductory source.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-380","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-380","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/thrissur/kurumba-bhagavathy-kdungalloor","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.20246,"lng":76.20965,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thirunavaya Navamukunda Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thirunavaya Navamukunda Temple stands beside the Bharathapuzha and is associated with the historic Mamankam gathering. Its pavilion, successive rebuilding and river setting offer a place to examine relationships between sacred buildings, political ceremony and the use of a river landscape.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the Vishnu shrine near Tirur and describes a Pazhukka Mandapam associated with the Zamorin family's viewing of Mamankam. The account also notes repeated renovation. These details connect the temple with a wider ceremonial landscape along the Bharathapuzha. The surviving building should be studied through its changing phases rather than dated as a single entity, and historical accounts of Mamankam require comparison with records beyond a tourism introduction.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-381","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-381","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/malappuram/thirunavaya-navamukunda","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Ponnani Valiya Juma Masjid","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Ponnani","state":"Kerala","about":"Ponnani Valiya Juma Masjid is a landmark within a historic port associated with Arab traders and Muslim scholars. The mosque offers a place-based entry into the study of maritime religious networks, Arabic-Malayalam intellectual life and the formation of community institutions.","full":"The Kerala Tourism account connects Ponnani's mosque with Zainuddin and the movement of Muslim spiritual leaders through the coastal trading town. It also discusses the region's Arabic-Malayalam writing culture. The relationship between port, religious institution and scholarly exchange provides a useful research framework. This introductory source does not establish the provenance of particular manuscripts or architectural elements, which require local records, epigraphy and studies of the mosque's successive building phases.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-382","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-382","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/ponnani/68","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.77865,"lng":75.92373,"approx":false},
{"name":"Pazhayangadi Mosque and Kondotty Qubba","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Pazhayangadi Mosque, also described as Kondotty Qubba, is associated with Muhammad Shah and a prominent domed shrine. Its architectural vocabulary and commemorative observances offer material for examining Sufi networks, religious gathering and shared cultural life in Malabar.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Pazhayangadi Mosque with the name Kondotty Qubba and associates it with Muhammad Shah, known as Kondotty Thangal. The account highlights a white dome and Mughal-influenced architectural elements, along with the annual Nercha gathering. The complex allows study of how a saint's memory is given spatial and material form. Founding dates, later construction phases and different accounts of the shrine's age should be checked against historical and custodial records.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-383","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-383","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/islam-in-kerala/pilgrim-centres/pazhayangadi-mosque","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mampuram Maqam","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mampuram Maqam is a dargah and mosque complex associated with Sayyid Alavi Thangal. Its religious gathering space and commemorative role provide a setting for studying the connections between spiritual leadership, social memory and Malabar's histories of reform and resistance.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the complex as housing the tomb of Sayyid Alavi Thangal and describes its Kerala-Islamic architectural character and annual Uroos observance. These features connect a physical memorial with recurring acts of collective remembrance. The site offers directions for research into religious leadership and regional social history. Personal names, genealogies and the chronology of particular buildings require specialist comparison because introductory accounts of the Mampuram tradition are not always consistent.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-384","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-384","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/islam-in-kerala/photo-gallery/mampuram-maqam/3201","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Triprangode Shiva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Tirur","state":"Kerala","about":"Triprangode Shiva Temple includes a pond, subsidiary shrines and mural paintings within a substantial sacred precinct. The relationship between images, water and ceremonial movement makes it relevant to the study of how temple space accommodates several kinds of ritual activity.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Shiva as the main deity and highlights mural paintings, a pond and subsidiary shrines. It also records forms of circumambulation and an annual arattu observance. These features provide evidence for examining how the precinct supports movement, visual narrative and water-related ritual. The account does not give a conservation history for the paintings or a measured plan of the complex, so dates and building phases remain matters for further documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-385","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-385","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/malappuram/triprangode-siva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":10.85556,"lng":75.9477,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thirumandhamkunnu Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Thirumandhamkunnu Temple at Angadipuram is a Kerala-style sacred complex associated with Bhadrakali worship and Pooram. The site brings architectural form into relation with recurring devotional obligations and provides a setting for studying Malabar's temple networks and community histories.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes a complex associated with both Shiva and Bhadrakali, built in Kerala's regional architectural style. Its account identifies the annual Pooram and the Mangalya Puja observance. These practices can be documented as cultural traditions located within a particular built setting. The source does not establish detailed building phases, workshop histories or a single interpretation shared by all participants, so the entry remains a discovery record for further architectural and community research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-386","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-386","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/malappuram/thirumandhamkunnu","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":8.50175,"lng":76.94883,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kadampuzha Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kadampuzha Bhagavathy Temple is known for worship conducted at an opening rather than a conventional deity image. Its offerings of flowers and coconuts provide a distinctive setting for studying how sacred presence is expressed through space, materials and repeated devotional action.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Kiratha Parvathy as the deity and describes worship directed toward an opening, with Poomoodal and Muttarukkal among the recognised offerings. The first uses flowers, while the second involves breaking coconuts. These details allow study of material choice and the organisation of sacred presence without assuming that an anthropomorphic image is essential. Foundation stories involving Shankara belong to religious tradition and require separate treatment from the history of the surviving building.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-387","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-387","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/malappuram/kadampuzha","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Trikandiyur Mahadeva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Trikandiyur Mahadeva Temple near Tirur contains an east-facing sanctum and a large temple pond within a layered religious precinct. The site offers a place to examine the relationship between sacred orientation, water storage, changing patronage and stories preserved through worship.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the east-facing Shiva shrine, its large pond and the temple's passage through different custodial and patronage arrangements. It also records foundation stories and rebuilding. The pond and sacred core can be documented as material features, while their religious explanations belong to local narrative. Establishing construction phases, former ownership and the history of water management requires comparison with records and measured evidence rather than accepting every introductory date literally.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-388","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-388","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/trikandiyur-shiva-temple-malappuram/35/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Tali Shiva Temple, Kozhikode","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Tali Shiva Temple combines a multi-storeyed sanctum, murals and stone sculpture with a history of Zamorin patronage. The site is also associated with Revathi Pattathanam, linking a distinctive sacred building with traditions of Sanskrit scholarship and intellectual recognition in Kozhikode.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the temple's Kerala-style construction, two-storeyed sanctum, mural paintings and granite sculptures depicting religious narratives. It connects the shrine with the Zamorins and Revathi Pattathanam, a gathering honouring scholars. These features support examination of the relationship between patronage, learned practice and sacred space. Mythic foundation stories and proposed early dates should be distinguished from evidence for the surviving building and its later restoration history.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-389","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-389","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/tali-temple-kozhikode/281/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mishkal Mosque","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mishkal Mosque at Kuttichira is a multi-storeyed timber mosque with carved pillars, doors and a wooden pulpit. Its regional building vocabulary offers material evidence for studying how Islamic worship spaces were made through Kerala's established traditions of carpentry and ornament.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the mosque with the merchant Nakhooda Mishkal and describes a multi-storeyed building without conventional domes or minarets. Carved wooden pillars, a decorated mimbar and detailed doorways are among its features. The site makes architectural exchange visible through material and form. Its history includes damage and restoration, so close documentation should distinguish surviving earlier timber from later repairs rather than assuming that every component belongs to the original foundation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-390","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-390","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/islam-in-kerala/pilgrim-centres/kuttichira-mithqal-mosque","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Muchundi Mosque","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Muchundi Mosque at Kuttichira preserves a stone inscription associated with a Zamorin land donation. The regional-style building and its written record provide a focused point of entry into the study of epigraphy, patronage and Muslim community institutions in medieval Kozhikode.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the mosque's Kerala architectural style and a thirteenth-century Vattezhuthu inscription recording a property donation by a Zamorin. The inscription makes the site important for investigating relationships between religious institutions and political authority. Its script, wording and historical interpretation should be checked through an epigraphic edition. The presence of a dated record does not automatically establish that every visible part of the present building belongs to the same period.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-391","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-391","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/muchunthi-palli-kuttichira/519/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kuttichira Jama Palli","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kuttichira Jama Palli contains extensive timber construction, carved ceiling motifs and Arabic inscriptions on wooden surfaces. The mosque offers a setting for examining the relationship between congregational space, regional carpentry and calligraphy within Kozhikode's historic Muslim neighbourhood.","full":"The Kerala Tourism account distinguishes Jama Palli from neighbouring Mishkal and Muchundi mosques. It describes timber panelling, lotus and geometric ceiling motifs, and Arabic inscriptions on walls and rafters. These features provide material for studying the sharing of craft vocabularies across regional religious buildings. A detailed architectural survey should establish the organisation of its prayer spaces and the dates of successive alterations rather than repeating unverified superlatives about its size or unusual roofing.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-392","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-392","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/mishkal-mosque/79","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Lokanarkavu Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Vadakara","state":"Kerala","about":"Lokanarkavu Temple is associated with the northern ballads and contains mural paintings illustrating divine figures. The site links painted surfaces with oral heroic traditions and recurring performance, making it a place to study relationships between architecture, storytelling and community memory.","full":"Kerala Tourism connects the Durga shrine with the Vadakkan Pattukal and the legendary hero Thacholi Othenan. It also highlights murals made with natural colours and performance traditions linked to the annual observance. These are different kinds of evidence: paintings can be recorded materially, while ballad associations require attention to oral transmission and variation. The chronology of particular panels and the histories of participating communities need further documentation beyond the introductory account.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-393","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-393","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kozhikode/lokanarkavu-vadakara","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":11.59864,"lng":75.61979,"approx":false},
{"name":"Azhakodi Devi Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kozhikode","state":"Kerala","about":"Azhakodi Devi Temple combines Kerala-style architecture with a distinctive martial image of Bhadrakali. Its sculptural attributes and ceremonial sequence offer a setting for studying how visual symbols, sacred building form and the Malayalam festival calendar are brought into relationship.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the goddess with a sword, trident and other attributes, and identifies the building as an example of Kerala-style architecture. Its account follows the annual observance from flag hoisting to the concluding ceremonial bath. These details allow investigation of iconography and ritual sequencing within a specific site. The date and authorship of the sacred image, as well as later building alterations, remain topics for specialist and custodial documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-394","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-394","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kozhikode/azhakodi-devi","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":11.26385,"lng":75.78927,"approx":false},
{"name":"S. M. Street, Kozhikode","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Kozhikode","state":"Kerala","about":"S. M. Street, or Mittayitheruvu, is a historic commercial street associated with sweet makers and trading communities in Kozhikode. Its shop-lined spaces offer a setting for examining the relationship between urban markets, migration and the transmission of food and retail knowledge.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes S. M. Street as a long-standing commercial hub and connects its sweet-making history with Gujarati settlers during the Zamorin period. Shops selling halwa and other goods continue the association between the street and everyday craft enterprise. The built market can be studied through shopfronts, movement and occupational networks, while individual business histories and recipe lineages require interviews and records rather than being inferred from a shared street name.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-395","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-395","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/campaigns/kerala365/sm-street-kozhikode","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":11.25086,"lng":75.78157,"approx":false},
{"name":"Mother of God Cathedral, Kozhikode","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Mother of God Cathedral near Kozhikode's Beach Road combines European design associations with Indian craftsmanship and a devotional portrait of Mary. The building offers a setting for studying architectural exchange and the place of religious images within Malabar's Christian communities.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the church as a Roman Catholic landmark and describes the involvement of Italian architects and Indian craftspeople. It highlights the building's architectural character and a portrait of Mary. The source combines references to different periods, so the entry avoids assigning the whole surviving building a single early date. A fuller account should compare construction records, decorative programmes and conservation evidence to distinguish foundation, rebuilding and later additions.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-396","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-396","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/mother-god-church-kozhikode/523/","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Bathery Jain Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Sultan Bathery","state":"Kerala","about":"Bathery Jain Temple preserves a granite-built sanctum and stone-pillared hall associated with Wayanad's Jain past. Its surviving masonry and histories of changing use offer evidence for studying religious networks, building techniques and the reuse of sacred structures across time.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes a granite structure with a pillared hall and surviving sanctum, protected by the Archaeological Survey of India. It connects the temple with a former Jain settlement and later non-religious uses. The building therefore supports examination of stone assembly, regional connections and reuse. Proposed medieval dates and the story linking the town's name with an ammunition battery should be assessed separately from measured evidence for the surviving architectural fabric.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-397","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-397","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/bathery-jain-temple-sulthan-bathery-unexplored/778/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":11.66016,"lng":76.25068,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thirunelli Mahavishnu Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thirunelli Mahavishnu Temple includes a stone aqueduct bringing water from the surrounding forest. The shrine and its water channels provide a substantial setting for studying how regional temple architecture integrates material engineering, hillside conditions and the requirements of ritual practice.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes an aqueduct whose stone channels are supported on pillars, supplying water to the temple from the forest. The site is dedicated to Vishnu and is associated with ancestral rites near the Papanasini stream. These features connect water conveyance with sacred-space planning. The source does not establish the aqueduct's builder or construction phases, so its hydraulic design and alterations should be documented through measured surveys and local maintenance histories.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-398","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-398","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/wayanad/thirunelli","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Valliyoorkkavu Bhagavathy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Valliyoorkkavu Bhagavathy Temple is a sacred place associated with several communities of Wayanad. Its goddess traditions and recurring floor art, dance and ritual observances offer a setting for studying how a site sustains relationships between landscape, performance and community memory.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the shrine near Mananthavady and describes Bhagavathy worshipped in several forms. Its account links the site with kalamezhuthu floor art and performance during its annual observance. These connections make the temple relevant to research on the spatial setting of transmitted knowledge. Community participation must be documented with care, recognising distinct voices and histories rather than treating all Adivasi peoples or local participants as one undifferentiated group.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-399","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-399","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/wayanad/valliyoorkkavu","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thrissilery Shiva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Mananthavady","state":"Kerala","about":"Thrissilery Shiva Temple is associated with carved architectural elements and a water-surrounded Jala Durga shrine. Its relationship with Thirunelli offers a place to examine how sacred buildings, water spaces and linked pilgrimage sites form a wider cultural landscape in Wayanad.","full":"Kerala Tourism highlights the temple's proportions and carvings and identifies a smaller Jala Durga shrine situated in a water body. It also records a connection with Thirunelli. These observations support research on how different sacred structures relate to water and to one another across the landscape. The source does not establish a measured hydrological connection or a precise construction chronology, so such questions require field documentation rather than assumptions drawn from tradition.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-400","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-400","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/wayanad/thrissilery-shiva","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":11.85156,"lng":76.01652,"approx":false},
{"name":"Ponkuzhi Seetha Devi–Hanuman Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Seetha Devi–Hanuman shrine near Ponkuzhi connects a tiled-roof Kerala building with Ramayana traditions beside a river landscape. Its form and narrative setting offer a place to study how epic memory becomes attached to particular routes and local sacred places.","full":"Kerala Tourism places this shrine on the Sulthan Bathery–Mysore road near the Ponkuzhi River and Muthanga, and describes a tiled roof in Kerala's regional architectural style. The temple's Seetha and Hanuman associations connect the site with Ramayana memory. This record follows the location and title given in the page body; detailed custodial history and the chronology of the present structure require further checking rather than inference from the epic narrative.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-401","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-401","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/wayanad/seetha-devi-lava-kusha","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pazhassi Tomb Memorial, Mananthavady","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Pazhassi Tomb Memorial at Mananthavady connects a commemorative monument with an interpretive museum holding letters, coins and material culture. Together, they provide a setting for examining public memory, regional resistance and the documentation of different communities' contributions to Wayanad's history.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the memorial in central Mananthavady and describes its integrated underground museum, including letters, coins, weapons and objects associated with different communities. The complex connects a commemorated individual with wider regional histories and ways of presenting material evidence. Particular objects require catalogue-level provenance, and interpretations of resistance should consider multiple voices rather than allowing a single memorial narrative to stand for all of Wayanad.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-402","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-402","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/thalassery/pazhassi/tomb-museum","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"St. Angelo Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Kannur","state":"Kerala","about":"St. Angelo Fort uses a triangular plan, laterite masonry and projecting bastions above Kannur's harbour landscape. Its surviving defensive structures provide evidence for studying how coastal terrain, construction materials and changing military technologies shaped a fort through successive administrations.","full":"The Kerala Tourism heritage account describes laterite construction with surkhi mixture, a triangular layout and substantial bastions. It records Portuguese construction followed by Dutch, Arakkal and British control. Cannons, barracks and tombstones are among the material features identified at the site. The fort allows investigation of defensive geometry and harbour surveillance, while its successive modifications should be distinguished through conservation evidence instead of attributing all visible fabric to its first phase.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-403","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-403","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/thalassery/tourist-circuits/culture/kannur-fort","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":11.85412,"lng":75.37198,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thalassery Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Thalassery","state":"Kerala","about":"Thalassery Fort developed from an East India Company trading establishment into a military and administrative centre. Its square plan, bastions, chambers and fortified entrance provide a setting for examining the relationship between commodity storage, coastal power and defensive construction.","full":"Kerala Tourism traces the site from a trading factory to a fortified enclosure and later administrative centre. The account identifies a square plan, flanking bastions, a massive entrance and underground chambers, some of which were adapted for different uses. These features help connect the movement and storage of goods with military and bureaucratic control. Individual stories about hidden passages, meetings or prisons should be checked against measured plans and archival records before being accepted literally.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-404","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-404","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/thalassery/tourist-circuits/harbour-town/thalassery-fort","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":11.74861,"lng":75.48668,"approx":false},
{"name":"Odathil Mosque","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Thalassery","state":"Kerala","about":"Odathil Mosque combines copper-covered roofing with intricate timber work on walls and pillars. Its regional form, without conventional domes or minarets, offers a setting for studying the adaptation of Kerala's carpentry traditions to a Muslim place of congregational worship.","full":"Kerala Tourism associates the mosque with the merchant Moosakkaka and highlights its copper roof and carved wooden surfaces. The account describes a building following Kerala's regional architectural vocabulary rather than conventional domed mosque forms. These elements connect local construction skills with networks of trade and religious patronage. Sources differ over the mosque's age and the origin of its name, so this record focuses on the documented material features rather than choosing one uncorroborated origin story.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-405","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-405","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/odathil-palli/127","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":11.74868,"lng":75.49007,"approx":false},
{"name":"Rajarajeswara Temple, Taliparamba","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Taliparamba","state":"Kerala","about":"Rajarajeswara Temple has a rectangular, two-tiered sanctum roofed with copper sheets. This Shiva shrine at Taliparamba offers a focused example for studying Kerala temple proportions, layered roof construction and the relationship between architectural form and continuing ritual practice.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the two-tiered rectangular sanctum and copper-sheet roofing as distinctive architectural features. The building is dedicated to Shiva and forms part of Taliparamba's sacred landscape. Its combination of timber-supported form and metal covering provides a basis for examining construction and maintenance, although the introductory page does not supply measured drawings. Claims that its spire was the tallest of an era require wider comparative evidence and are not adopted as a verified ranking.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-406","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-406","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kannur/raja-rajeswara-taliparamba","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":12.03735,"lng":75.36028,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thodeekulam Shiva Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thodeekulam Shiva Temple preserves mural panels illustrating Shiva and Vishnu narratives around its sanctum. The paintings provide a substantial setting for studying regional composition, storytelling and the relationship between decorated walls and the architecture of a continuing place of worship.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies numerous paintings arranged in panels across the sanctum walls and describes their Shaiva and Vaishnava narrative subjects. The temple stands near Kannavam in Kannur district. The surviving panels invite close documentation of composition, iconography and conservation condition. The source's broad claim about the age of the temple should not be transferred automatically to the murals, and accounts of particular painter communities require verification through specialist studies and conservation evidence.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-407","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-407","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kannur/thodeekulam","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thiruvangad Ramaswamy Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thiruvangad Ramaswamy Temple combines copper roofing, wood carving, terracotta work and painted ceiling panels. The Thalassery shrine offers evidence for examining the coordination of several craft media within a temple and the preservation of images, inscriptions and devotional narratives.","full":"Kerala Tourism notes the copper-sheeted roof behind the building's popular name, Brass Pagoda, and highlights wood carvings, terracotta, mural work on ceiling planks and lithic records. These features make the complex a useful setting for comparing the roles of structural timber, metal covering and decorated surfaces. Detailed documentation would need to separate individual materials and historical phases, and to establish the provenance and wording of inscriptions rather than relying on general architectural praise.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-408","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-408","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kannur/thiruvangad-ramaswamy","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Payyanur Subramanya Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Payyanur","state":"Kerala","about":"Payyanur Subramanya Temple has a two-storeyed sanctum described as gajaprishta, or elephant-back shaped, within a substantial enclosure. Its building form and association with a ritual ring connect sacred geometry, enclosure design and specialised metalworking traditions in northern Kerala.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the two-storeyed gajaprishta sanctum and a substantial compound wall, alongside the temple's association with the pavitra mothiram ring. The shrine therefore offers material for studying architectural form and the settings through which crafted ritual objects acquire significance. The enclosure and sanctum provide concrete features for comparing regional building forms. Measured surveys and workshop documentation are needed before inferring design rules, material specifications or historical continuity from an introductory description.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-409","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-409","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kannur/payyanur-subramanya","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":12.09777,"lng":75.20467,"approx":false},
{"name":"Trichambaram Krishna Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Taliparamba","state":"Kerala","about":"Trichambaram Krishna Temple contains murals and carvings alongside an image of Krishna in a fierce posture. The site brings regional visual storytelling into relation with a recurring festival and deity-bearing performance, offering a setting for studying the movement between image and ritual.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Krishna in Raudra Bhavam and highlights the temple's mural and carved imagery. Its account also links the annual observance with Thidampu Nritham. These features provide different kinds of evidence: images can be recorded through material and iconographic examination, while performance knowledge requires engagement with practitioners. Dates assigned to the artworks in introductory accounts should be corroborated through conservation or specialist studies rather than treated as uniform across the complex.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-410","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-410","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kannur/trichambaram-krishna","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":12.03128,"lng":75.36801,"approx":false},
{"name":"Peralassery Subramanya Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Peralassery Subramanya Temple is particularly noted for a deep pond reached by many descending steps. The relationship between stepped water access and a multi-shrine precinct provides a setting for studying temple water architecture and the material organisation of sacred space.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the pond as a well-like structure with numerous descending steps and identifies Ayyappa and Subramanya shrines within the compound. The stepped geometry allows examination of access to water within a sacred precinct. Understanding construction phases, drainage and maintenance would require technical documentation. Stories attributing consecration to Rama belong to religious tradition and should be considered separately from evidence about the age and design of the surviving pond or shrines.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-411","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-411","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kannur/peralassery-subramanya","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Parassinikadavu Muthappan Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Anthoor","state":"Kerala","about":"Parassinikadavu Muthappan Temple stands beside the Valapattanam River and is closely associated with performed worship. Its interaction between devotees and a Muthappan performer offers a setting for studying how a physical shrine supports oral exchange, hospitality and living religious knowledge.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the riverbank site and the role of a Theyyam performer through whom devotees address Muthappan. It also identifies hospitality and a harvest-related observance as part of the temple's cultural setting. The building is therefore relevant to research on how space accommodates encounter, performance and communal provision. Practitioner and community accounts are essential to understand meanings and responsibilities; an introductory tourism page cannot stand for all participants' experiences.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-412","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-412","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kannur/parassinikadavu-muthappan","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":11.98302,"lng":75.40143,"approx":false},
{"name":"Chandragiri Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Melparamba","state":"Kerala","about":"Chandragiri Fort preserves laterite walls above the confluence of a river and the Arabian Sea. Its elevated position and association with the Keladi Nayakas provide evidence for studying how defensive construction responded to coastal routes, borders and an estuarine landscape.","full":"Kerala Tourism associates the fort with Shivappa Nayak and describes surviving laterite walls on a hill overlooking the river mouth. It places the site within the changing political landscape between Kolathunadu and Tulunadu. The relationship between high ground, waterways and fortification can be studied through the surviving enclosure. Precise interpretations of damage marks, construction sequences and former administrative boundaries require historical and archaeological corroboration beyond the introductory description.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-413","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-413","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/chandragiri-fort/149","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":12.46991,"lng":75.00329,"approx":false},
{"name":"Hosdurg Fort","kind":"heritage","host":"Archaeology and architecture","city":"Kallanchira","state":"Kerala","about":"Hosdurg Fort at Kanhangad preserves rounded bastions and remnants of a once larger defensive enclosure. Its relationship with the Ikkeri Nayakas and later administrations offers material for studying regional fortification forms, coastal routes and the reuse of a strategic site.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the fort with the Ikkeri or Bednur Nayakas and describes rounded bastions, wall walkways and related lookout features. The account notes similarities with other northern Kerala forts while distinguishing Hosdurg as its own site. These elements invite comparison of regional defensive design and changing use. Conflicting accounts of patrons, extent and dates should be assessed through historical documentation rather than resolved by assigning all remnants to one ruler.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-ARC-414","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-ARC-414","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/hosdurg-fort-kanhangad/158/","group":"Archaeology and architecture"},"lat":12.30904,"lng":75.09469,"approx":false},
{"name":"Ananthapura Lake Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Ananthapura Lake Temple places a Vishnu shrine within a water setting and preserves carved wooden narrative imagery. Its pavilion ceilings include Vishnu's incarnations and planetary figures, making the site relevant to the study of sacred carpentry, iconography and the shaping of a religious landscape.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Anantha Padmanabha seated on the serpent Anantha and identifies wooden ceiling carvings illustrating the Dashavatara. It also notes Navagraha and guardian imagery elsewhere in the temple. The lake setting and carved visual programme offer a basis for examining relationships between built form, water and religious narrative. Foundation dates and claims linking this shrine to another temple belong to traditions requiring corroboration rather than direct proof from the surviving fabric.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-415","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-415","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kasaragod/kasaragod-ananthapura-lake","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Madhur Madanantheswara Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Madhur Temple combines layered copper-and-tile roofing with carved timber, stucco imagery and a Ramayana-decorated pavilion beside the Madhuvahini River. The building offers a rich setting for examining how different materials and visual narratives are coordinated within northern Kerala's sacred architecture.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes a three-tiered upper form with copper covering on the upper levels and tiles below, alongside carved wooden pillars and beams. The Namaskara Mandapam carries Ramayana scenes, while stucco images decorate walls and roofs. The shrine is associated with Shiva and Ganapathy. These features support material and iconographic study; claims about royal construction dates or the well's powers require different forms of evidence and are not adopted uncritically.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-416","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-416","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/destination_detailed_view/madhur-temple/158","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Malik Dinar Mosque, Thalankara","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Malik Dinar Mosque at Thalankara has a multi-storeyed form with large beams in Kerala's regional architectural vocabulary. Its continuing place in coastal Muslim memory provides a setting for studying sacred carpentry, changes in building form and narratives of maritime religious contact.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the present building as a two-storeyed structure with substantial beams, distinct from an earlier small thatched form. It connects the mosque with the Malik Dinar tradition. The distinction between foundation narrative and later architecture is important: surviving fabric can support investigation of construction and repair, while early arrival stories require historical scrutiny. The site therefore brings material evidence into conversation with community memory without equating the two.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-417","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-417","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/campaigns/kerala365/malik-dinar-mosque","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Aravath Subramanya Temple","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"Palakunnu","state":"Kerala","about":"Aravath Subramanya Temple is a two-storeyed Kerala-style shrine constructed with red laterite. Its compact material description provides a focused point of entry into the study of local stone use and layered sacred building forms in the region around Pallikkara.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the temple's dwitala, or two-storeyed, form and the use of red laterite at Aravath near Pallikkara. The building is dedicated to Subramanya and is distinct from nearby Bekal Fort. Its combination of material and form provides a basis for further documentation of walling, roofing and maintenance. The introductory entry does not give a construction date, detailed plan or workshop history, so none is inferred here.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-418","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-418","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/temples/kasaragod/aravath-subramanya","group":"Built and community heritage"},"lat":12.41325,"lng":75.04422,"approx":true},
{"name":"Maipady Palace","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Maipady Palace was a seat of the Kumbala rulers on the Kasaragod–Perla route. The surviving residence provides a place-based entry into the study of regional courts, domestic authority and the relationships between political centres and nearby sacred landscapes.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Maipady Palace as a former seat of the Kumbala kings and locates it on the Kasaragod–Perla road near Madhur. That institutional association makes it a candidate for studying the material setting of regional rule. A fuller account requires measured architectural documentation, family or administrative records, and evidence for the use of individual spaces. The brief official source does not establish a precise building date or detailed construction history.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-419","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-419","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/bekal/maipady-palace.php","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Our Lady of Sorrows Church, Bela","kind":"heritage","host":"Built and community heritage","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Bela Church, dedicated to Our Lady of Sorrows, is associated with the settlement of Konkani-speaking Catholics in northern Kerala. Its histories of relocation and worship provide a setting for examining how migrating communities establish religious buildings and preserve memories across regional boundaries.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the church as a historic Christian landmark and recounts a movement from an earlier thatched worship space at Kudrepady to its later Bela location. It associates this history with Konkani Catholics who migrated from the Srirangapatna region. The account offers a framework for researching settlement and sacred-building change, while specific migration episodes, dates and construction phases require comparison with parish records rather than acceptance of a single introductory narrative.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-HER-420","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-HER-420","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/pilgrim-centres-manjeswaram/156","group":"Built and community heritage"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Museum of Kerala History, Edappally","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Museum of Kerala History presents regional history through sculptural scenes, art and audiovisual interpretation. Established by the Madhavan Nayar Foundation at Edappally, it brings historical personalities and episodes into a public setting for learning about Kerala's cultural development.","full":"The Edappally museum uses life-size figures and narrated displays to arrange episodes from Kerala's past into an accessible historical account. Its associated art collections add a second way of examining how the region has represented itself. The institution is relevant to the public transmission of historical knowledge and to museum interpretation, while a staged scene should be distinguished from an original archaeological object or a contemporary document.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-421","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-421","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/museum-kerala-history-edappally/183/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Indo-Portuguese Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Kochi","state":"Kerala","about":"The Indo-Portuguese Museum preserves church furnishings, metalwork and other objects associated with Kerala's Portuguese connections. Its collections at Fort Kochi allow religious craft, local materials and the movement of artistic designs across the Indian Ocean to be examined together.","full":"Founded under Bishop Joseph Kureethra, the museum brings together material from churches in the Kochi region. Its sections cover altars, treasures, processions, civil life and the cathedral. A teak altar fragment and a silver-and-wood processional cross illustrate how religious objects combine material skill with ceremonial use. The collection offers evidence for cultural exchange, although the origin and date of each piece should be established from its catalogue.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-422","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-422","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/indo-portuguese-museum/336/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":9.96231,"lng":76.24027,"approx":false},
{"name":"Arakkal Kettu Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Arakkal Kettu Museum occupies the former court hall of the Arakkal family at Ayikkara. Household objects, manuscripts and royal belongings document a Muslim ruling house whose history connects coastal Kerala with maritime trade and the Lakshadweep islands.","full":"The museum displays material associated with the Arakkal Ali Rajas and Beevis, including family seals, grain-storage boxes, weapons, a telescope and copies of the Quran. These objects connect administration and domestic life with the family's coastal trading networks. The museum is part of the wider palace complex, and its collection provides a starting point for studying matrilineal succession, maritime connections and the material practices of a regional court.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-423","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-423","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/arakkal-kettu-museum-kannur/84/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"KIRTADS Ethnological Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Thondayad","state":"Kerala","about":"The Ethnological Museum at KIRTADS holds objects documenting the lives of Kerala's Scheduled Tribe communities. Agricultural tools, household equipment, ornaments and musical instruments offer material evidence of skills and practices that should be understood alongside the knowledge of their makers.","full":"KIRTADS combines research, training and cultural documentation with an ethnological collection at Chevayur. The museum includes clothing, jewellery, tools and instruments, while the institute also maintains a reference library and documentation facilities. The collection can support study of food production, craft and performance across different communities. Its displays are historical selections, however, and should not be treated as complete or unchanging accounts of contemporary Adivasi lives.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-424","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-424","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/kirtads-kozhikode/253/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":11.27207,"lng":75.81706,"approx":false},
{"name":"Pazhassi Raja Archaeological Museum, Kozhikode","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Kozhikode","state":"Kerala","about":"The Pazhassi Raja Museum at East Hill preserves archaeological and historical material from Kerala. Coins, pottery, bronzes and stone objects allow regional craft and exchange to be considered through surviving artefacts rather than through a single narrative of royal history.","full":"Named after Pazhassi Raja, this museum in Kozhikode is a separate institution from his tomb at Mananthavady. Its archaeological displays include pottery, coins, sculptures and inscriptions, bringing several kinds of material evidence together. Such collections can help readers compare artistic production, writing and circulation of goods in Kerala. Dates and findspots must be checked object by object; a museum's commemorative name does not establish that all exhibits belonged to the ruler.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-425","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-425","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/pazhassiraja-museum-art-gallery-kozhikode/258/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":11.24506,"lng":75.77547,"approx":true},
{"name":"Wayanad Heritage Museum, Ambalavayal","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Ambalavayal","state":"Kerala","about":"The Wayanad Heritage Museum gathers archaeological finds and community objects from the district. Hero stones, pottery, sculptures, farming implements and instruments connect commemorative art with practical knowledge about cultivation, hunting, fishing and everyday life in the Western Ghats.","full":"The museum at Ambalavayal organizes its collections in four sections known as Veerasmruthi, Gothrasmruthi, Devasmruthi and Jeevanasmruthi. Hero stones are displayed alongside sculpture, terracotta and objects associated with community life. Its agricultural and fishing implements are particularly useful for examining how skills were embodied in tools. The collection combines objects of different periods and origins, so no single date or cultural attribution should be applied to the museum as a whole.","branch":"heritage","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-426","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-426","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/wayanad/wayanad-heritage-musuem.php","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":11.6192,"lng":76.21202,"approx":true},
{"name":"Nilambur Teak Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Nilambur","state":"Kerala","about":"The Teak Museum at Nilambur examines the tree as a biological resource and a material used in Kerala's buildings, storage structures and boats. Forestry exhibits and crafted objects connect the study of timber with the history of its cultivation and use.","full":"Established as a Kerala Forest Research Institute sub-centre, the museum brings together displays on teak anatomy, propagation and plantation history. A traditional granary and a miniature uru show how timber knowledge enters storage and maritime construction. The institution therefore links practical woodworking with scientific forestry. Historical plantation narratives should also be read in relation to changing land use, labour and forest access, rather than as a timeless account of resource management.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-427","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-427","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/teak-museum-nilambur/344/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":11.30009,"lng":76.25041,"approx":false},
{"name":"Thunchan Memorial Literary Museum and Library","kind":"archive","host":"Archives and manuscripts","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thunchan Memorial combines a literary museum, research library and manuscript collection at Tirur. Dedicated to Thunchath Ezhuthachan, it supports study of Malayalam literary history and provides a setting where manuscript preservation, public reading and initiation into letters meet.","full":"The memorial's activities include a literary museum interpreting the development of Malayalam, a research library and work connected with manuscript documentation. Its public programmes and Vidyarambham observances connect scholarship with the social transmission of literacy. Ezhuthachan's retellings of epic narratives are central to the institution's identity. Literary seminars, poetry readings and cultural performances during the Thunchan festival bring readers and scholars into this setting of research and remembrance.","branch":"language","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MAN-428","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MAN-428","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/thunchan-memorial-tirur/400/","group":"Archives and manuscripts"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Water Heritage Museum and Biopark","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Water Heritage Museum at CWRDM interprets Kerala's relationship with water through models, images and displays. Wells, canals, farming systems and livelihoods are brought together to show how environmental knowledge connects domestic routines with wider systems of resource management.","full":"This museum initiative of the Centre for Water Resources Development and Management presents water as both an ecological resource and a part of social life. Displays refer to wells, canals, Pokkali cultivation, coir work and other water-related practices. The exhibits can be used to compare traditional livelihoods with modern hydraulic infrastructure. They are educational interpretations, and specific claims about the sustainability or history of a practice still need to be checked against field research.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-429","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-429","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/museum-kunnamangalam-kozhikode/418/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"KCS Panicker Gallery","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The KCS Panicker Gallery preserves a collection of paintings and sculptures by the artist. His combinations of words, signs and painted forms offer a way to examine how modern Indian art reworked inherited visual vocabularies and ideas about knowledge.","full":"Located in the Thiruvananthapuram museum compound, the gallery traces Panicker's artistic development through paintings and bronze sculptures. The Words and Symbols series is an important part of the collection, bringing writing-like marks into relation with colour and composition. This is a modern artistic engagement with signs, rather than a manuscript archive or a recovered ancient code. Interpretations of particular motifs should be grounded in curatorial and art-historical research.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-430","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-430","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/kcs-panicker-gallery-trivandrum/216/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sree Chithra Art Gallery Collection","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Sree Chithra Art Gallery collection brings Kerala painting into conversation with other Indian and Asian schools. Works associated with Travancore collecting, miniatures and mural traditions allow artistic techniques, patronage and the circulation of visual ideas to be studied together.","full":"The gallery was established with Travancore royal patronage and holds paintings associated with several Indian and Asian traditions. Mughal, Rajput, Bengal and Tanjore works sit within a collecting history that extends to Chinese, Japanese and Tibetan art. Kerala Tourism also describes the separate Raja Ravi Varma gallery within the same museum campus, where major works by Ravi Varma and his relatives are displayed. Individual works retain their own attribution and conservation histories.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-431","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-431","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/sree-chithra-art-galler-trivandrum/225/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Koyikkal Folklore and Numismatics Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Nedumangad","state":"Kerala","about":"Koyikkal Palace houses linked folklore and numismatics collections in Nedumangad. Musical instruments, household utensils and coins bring domestic craft, performance and exchange into one setting, allowing everyday forms of knowledge to be considered alongside the history of regional currency.","full":"The state Archaeology Department's collections at Koyikkal include work tools, wooden kitchenware and musical instruments, as well as a numismatic section. The Chandravalayam instrument and regional coin types are among the objects highlighted by Kerala Tourism. The collections occupy a two-storeyed palace, providing an architectural setting for material from domestic and ceremonial life. Comparing objects with their documented uses connects instrument-making, daily work and the histories of regional currency and exchange.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-432","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-432","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/campaigns/kerala365/koyikkal-palace-kerala","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":8.60919,"lng":77.00293,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kottarakkara Thampuran Memorial Museum of Classical Arts","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The classical arts museum at Kottarakkara presents costumes, ornaments, figures and hand gestures associated with Kerala performance. Its displays make the material and visual systems of Kathakali and Mohiniyattam accessible for comparison with the training and practice of living performers.","full":"Also called the Kottarakkara Kathakali Museum, this institution commemorates Kottarakkara Thampuran and the regional history of performance. Life-size figures, costumes, ornaments and representations of hand gestures form part of its displays. These materials can help readers examine how character, movement and expression are communicated visually. The museum's commemorative narrative should not reduce the development of Kathakali to a single event or substitute for studying its many performing lineages.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-433","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-433","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/kottarakkara-kathakali-museum/607/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Revi Karunakaran Memorial Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Revi Karunakaran Memorial Museum holds an art collection assembled across generations of a coir-trading family. Its Kerala Room and varied crafted objects provide a setting for considering regional material culture alongside objects collected through wider commercial and cultural connections.","full":"The museum's collections include carved objects, porcelain, crystal and sculpture, while a dedicated Kerala Room uses terracotta flooring and carved teak to present regional craft. These holdings reflect a particular family's collecting choices and its contact with wider markets. They can support discussion of workmanship, taste and cultural exchange. The private collection should not be treated as a comprehensive representation of Kerala, and object materials and provenance need catalogue-level checking.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-434","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-434","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/revi-karunakaran-memorial-museum/417/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kerala Biodiversity Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"The Kerala Biodiversity Museum links natural-history interpretation with local knowledge of plants, rice varieties and ecosystems. Established by the State Biodiversity Board, its exhibits connect conservation questions with the livelihoods of farmers, artisans and communities whose work depends on biological resources.","full":"The museum presents biodiversity through models, multimedia and displays of species and ecosystems. A medicinal-plant garden and material concerning traditional rice varieties connect the exhibits with local resource use. It is a public education institution where scientific classification can be considered alongside agricultural and community knowledge. The presence of a medicinal plant in a display does not establish treatment efficacy, and ecological claims should be checked against the underlying research.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-435","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-435","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/kerala-biodiversity-museum/670/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":8.47866,"lng":76.92995,"approx":false},
{"name":"State Soil Museum and Soil Information Centre","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"The State Soil Museum presents Kerala's soils through preserved profiles, maps and educational displays. By relating layers of soil to cultivation and land use, it offers a material reference for comparing farmers' experience of land with modern systems of soil classification.","full":"Soil monoliths preserve vertical sections showing layers that are usually hidden beneath a field's surface. The museum combines these sections with maps and explanations of formation, fertility, erosion and crop suitability. It can therefore support discussions about how knowledge of land is recorded and communicated. The collection is an educational resource rather than a substitute for current field testing, and regional soil categories should not be used as uniform prescriptions for cultivation.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-436","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-436","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/state-soil-museum-and-information-centre/674/","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":8.5376,"lng":76.9375,"approx":false},
{"name":"International Coir Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"Kalavoor","state":"Kerala","about":"The International Coir Museum documents the development of coconut-fibre processing in Alappuzha. Retting, fibre extraction and later mechanisation are presented together, connecting household and workshop skills with research, industrial change and the many uses of coir in everyday objects.","full":"Established by the Coir Board, the museum presents traditional coir processes alongside technological developments in the industry. Its interpretation includes the extraction of fibre from coconut husks, the growth of manufacturing and examples of coir products. This makes it useful for examining how manual expertise and machinery interact. The museum's account is an institutional history, so questions about working conditions, environmental effects and the experience of individual producers require additional evidence.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-437","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-437","src":"https://coirboard.gov.in/?page_id=1731","group":"Museums and collections"},"lat":9.56307,"lng":76.32837,"approx":false},
{"name":"Southern Naval Command Maritime Museum","kind":"museum","host":"Museums and collections","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Maritime Museum at Fort Kochi presents the history of the Indian Navy alongside Kerala's seafaring connections. Its displays provide a public setting for exploring navigation, vessels and the coastal networks through which materials, people and technical ideas have circulated.","full":"Located within the INS Dronacharya establishment, the museum interprets maritime and naval history through artefacts, models and displays. Kerala's coastal connections and traditions of shipbuilding give a regional context to the wider naval story. The museum is relevant to the history of technologies at sea and the institutions that preserve them. Naval commemoration and reconstructions should be distinguished from primary evidence of earlier merchant navigation and local boat-building practices.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-MUS-438","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-MUS-438","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/maritime-museum/73/","group":"Museums and collections"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Unnayi Warrier Smaraka Kalanilayam","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Unnayi Warrier Smaraka Kalanilayam trains practitioners in Kathakali acting, music, percussion and the work of make-up and costume. Its programme at Irinjalakuda shows how a performance tradition is sustained through several coordinated bodies of practical knowledge rather than acting alone.","full":"Kerala Tourism's institutional directory lists courses in Kathakali vesham, singing, chenda, maddalam, chutty and costume-related work at this centre. The school also teaches other arts and maintains facilities including a library. Its relevance lies in the organization of long-term instruction across the interdependent skills needed for a production. The directory is evidence of the institution's educational profile, while current admissions, course structures and qualifications should be confirmed directly.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-IKS-439","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-IKS-439","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/trainingcentres/centre_details.php?id=106","group":"Research institutions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sopanam Institute of Performing Arts and Research","kind":"institution","host":"Research institutions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"The Sopanam Institute connects theatre research with training in performance and music. Its work with regional songs, tales and older dramatic texts offers an example of how inherited artistic material can be studied, taught and reinterpreted within a contemporary cultural institution.","full":"The institute's profile describes workshops, productions, seminars and publications associated with Kavalam Narayana Panikkar's theatre practice. Training includes Indian theatre, Mohiniyattam and Sopana Sangeetham, while its stated interests extend to dramatized poems and folk narratives. The institution can be studied as a meeting point between research and practical experimentation. A modern adaptation should be identified as such, rather than presented as an unchanged survival of an earlier performance.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-IKS-440","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-IKS-440","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/trainingcentres/centre_details.php?id=28","group":"Research institutions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kerala Mural Painting Tradition","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kerala mural painting combines narrative imagery with knowledge of pigments, wall preparation and composition. Its familiar five-colour palette appears in temple and palace paintings, while contemporary artists also adapt the visual language to canvas, clothing and other surfaces beyond its architectural settings.","full":"The mural tradition organizes figures, ornament and colour within an architectural surface. Kerala Tourism describes a palette known as panchavarna and pigments associated with minerals, soot, lime and plant materials. Outlining, filling and the finishing of the eyes are important stages in its account. Surviving historic murals and contemporary adaptations should be distinguished: a canvas in the mural style is not automatically made with the same materials or methods as an old wall painting.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-441","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-441","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/shopping-options/shop-mural-paintings-kerala/8/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Beypore Uru Shipbuilding","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Beypore","state":"Kerala","about":"Beypore's shipwrights construct wooden urus associated with Indian Ocean trade. The craft joins timber selection, shaping and plank assembly with knowledge retained through workshop practice, demonstrating how a large seagoing vessel can depend on coordinated expertise passed between generations of makers.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Beypore as a major centre for building the wooden trading vessels called urus or dhows. Its account emphasizes the skill of shipwrights, joining of timber planks and collective organization of work. This maritime craft offers evidence of connections between Kerala's timber resources and overseas trading networks. Claims that all builders work without drawings or that the technique has remained unchanged should be checked against the practice of the individual workshop.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-442","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-442","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2010/uru-craft-ship-building/73","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":11.17907,"lng":75.81018,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chendamangalam Handloom Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Chendamangalam","state":"Kerala","about":"Chendamangalam's handloom makers produce set-mundu, sarees and towels using skills in thread preparation and loom operation. The weaving tradition is associated with distinctive borders and a regional textile identity, while present-day designs also respond to changing markets and uses.","full":"The handloom cluster at Chendamangalam is known for Kerala garments including the two-piece set-mundu and kasavu-bordered sarees. Kerala Tourism highlights the Puliyilakkara border and describes weaving on conventional looms. These textiles connect material preparation, patterned borders and cooperative livelihoods with regional clothing practices. Contemporary motifs are incorporated into the repertoire alongside established designs, showing weavers responding to present-day uses while maintaining the practical knowledge involved in preparing yarn and operating a handloom.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-443","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-443","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2023/chendamangalam-handloom-ernakulam/1333","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":10.16917,"lng":76.23409,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kuthampully Handloom Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Kuthampully","state":"Kerala","about":"Kuthampully is a handloom cluster associated with kasavu-bordered Kerala garments. Sarees, dhotis and set-mundu are treated here as products of one connected weaving tradition, whose knowledge includes yarn handling, border design and the coordination of work at the loom.","full":"The weaving settlement of Kuthampully is documented for textiles that connect ceremonial clothing with a continuing craft livelihood. Kerala Tourism describes garments such as double dhotis, veshti and set-mundu, as well as later design and embroidery adaptations. Borders and garment forms connect work at the loom with regional ideas of dress and ceremony. Accounts of migration and court patronage are useful research leads, to be compared with archival evidence and the histories told by weaving families.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-444","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-444","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2015/handloom-kerala/534","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":10.74375,"lng":76.39877,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kasaragod Saree Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Kasaragod","state":"Kerala","about":"Kasaragod sarees represent a regional handloom tradition with particular attention to yarn, warp sizing and border design. The craft links the preparation of cotton and coloured threads with weaving skills maintained through local producers and a cooperative textile network in northern Kerala.","full":"The official account of Kasaragod sarees describes the selection of threads, colours and designs and identifies warp sizing as a distinctive part of production. Both natural-coloured cotton and dyed yarn are used. These practices make the saree a useful record of material judgement and regional textile identity. Quality claims made in promotional descriptions should not be generalized to every item, and contemporary fabric composition or certification needs to be confirmed with its producer.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-445","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-445","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2023/kasaragod-sarees/1270","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":12.4222,"lng":75.19044,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kanhirode Handloom Cooperative","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Kanhirode","state":"Kerala","about":"Kanhirode's weaving cooperative maintains handloom production for clothing and furnishing fabrics. Its looms and workshop routines connect inherited textile skills with organized collective production, making it a useful place to study how manual craft adapts to institutional support and changing demand.","full":"The Kanhirode Weavers Cooperative Society produces cotton and silk textiles and furnishing materials. Kerala Tourism describes traditional loom operation and the aim of supporting weavers while preserving their skills. The institution offers a specific setting for examining the relationship between manual weaving, preparation and finished fabric. Its recorded product range should not be read as a claim that every process is unchanged or that all materials and dyes used today are traditional.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-446","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-446","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/destination/kanhirode-weavers-kannur/20/","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":11.91743,"lng":75.4638,"approx":true},
{"name":"Payyannur Pavithra Ring Craft","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Payyanur","state":"Kerala","about":"The Payyannur Pavithra ring is a regional gold ornament known for its intricate knot-like design. Its making connects fine metalwork with ceremonial meanings and family workshop traditions, while beliefs about the ring form part of its cultural history rather than evidence of physical effects.","full":"Kerala Tourism associates the Pavithra ring with craft families at Payyannur and the local Subrahmanya temple. Its central knot recalls ritual rings made from darbha grass, translating a temporary material form into metal. The design offers a focused example of how jewellery can carry religious meanings as well as ornament. Stories about origin, purity and good fortune should be attributed to tradition; they do not establish health benefits or a single documented date of invention.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-447","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-447","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2018/pavithra-mothiram/758","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":12.09189,"lng":75.19518,"approx":true},
{"name":"Screw-Pine Leaf Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Screw-pine weaving turns fibrous leaves into mats, baskets and other articles. Often practised by women, the craft requires judgement in preparing and interlacing a material that grows near waterways, connecting household work with detailed knowledge of local plants and their properties.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes the use of screw-pine leaves for floor mats, baskets, bed mats and wall hangings. The leaves have thorned edges and fibrous surfaces, so preparing them is an important part of the work before weaving. The tradition illustrates how plant knowledge and manual patterning meet in useful objects. A material being plant-based does not by itself establish the environmental impact of every product, which also depends on harvesting, processing and later treatments.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-448","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-448","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/campaigns/kerala365/screw-pine-handicrafts-kerala","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Alleppey Coir Processing and Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Alappuzha","state":"Kerala","about":"Alleppey coir production transforms coconut husks into yarn, rope, mats and other goods. Retting, separating, drying and spinning fibres connect knowledge of a locally available material with household work, cooperative production and the industrial history of Kerala's coastal waterways.","full":"The Kerala Industries Department's account explains coir as fibre extracted from coconut husks and describes traditional retting followed by drying and spinning. The Alappuzha industry links these skills to markets for ropes, mats and related products. Its history shows craft and mechanized processes developing together rather than a simple division between old and new work. Retting conditions, processing equipment and environmental effects differ, and should be studied at the level of the actual production unit.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-449","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-449","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/alleppey-coir","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":9.50034,"lng":76.41234,"approx":true},
{"name":"Koyilandy Brass-Broidered Coconut-Shell Craft","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Koyilandy","state":"Kerala","about":"Koyilandy's coconut-shell craft combines shaped shell, wood and cast metal in ornamental objects historically associated with hookahs. It brings together several material skills and records connections between coastal workshops and overseas customers through the production and circulation of composite objects.","full":"The Kerala Industries Department describes the making of brass-broidered coconut-shell objects through the example of Koyilandy hookahs. The production uses shell for the water chamber, wood for the stem and patterned metal components made with clay and wax. This combination is relevant to the study of composite craft and coastal exchange. Accounts of workshop production offer a starting point for studying materials and assembly, while the provenance of individual surviving pieces needs separate documentation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-450","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-450","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/brass-broidered-coconut-shell-crafts-of-kerala","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":11.43836,"lng":75.69689,"approx":true},
{"name":"Palakkad Maddalam Making","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Palakkad","state":"Kerala","about":"Making a maddalam joins woodworking, hide preparation and the adjustment of tension in a percussion instrument. The Palakkad craft supplies performance traditions such as Kathakali and Panchavadyam, showing how musical knowledge depends on the practical judgement of instrument makers as well as players.","full":"The Development Commissioner for Handicrafts describes the maddalam as a drum shaped from a single piece of jackfruit wood with leather-covered ends. Its two principal forms are associated with different ways of suspending the instrument during performance. Hollowing, covering and tensioning the drum connect material properties with the sound expected by musicians. The compendium gives an introduction to these materials and forms; detailed workshop methods and tuning practices require documentation with instrument makers.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-451","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-451","src":"https://www.handicrafts.nic.in/CmsUpload/12222017102212GI%20BOOK%20FINAL%202-5-17_resized.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":10.78737,"lng":76.47422,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kunhimangalam Bell-Metal Craft","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Pilathara","state":"Kerala","about":"Kunhimangalam's Moosari artisans make lamps, sculptures and ritual objects in bronze and bell metal. Their work connects model-making, moulds and metal casting with the visual conventions of religious and domestic objects, preserving skills that are learned through workshop practice.","full":"Kerala Tourism documents Kunhimangalam as a village associated with inherited expertise in bronze and bell-metal objects. Its account describes the use of individual moulds in casting and the drawing of designs from religious narratives. Lamps and sculptural figures make the craft's relationship to everyday and ceremonial use visible. Productive techniques vary among workshops, and claims that all objects are made without any machinery or moulds should be checked against the specific maker's process.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-452","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-452","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2021/kunhimangalam-heritage-village/1080","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":12.07236,"lng":75.23655,"approx":true},
{"name":"Aruvacode Pottery and Kumbham Craft Project","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Aruvacode's potters work with clay in a tradition associated with Kumbharan families. The Kumbham project supported new uses for their skills, connecting familiar vessels with terracotta furniture, architectural pieces and other designs developed in response to changing markets for handmade products.","full":"The official account describes pottery-making at Aruvacode and the challenges created by competition from plastic utensils. The Kumbham Handicraft Project, developed with designer K. B. Jinan and local potters, broadened production into garden furniture, architectural accessories and household wares. This is evidence of adaptation rather than an unchanged craft frozen in the past. The makers' knowledge of shaping and working clay remains central even when designs and uses change.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-453","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-453","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/aruvacode/63","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kannadippaya Bamboo-Mat Weaving","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kannadippaya is a finely woven bamboo mat known for square patterns that change in appearance with the light. Its makers prepare thin reed-bamboo slivers and organize them into flexible surfaces, transmitting material and pattern knowledge through closely observed, hands-on learning.","full":"The GI application, facilitated by KSCSTE–Kerala Forest Research Institute, documents Kannadippaya weaving among several Adivasi communities, including Oorali, Mannan, Muthuva, Malayan and Kadar makers. Thin bamboo slivers create the kannadi patterns through their arrangement in warp and weft. The craft links the choice and preparation of forest material to optical effects and domestic or ceremonial use. Documentation should acknowledge particular makers and communities, rather than treating the technique as anonymous knowledge available without attribution.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-454","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-454","src":"https://search.ipindia.gov.in/GIRPublicSearch/Application/Details/927","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pokkali Rice-Prawn Farming","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Ernakulam","state":"Kerala","about":"Pokkali farming alternates saline-tolerant rice with prawn production in Kerala's coastal wetlands. Cultivation follows seasonal changes in salinity and water levels, connecting seed choice, sluice management and local experience of the monsoon with an integrated system of agricultural and aquatic livelihoods.","full":"The official GI account describes rice cultivation during the lower-salinity monsoon phase and prawn farming during the more saline part of the year. Sluice gates regulate movement of water, and the harvested fields become part of the aquatic production cycle. Pokkali is therefore more than the name of a grain: it records a relationship between a crop, seasonal waters and collective landscape management. Current environmental conditions and actual farm practices need site-specific assessment.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-455","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-455","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/pokkali-rice","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":10.06367,"lng":76.246,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kaipad Rice-Fish Farming","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Kannur","state":"Kerala","about":"Kaipad farming uses northern Kerala's brackish coastal fields for a seasonal cycle of rice and aquatic production. Farmers work with monsoon rainfall, tides and salinity, including the preparation of mounds for rice, to coordinate food cultivation with fishing or shrimp production.","full":"The Kaipad account maintained by Kerala's Industries Department locates this system in the coastal tracts of Kannur, Kozhikode and Kasaragod. Rice grows in the less saline phase, while fish or shrimp production uses the high-salinity season. The system illustrates detailed observation of the changing wetland environment and the organization of smallholder work. Its regional fields, terminology and practices give it a distinct identity within Kerala's coastal agriculture.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-456","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-456","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/kaipad-rice","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":11.87638,"lng":75.3738,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kuttanad Below-Sea-Level Farming System","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Kuttanad","state":"Kerala","about":"Kuttanad farming combines bunded rice fields, drainage and aquatic livelihoods in a landscape partly below sea level. Farmers' knowledge of water control, soils and seasonal conditions is central to this agricultural system, recognized by FAO as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System.","full":"FAO describes Kuttanad as a mosaic of paddy wetlands, garden lands and water areas supporting fishing and shell collection. Bunds and dewatering make cultivation possible in reclaimed fields, while fish and duck production can form part of the wider system. The record concerns this managed landscape and its accumulated farming knowledge. Recognition as agricultural heritage does not imply freedom from environmental pressure; maintenance, biodiversity loss and changing livelihoods are part of its contemporary history.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-457","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-457","src":"https://www.fao.org/giahs/giahs-around-the-world/india-kuttanad-farming-system/en","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":9.45075,"lng":76.43082,"approx":true},
{"name":"Navara Rice Cultivation and Conservation","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Navara is a Kerala rice tradition whose conservation depends on growers maintaining seed and cultivation knowledge. Associated with food and Ayurvedic practice, it offers a record of how a named crop can carry agricultural, dietary and cultural meanings without those meanings proving medical efficacy.","full":"The official GI profile identifies Navara as an indigenous paddy associated with black and golden forms and a short growing cycle. It also describes farmers organizing to renew cultivation after a period of decline. These details support study of crop diversity, seed continuity and the relationship between growers and markets. Traditional medicinal associations are part of the crop's cultural history here; therapeutic claims in promotional material are not adopted as clinical conclusions.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-458","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-458","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/navara-rice","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Marayoor Jaggery Making","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Marayoor","state":"Kerala","about":"Marayoor jaggery links local sugarcane cultivation with the skilled concentration and shaping of cane juice. Its production connects farming and processing work in the high-range landscape, where makers judge changes in the liquid as it becomes a dark, hand-formed sweetener.","full":"The Kerala Industries Department describes Marayoor jaggery as a handmade regional product. Its account follows cane crushing, separation of the juice, heating and hand-forming the thickened material, with cane residue used as fuel. The craft depends on practical judgement during processing as well as the supply of cane from the surrounding area. A geographical indication records regional identity; it should not be taken as proof of health benefits or the quality of every product sold under the name.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-459","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-459","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/marayoor-jaggery","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":10.27574,"lng":77.16204,"approx":true},
{"name":"Central Travancore Jaggery Making","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Central Travancore jaggery production connects riverbank sugarcane farming with local processing skills. Makers concentrate cane juice and finish it in solid or semisolid forms, drawing on judgement of heat, texture and consistency within a regional tradition distinct from high-range Marayoor production.","full":"The official profile locates this jaggery tradition along the Manimala, Pamba, Achankovil and Meenachil river systems. It describes juice boiled in open pans over kilns and finished as rolled unda or semisolid pathiyan. The account connects the crop with soils affected by river flooding. This provides a basis for comparing landscape-specific food processing, while the source's nutritional and chemical claims require independent assessment and are not used here as health guidance.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-460","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-460","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/central-travancore-jaggery","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Wayanad Jeerakasala Rice Cultivation","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Wayanad","state":"Kerala","about":"Jeerakasala is an aromatic rice cultivated in Wayanad's highland farming landscapes. Its regional identity depends on growers recognizing and maintaining a particular crop, connecting seed selection and environmental conditions with the grain's use in local food traditions and its protected geographical name.","full":"The official GI profile describes Jeerakasala as one of Wayanad's traditional rice varieties and distinguishes it by aroma and grain form. It connects cultivation with the highland setting and identifies a local aromatic-rice growers' organization. The crop can be studied as part of the relationship between farmers, seed diversity and culinary preference. A named variety is not interchangeable with every aromatic rice, and current cultivation details should be confirmed with growers rather than inferred from packaging.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-461","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-461","src":"https://www.gikerala.in/products/wayanad-jeerakasala-rice","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":11.71513,"lng":76.12712,"approx":true},
{"name":"Attappady Mixed-Millet Farming","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Attappadi","state":"Kerala","about":"Attappady's millet farming connects crop diversity with the food practices of Adivasi communities. Recent programmes supporting ragi, little millet, kodo millet and other crops provide a setting for studying the continuity and revival of local cultivation, seed and food-processing knowledge.","full":"Kerala's Local Self Government Department documents support for mixed food cultivation in Attappady through the Bhakshyavanam initiative. Its account includes millets, paddy, pulses, vegetables, tubers and spices, with farming groups and households involved in production. The programme is a contemporary intervention drawing on local agricultural traditions, not the origin of those traditions. Research should distinguish its stated aims from measured results and acknowledge the particular knowledge of Irula, Muduga, Kurumba and other growers.","branch":"science","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-462","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-462","src":"https://lsgkerala.gov.in/en/kudumbashree/featured-topics/plan-cultivate-paddy-ragi-sama-rice-millet-sugarcane-koda-millet-other","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":11.05946,"lng":76.54296,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mohiniyattam","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mohiniyattam is a classical dance associated with controlled swaying movement, circular patterns and expressive gesture. Its repertoire combines music, rhythm and interpretation, making the relationship between bodily training and narrative meaning visible in a performance tradition that continues to develop through different schools.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Mohiniyattam through its movement vocabulary, expressive acting, costume and musical accompaniments. It notes both Carnatic and Sopanam-based approaches and recognizes variation between schools. The form offers a way to study how emotion, rhythmic structure and gesture are taught through repeated practice. Mythological explanations of its name should be separated from documented performance history, including nineteenth-century patronage and later institutional changes that shaped its modern repertoire.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-463","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-463","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/mohiniyattam-classical-dance/19/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thullal","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thullal is a Malayalam solo narrative performance that combines dance, verse and satire. Associated with Kunchan Nambiar, it uses accessible language and audience interaction to communicate stories and social criticism, drawing together literary composition, rhythmic movement and the performer's capacity for improvisation.","full":"Thullal performances follow composed verses while allowing the artist to add spontaneous dialogue and respond to an audience. Kerala Tourism describes stylized costume, music and rhythm alongside the form's use of humour. Its named performance varieties belong to this connected tradition. The account of Nambiar inventing Thullal immediately after being mocked during Koothu is an origin legend; it should not be confused with documentary evidence for the full process of the form's development.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-464","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-464","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/thullal-performing-art/23/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chakyar Koothu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Chakyar Koothu brings learned storytelling into live performance through spoken interpretation, humour and expressive acting. Its practitioners work with epic narratives and inherited theatrical knowledge while responding to an audience, linking literary study with the timing and judgement of an experienced oral performer.","full":"The documented training and work of performers such as Kalamandalam Rama Chakyar show Koothu closely connected with the textual and expressive disciplines of Kutiyattam. Kerala Tourism's profile highlights performance knowledge passed through teachers and family lineages, and identifies wit and spoken expression as important qualities. The form can be examined as a meeting of scholarship and live narration. The experience of one artist illuminates a lineage without representing every Koothu tradition.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-465","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-465","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2011/kalamandalam-rama-chakyar/106","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Nangiarkoothu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Nangiarkoothu is a solo theatre tradition associated with women performers and the wider world of Kutiyattam. A single artist interprets narrative through gesture and expression, shifting between characters while musicians support a performance grounded in texts and carefully learned conventions of enactment.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Nangiarkoothu as an independent solo form drawing on Sree Krishna Charitham and performance instructions called attaprakaram. Mizhavu and cymbals accompany the interpretation, and the transformation of one performer between characters is known as pakarnnattam. These practices connect textual study with embodied memory and musical timing. Historical community-specific temple rights and contemporary training beyond those communities should be distinguished rather than reduced to a single unchanging rule.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-466","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-466","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/koothu-performing-art/6/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Padayani","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Thiruvananthapuram","state":"Kerala","about":"Padayani combines painted headgear, movement, songs and percussion in a ritual theatre tradition of Central Travancore. Its kolam figures are made from locally available materials, connecting visual design and craft preparation with rhythm, narrative and the collective organization of festival performance.","full":"The official account of Padayani describes kolams made with areca-palm spathes, coconut leaves and coloured materials such as charcoal and turmeric. Thappu drumming, songs and rhythmic steps accompany the figures, while satire also forms part of a presentation. The tradition makes the interdependence of making, singing and movement particularly visible. Its mythical narratives belong to the ritual's meaning; broad prehistoric or pre-Dravidian origin claims are not treated as established dates here.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-467","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-467","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/padayani/padayani-presentation/3","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":8.51726,"lng":76.98164,"approx":true},
{"name":"Tholpavakoothu Shadow Puppetry","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Tholpavakoothu uses leather puppets, light and a screen to present stories from the Ramayana. Puppeteers combine manipulation with recitation and explanation, making this Kerala theatre tradition a setting where material craft, literary interpretation and the timing of an ensemble work together.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Tholpavakoothu in Bhagavathi temple theatres called koothumadams and also notes its public performances beyond temples. The repertoire draws on Kambar's Ramayana, with live explanation accompanying the verses. Puppeteers associated with Pulavar families sustain both technical and literary knowledge. The form should be distinguished from glove puppetry: here the relationship between the puppet, lighting and screen is central to how the narrative is seen.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-468","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-468","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/tholppavakkoothu-shadow-puppetry/9/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Oppana","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Oppana is a celebratory song-and-dance tradition associated with Mappila weddings. Singers and dancers coordinate clapping, movement and chorus around the bride, connecting musical memory with social celebration and the skills through which performers learn to respond to one another in a group.","full":"The official account places Oppana within the wedding and festive life of Kerala's Mappila communities. A lead singer begins a line and the group takes up the chorus, while dancers maintain a shared rhythm and circular movement. The repertoire includes playful references to marriage and the bride's emotions. Contemporary festival-stage versions may use instruments and altered arrangements, so they should not be assumed to reproduce every feature of a household wedding performance.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-469","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-469","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/islam-in-kerala/performing-arts/oppana","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Margamkali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Margamkali is a Christian group-dance tradition performed around a lamp. Its songs, circular formations and coordinated steps combine local performance techniques with religious narrative, making the form an example of cultural translation and learning through shared movement and sung text.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Margamkali's lamp-centred formation and songs associated with the story of St Thomas. A lead singer and responding dancers coordinate changes of pace and movement. The form is associated particularly with Syrian and Knanaya Christian communities, while its practitioners and public settings have changed over time. Religious narratives explain the performance's meaning but do not alone establish its date of origin or the historical details of the events they recount.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-470","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-470","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/margomkali-folk-art/12/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Chavittu Natakam","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Chavittu Natakam is a sung dance-drama recognized for forceful stamping, elaborate costume and large historical or religious narratives. Its performers combine movement, music and dramatic speech, reflecting encounters between Kerala theatrical practices and European-influenced stories within coastal Christian community life.","full":"The official account describes performance texts known as chuvati and a training tradition led by an annavi or asan. Actors sing while performing rhythmic steps on a wooden platform, with costume and character types shaping the visual drama. The language and repertoire record connections between Tamil, Malayalam and Christian historical narratives. Present-day shorter productions are part of the form's development, rather than evidence that every earlier performance had the same length or structure.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-471","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-471","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/chavittunatakom-performing-art/16/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thiruvathirakali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thiruvathirakali brings women together in a circular formation around a lamp, coordinating steps and clapping with songs. Associated with Thiruvathira and Onam celebrations, it links shared musical memory, social gathering and the learning of measured movement within a recognizable regional performance tradition.","full":"Also known as Kaikottikali, this dance uses coordinated hand movements and changing pace while performers move around a central lamp. Kerala Tourism describes both devotional and narrative songs and notes its association with particular festival settings. Its apparent simplicity depends on timing, mutual attention and familiarity with the repertoire. Modern competitions and large staged versions should be distinguished from smaller domestic gatherings, and ritual meanings can vary between communities and occasions.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-472","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-472","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/onam/onam-games/thiruvathirakkali","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kolkali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kolkali is a group dance in which performers strike short sticks while moving through coordinated patterns. Expanding and contracting circles require close rhythmic attention, connecting song, bodily discipline and cooperation in a folk tradition widely practised in Kerala's northern districts.","full":"The Malabar account describes each dancer holding sticks and moving in a circle while maintaining the rhythm of the accompanying music. Patterns change and the tempo increases as the performance develops. The form makes the coordination of space, time and partners visible, and is associated with several community settings. Regional relationships with other stick dances are useful comparative questions, but broad diffusion claims and exact dates of origin require more evidence than an introductory tourism page.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-473","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-473","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/kolkali/9","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Duffmuttu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Duffmuttu combines frame-drum playing with coordinated movement and lead-and-chorus singing. Performed in Mappila social and religious settings, it brings rhythmic skill and remembered verse together, with the drum helping to organize both the pace of the song and the movement of the group.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies the shallow round duff as the central instrument of Duffmuttu. A lead performer sings and the others respond while drumming and moving in time. Songs can honour saints, martyrs and other religious figures, and the form appears at weddings and communal events. The relationship between voice, instrument and bodily motion is central to its knowledge system; exact ensemble sizes and performance arrangements are not assumed to be uniform.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-474","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-474","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/duffmuttu/7","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Aravanamuttu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Aravanamuttu is a Malabar ensemble tradition related to Duffmuttu but associated with the larger arabana drum. Its coordinated drumming and movement record a specific local performance vocabulary, bringing instrumental skill together with the social practices through which groups rehearse and present ceremonial entertainment.","full":"Kerala Tourism's Malappuram guide distinguishes Aravana Mutt from Duff Mutt by the larger, thinner arabana instrument and the more demanding movements described in its account. It also records performances associated with welcoming dignitaries, placing the music in a ceremonial setting where group movement forms part of the presentation. The guide is introductory, so detailed musical structures, histories of particular groups and changing performance settings require consultation with practitioners.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-475","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-475","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/images/ebooks/pdf/malappuramlrj2820240628081506.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kummattikkali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kummattikkali is a masked folk performance associated with Onam and other seasonal celebrations. Performers move through streets and visit houses in costumes made with grass or leaves, joining mask-making and material preparation with songs, movement and the social exchange of a village procession.","full":"The official account describes Kummatti performers in masks and plant-based costume moving among households. Thrissur's Onam presentations are prominent, while other local settings follow different seasonal occasions. The craft of preparing a mask and wearable vegetation is inseparable from the group's movement and public encounters. Stories linking the dance to Shiva and Arjuna belong to its legendary background, and should not be converted into a documentary explanation of its origin.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-476","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-476","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/kummattikkali-folk-art/26/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pulikali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Pulikali combines body painting with dance and percussion in performances associated with Onam. Artists portray tigers and hunters through colour, masks and movement, making the preparation of a painted body and the coordination of a street ensemble central parts of the tradition.","full":"Kerala Tourism's filmed account at Thrissur describes the patient preparation of tiger-like body painting and the actions of dancers alongside a hunter character. Percussion supports the movement and dramatic exchanges. The form provides a setting for examining ephemeral painting, characterization and collective preparation outside a conventional theatre. Colours, participants and presentation styles have changed, so neither one filmed performance nor a popular patronage story should be treated as the complete history of Pulikali.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-477","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-477","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/video-gallery/pulikali/165/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kannyarkali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kannyarkali, also called Desathukali, combines energetic movement, comedy and folk song in Palakkad's performance culture. Dancers work around a lamp to instrumental accompaniment, connecting rhythmic training and bodily coordination with a local dramatic repertoire presented in temple and community settings.","full":"The official description identifies Kannyarkali with the Palakkad region and relates its agile movements to martial practice. It lists percussion including chenda, maddalam, edakka and udukku, and describes staged sequences with different names over several days. The performance is useful for studying how disciplined movement can become narrative and comic action. Its reported military origin is a research lead rather than an independently demonstrated explanation for every element of the form.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-478","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-478","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/marathi/artforms/ritual-art/art.php?id=7","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Poothan and Thira","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Poothan and Thira are costumed ritual figures who move through villages during festival seasons. Masks, carved headgear, bells and percussion make their arrival recognizable, connecting craft and bodily skill with household visits and the performance of relationships between a community and its deities.","full":"Kerala Tourism locates Poothan Thira in parts of Palakkad, Malappuram and Thrissur. Performers representing Bhagavathy and a companion spirit visit houses and appear in temple precincts. The account describes light-wood headgear, facial decoration and the sound of drums and bells announcing the procession. These elements illustrate a tradition carried through making, dressing and movement. Its deity narratives are interpreted as ritual meanings, while regional variations require the accounts of local performers.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-479","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-479","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/poothanumthirayum/3/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Theeyattu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Theeyattu joins powder drawing, song and a costumed solo enactment within a ritual setting. Its Bhadrakali and Ayyappan forms connect visual preparation and recitation with movement, showing how several artistic skills work together in a performance organized around a carefully prepared kalam.","full":"The official account distinguishes Bhadrakali Theeyattu from Ayyappan Theeyattu. For the Bhadrakali form it describes the preparation of a coloured-powder image, songs and a night-time enactment concerning the goddess and Darika. A lamp and the performer's headgear help organize the narrative setting. The tradition can be examined through the sequence linking image-making, music and acting, while particular ritual requirements and community rights should be documented locally rather than generalized across all venues.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-480","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-480","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/theeyattu-ritual/14/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Arjuna Nritham","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Arjuna Nritham, also called Mayilpeeli Thookkam, combines peacock-feather costume with rhythmic movement and songs. Its performers interpret named patterns through steps and explanation, connecting embodied training with oral knowledge of rhythm in a ritual tradition associated with goddess worship.","full":"Kerala Tourism places this form especially in Alappuzha and Kottayam and describes the feathered lower garment, painted face and headgear. Songs known as kavithams are organized by rhythm, with performers explaining a pattern before dancing it. Chenda, maddalam and cymbals support the presentation. The account links the form with Ezhava performers and an Arjuna legend; the legend gives a cultural frame rather than proof of an epic-period date for the practice.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-481","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-481","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/arjuna-nritham/8/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Velakali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Velakali is a martial dance performed with swords and shields during temple festivities in southern Kerala. Its formations, headgear and measured actions translate the appearance of a warrior group into organized performance, linking bodily discipline and rhythmic coordination with ceremonial interpretation.","full":"The official description identifies traditional warrior-style costume, vigorous movement and coordinated sword play supported by instrumental music. Velakali is associated with Ambalappuzha and also appears in the festival traditions of Thiruvananthapuram. It can be examined as staged martial knowledge rather than as a direct demonstration of battlefield technique. Stories about its first patron or a mythic battle should be separated from the documented histories of particular teaching groups and performances.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-482","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-482","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/japanese/artforms/ritual-art/art.php?id=10","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kakkarissi Natakam","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kakkarissi Natakam is a musical folk theatre built around dialogue, dance and satire. A question-and-answer exchange between characters helps move the play forward, connecting sung performance with humour and social observation in a tradition whose language draws on both Malayalam and Tamil.","full":"The official account describes Sundara Kakaan and the questioner called Thampuran as important figures in this dramatic structure. Songs and rhythmic steps alternate with spoken exchanges, supported by harmonium, mridangam, ganjira and kaimani. The form makes the performer's command of dialogue and audience response as significant as musical ability. Community histories and proposed connections with Tamil performance traditions require more detailed research than an introductory account can provide.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-483","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-483","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2022/kakkarissi-natakam/1198","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Poorakkali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Poorakkali combines group singing with dance around a lamp in the Bhagavathi festival traditions of northern Kerala. The performers provide their own voices and rhythm, coordinating a sequence of movements whose learning joins bodily practice with familiarity with epic stories and sung texts.","full":"The official Malabar account describes a leader called Panicker, with other performers repeating the sung lines while moving around a lamp. It identifies eighteen stages or niram and notes that a separate orchestra is not required. The tradition links musical memory and disciplined movement with community ceremony. Associated scholarly debate known as Marathukali belongs to the same festival world, but descriptions of every local programme should be checked rather than inferred from a general outline.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-484","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-484","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/malabar/poorakkali/12","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Parichamuttukali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Parichamuttukali is a group dance performed with swords and shields, often during church festivities. Dancers respond to a leader's song and strike shields in rhythm, connecting martial-looking actions with religious verse and the collective discipline needed for a coordinated ceremonial performance.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes performers gathering around a lamp with vaal and paricha, the sword and shield that give the form its character. An asan leads the song as the group repeats the lines and the pace increases. Saints such as George and Sebastian appear among the song subjects. The practice can be compared with other martial dances, while resemblance to Kalaripayattu should not be treated as proof that every movement is a direct survival of combat training.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-485","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-485","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/christianity/parichamuttu-kali/37","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Gadhika","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Gadhika is a performance tradition of the Adiya community in Wayanad. Its named social and ritual forms connect song and movement with community ideas about well-being, providing a setting for studying oral transmission and ceremonial practice without treating ritual beliefs as clinical evidence.","full":"Kerala Tourism's documentation identifies the Adiya community as practitioners and distinguishes Naattu Gadhika from Pooja Gadhika. Its filmed presentation connects the tradition with a community arts and research group in Wayanad. The record is relevant to how narratives, music and ceremonial roles are learned and shared. Descriptions that call Gadhika a healing dance express a cultural understanding of the rite; they do not demonstrate that participation treats or cures a medical condition.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-486","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-486","src":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WLcAYWtlOX8","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mannankoothu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mannankoothu is a night-time dance-drama of the Mannan community in Idukki. Stories of Kannaki and Kovilan are presented through characters, music and make-up, connecting a regional oral performance tradition with the wider narrative world associated with the Tamil epic Chilappathikaram.","full":"The official account identifies Pen Koothadi, Aan Koothadi and Komali among the characters and places performances in worship, harvest and wedding settings. Instruments and an opening announcement form part of the presentation, while face make-up uses a rice-flour and oil mixture. The form records the local interpretation of an epic narrative through community practice. Similarities with other dance-dramas are comparative observations rather than evidence that Mannankoothu is merely a version of Kathakali.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-487","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-487","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2022/mannankoothu/1207","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kalamezhuthu and Pattu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kalamezhuthu creates images on the ground with coloured powders, accompanied by songs and ritual actions. Its makers combine knowledge of materials, proportion and visual convention with performance, producing an image whose planned erasure is part of the event rather than a failure of preservation.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes powder images made for deities such as Bhadrakali, Ayyappan and serpent figures. Rice powder, turmeric, husk charcoal, lime mixtures and leaves contribute colours in its account, with singers and percussionists taking part in the rite. The practice connects visual design to time, sound and ceremonial use. Specific deity traditions and the rights and techniques of different artist communities should not be assumed to be identical across Kerala.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-488","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-488","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/kalamezhuthu-ritual-art/11/","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Mappilappattu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Mappilappattu brings the poetic traditions of Kerala's Mappila communities into song. Devotional, narrative, wedding and other genres preserve a varied literary repertoire, linking memory and musical performance with Arabi Malayalam writing and the region's multilingual cultural exchanges over time.","full":"The official literature account lists several genres within Mappilappattu, including devotional mala songs, war narratives, love songs and letter songs. Their variety shows that the tradition cannot be reduced to a single occasion or theme. Oral preservation and later written and recorded circulation are part of its history. A song may combine historical reference with poetic invention, so its narrative should not automatically be treated as a literal chronicle of the events it describes.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-489","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-489","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/islam-in-kerala/language-literature/mappilappattu","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Sopana Sangeetham","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Sopana Sangeetham is a temple-associated singing tradition whose name is linked to the steps leading toward the sanctum. Voice and idakka accompaniment shape a distinctive musical practice, joining devotional texts with learned approaches to melody, rhythm and the timing of ritual presentation.","full":"Kerala's higher-secondary music textbook describes Sopana Sangita as an indigenous system closely associated with temple music. It discusses idakka accompaniment, characteristic ragas and talas, and the importance of devotional compositions including Ashtapadis. The text also states that its exact period of origin is not established. These details support studying the tradition through repertoire and musical practice, without using an imagined ancient date or treating it as identical to present-day Kathakali singing.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-490","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-490","src":"https://samagra.kite.kerala.gov.in/files/samagra-resource/uploads/tbookscmq/Class_XII/Music/Music.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Panchavadyam","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Panchavadyam coordinates five principal instruments in an ensemble tradition associated with Kerala festivals. Drums, cymbals and a horn create a changing rhythmic structure, requiring performers to listen collectively and manage the movement from a measured beginning toward faster and denser passages.","full":"The official account names maddalam, edakka, thimila, ilathalam and kombu as the ensemble's instruments. Their contrasting sounds and functions make the performance depend on both individual skill and a shared rhythmic framework. It offers a setting for studying how percussion and wind players organize time together. Ensemble size and arrangement change between occasions, and the literal meaning of five instruments should not be confused with a fixed number of five performers.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-491","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-491","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2015/panchavadhyam/562","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kerala Chenda Melam Ensembles","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Chenda melam brings drums, cymbals and wind instruments into large rhythmic ensembles. Forms such as Panchari and Pandi use different cycles and staged changes of tempo, showing how shared patterns, leadership and disciplined listening allow many musicians to shape one coordinated performance.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes Panchari Melam and Pandi Melam with chenda, ilathalam, kombu and kuzhal. It distinguishes six-beat Panchari from seven-beat Pandi and explains their development through successive stages. The changing cycles connect the work of drummers, cymbal players and wind musicians within an extended musical structure. Such performances depend on shared listening and knowledge of the sequence, while the detailed interpretation of rhythmic patterns is learned within particular performing lineages.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-492","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-492","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2011/traditional-orchestras/134","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Thayambaka","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Thayambaka foregrounds a lead chenda player's rhythmic invention against an accompanying group that keeps time. The practice connects vocalized learning, palm-and-stick technique and the shaping of increasing tempo, making improvisation possible within a framework shared by the principal artist and supporting musicians.","full":"L. S. Rajagopalan's study in the Music Academy journal describes Thayambaka as laya vinyasa on the chenda. It explains the lead player's use of palm and stick, accompanying timekeepers and progression through named stages. Recitation of vayattari is identified as part of learning. This detailed historical account is valuable evidence of musical practice in its period; later performers may vary or extend the structures and techniques that the author recorded.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-493","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-493","src":"https://musicacademymadras.in/catalogue/files/journals/Vol.38_1967.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pulluvan Pattu","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Pulluvan Pattu is a song tradition associated with serpent worship and the Pulluvar community. Sung narratives and distinctive musical accompaniment connect oral repertoire with sacred-grove and household settings, preserving a form of knowledge carried through performance rather than through written texts alone.","full":"The Responsible Tourism resource directory describes Pulluvan songs in relation to Kerala's serpent shrines and lists narratives concerning serpent figures from epic traditions. The repertoire includes named songs and is not limited to one melodic pattern or ritual occasion. It can be studied through the relationship between singers, instruments, patrons and place. Beliefs that a performance protects a household or removes misfortune are recorded as ritual meanings, not demonstrated physical effects.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-494","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-494","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/responsible-tourism/pdf/VAIKOM-RESOURCE-DIRECTORY-ENGLISH.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Krishnanattam","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"Palakunnu","state":"Kerala","about":"Krishnanattam presents episodes from Krishna's life through a cycle of dance-drama. Its repertoire links the Sanskrit text Krishnageeti with group choreography, expressive acting and music, preserving a performance tradition associated with Zamorin patronage and its later institutional home at Guruvayur.","full":"The official account identifies Manaveda's Krishnageeti as the literary basis and describes eight stories covering episodes from Krishna's life. Performances use singing, percussion, distinctive costume and masks for some characters. The form shares techniques with neighbouring dance-dramas while maintaining a specific repertoire and teaching history. Its role as a devotional offering is part of the tradition's meaning; promised outcomes attributed to particular plays should not be presented as verified effects.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-495","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-495","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/artforms/krishnanattom-temple-art/18/","group":"Living traditions"},"lat":12.42486,"lng":75.02491,"approx":true},
{"name":"Pavakathakali Glove Puppetry","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Pavakathakali adapts the visual and narrative world of Kathakali to glove puppets. Small costumed figures are animated by a puppeteer's hands, joining carving, costume-making and precise manipulation with sung stories in a performance medium distinct from both human dance-drama and shadow puppetry.","full":"The theatre researcher and practitioner G. Venu documents Pavakathakali as a glove-puppet tradition associated particularly with Paruthippully and the Palakkad region. His account explains how Kathakali costume and attakatha themes entered an existing puppet practice. The form makes the relationship between a performer's hand and the puppet's visible action central to storytelling. Its historical connections do not mean that every modern puppet troupe has the same repertoire or method of training.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-496","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-496","src":"https://www.sahapedia.org/sites/default/files/2019-03/Pavakathakali%20-%20The%20Traditional%20Glove-Puppet%20Play%20of%20Kerala%20-%20G.%20Venu_0.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kuthiyottam Dance Tradition","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kuthiyottam is a ritual dance tradition associated with offerings during the Chettikulangara Bharani season. Boys learn coordinated steps to songs and instrumental accompaniment, connecting household preparation, teaching and community ceremony within a practice whose local forms require careful, context-specific documentation.","full":"Kerala Tourism identifies Kuthiyottam as a ritual performed in houses of Chettikulangara and neighbouring areas before the Bharani festival. Its description emphasizes learned dance steps with folk music and instruments. The performance is useful for examining how rhythm and ceremonial roles are taught in a local setting. Related rites can involve demanding observances, and documentation should distinguish the dance from other practices and avoid presenting participation as a visitor activity or a universal obligation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-497","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-497","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/1000festivals//assets/uploads/pdf/1507654308-0.pdf","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Vanchipattu Boat-Song Tradition","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Vanchipattu accompanies Kerala's boat traditions with rhythmic singing that helps coordinate collective rowing. Its songs connect the labour of moving a vessel with village identity, festival gatherings and oral composition, allowing a large crew to organize movement through shared musical time.","full":"Kerala Tourism describes oarsmen rowing in synchronization to traditional boat songs during vallamkali. Village boats and their crews make the repertoire part of a wider social setting of practice and competition. The songs can therefore be studied as both performance and a means of coordinating physical work. Repertoires and styles vary, including distinct ceremonial settings, so no single race, text or modern competition represents the entire tradition of singing connected with boats.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-498","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-498","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2020/vallamkali-resplendent-water-regattas-of-kerala/1017","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Seethakali","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Seethakali retells episodes from Seetha's journey in the Ramayana through folk songs, colourful costume and movement. Associated with Perinad and the Vedar and Pulayar communities, it links orally transmitted narrative with locally made props and the work of recent performance-revival groups.","full":"The official account describes Seethakali as a house-to-house Onam performance associated with Perinad. Bamboo and palm leaves are used in props and instruments, while the songs and actions carry the narrative. It also records the role of the Perinad Seethakali Sangham in renewing public performance. This revival should be understood alongside the knowledge of earlier practitioners, without treating one registered group's present status as proof that no other community memory survives.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-499","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-499","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/kerala-article/2024/seethakali-a-vibrant-folk-art-form-reviving-keralas-cultural-heritage/1465","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Kavadiyattam in Kerala","kind":"tradition","host":"Living traditions","city":"","state":"Kerala","about":"Kavadiyattam combines devotional procession with rhythmic movement while carrying a decorated kavadi on the shoulders. The performance links balance and bodily coordination with the making of a portable structure, reflecting a tradition shared across Kerala and neighbouring Tamil cultural settings rather than confined to one state.","full":"The official description explains kavadiyattam as a votive performance associated with Subrahmanya temples. Kavadis take different shapes and may use flowers, peacock feathers or tower-like designs. Dancers turn and move to percussion, with musical arrangements varying by occasion. The tradition provides a setting for studying wearable structure, balance and ceremonial movement. Its devotional meanings should be recorded respectfully without treating all vows or local observances as identical or recommending demanding ritual participation.","branch":"arts","source":"atlas","atlas":{"id":"KER-TRD-500","url":"https://iks-atlas.vercel.app/records/KER-TRD-500","src":"https://www.keralatourism.org/marathi/artforms/ritual-art/art.php?id=14","group":"Living traditions"},"noMap":true,"approx":true},
{"name":"Indian Institute of Scientific Heritage (IISH)","kind":"institution","host":"Charitable trust · National Heritage Centre","city":"Thrissur","state":"Kerala","lat":10.62416,"lng":76.13539,"approx":true,"founded":"1999","about":"A charitable trust founded in August 1999 to study and teach India's scientific and cultural heritage through lectures, study classes, seminars, exhibitions and publications, including e-books on the four Vedas.","full":"Founded on Śrāvaṇa Pūrṇimā in August 1999 and registered as a charitable trust, IISH works to teach India's scientific, technological and cultural heritage, the Vedas, Vedāṅgas, Upavedas and darśanas, to a wide public. It publishes books, recordings and e-books (including the four Vedas), holds lectures, study classes, seminars, exhibitions, camps and study tours, and runs the National Heritage Centre at Mazhuvanchery near Thrissur. Its founder, the CSIR scientist Dr. N. Gopalakrishnan, died in April 2023.","branch":"science","source":"iish","url":"https://www.iish.org/","peopleNote":"IISH's contact page still lists its founder, Dr. N. Gopalakrishnan, as Director, but he died in April 2023, so no current head is named here. Contact the office below.","contact":{"address":"National Heritage Centre, Mazhuvanchery, Eranellur, Thrissur 680501, Kerala","phone":"0487 242 9818 · 04885 240236 (National Heritage Centre)","email":"iishservice1@gmail.com (IISH)"}}
